      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>STUDENT'S SONG.</head>
        <p>THE STUDENT'S SONG.</p>
        <p>I had worked hard at Euclid. Rnd Latin, and Greek, Till my eyesight was dim, and my chest was goite weak; There were pains in my head, there were pains in my back. And I felt just as if I'd been placed on a rack. They sent for a Doctor, they sent for a Nurse. But all was in vain, I grew rapidly worse, Spite of poultice and potion, and powder and pill. When—who should come in but my old friend, dearlWill. He cried," Cheer up, old fellow, we'll soon have you well, As strong as an ox, aid as sound as a belt ; All you want's Quinine Bitters—of med'cioe the best, And cricket and football will Jo all the est." I took Quinine Bitters and found it Wai true. Now with weakness and pain I have n.thing to do; And as for the potions, and powders, and pills. Why, the Doctor may take them hims,lf if he wills. Now. "Hurrah for the Bitt-,rs." of tonics th- King, I'll shout till the root and rafters shall ring ; And I'll say to whoever may enter my door, It's the best of all med'cines for rich or for poor." Gw ILY Si EVANS</p>
        <p>QUININE BITTERS.</p>
