      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>BEFORE THE MA.GISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MA.GISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A "PET" DOG. -SCENIC IN TEM POLICES COURTN</p>
        <p>James Hamblett, 148 Knutsford-road, was charged at the Warrington Borough Court,on Monday,with allowing, a ferocious dog to be at large unmuzzled in Knutsford- Toad on June 18.—P.S. Cheney said that in consequence of complaints he received,he went to see the dog in ques- tion. The dog appeared to be a great pet and he wider- stood that it slept with the family. There were loud complaints from the neighbours abut it.—Mr. Grounds said that he was going home on the above date, and wag bitten on the leg by the doz. He ran after it arid said he would kill it, but Mrs. Hamblen said that if he did so he would have to piy for it.—A little boy named Carter also gave evidence to being bitten by the same dog.—Alderman Harrison said that he thought the dog ought to be destroyed, whereupon Mrs. Hamblett, who was in court. commenced weeping copiously, and begged of the magistrates not to kill her dog.—Alderman Harrison considered this to be.very foolish conduct, and said that she could not have been much more put about if her son was going to be put to death. Mrs. Hamblett however continued to beg for the life of her clog, and eventually had to be taken out of the Court.—Alderman Harrison then asked Fiamblett if he would consent to destroy the dog, but he replied that Ic would rather pay a fine. He was accordingly fined Si. and costs, 19s. in all.</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO ANIMALSCASFS:</p>
        <p>Wm. Henri-C—artledge, a youth residing with his parents at Turner-et., was charged at the Warrington Borough Court, on Friday mrnin. wit'i cruelly illtreat ing a horse by working and travelling the seine whilst- in an unfit state. P.C. Forster stated that on the 15th Inst. he saw the defendant taking a pony out of a cart containing scwts. of rags. Both knees were badly bro- ken,and the animal was almost unable to walk.—lnspec- tor Luff stated that both knees of the animal were broken On the off knee there was a wound cut ccmpletely to the bone. The lad told him that he didn't take the animal out with broken knees, but that it fell down at Earles- town. The animal was totally unfit for work.—Defend- ant was fined ss. and costs.—Wm. Russell, jun., Suez- st., was charged with workiug a horse in an unfit state, on the 13th inst., and Wm. Palmer, foreman, and Edward Connor, proprietor, were charged with causing the horse to be so worked. Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, appeared for the defence. Inspector Luff, stated that on the date named he saw the animal in question with two ethers attached to a bus. He noticed the animal in pain and stopped it. The horse was unable to keep its forefeet to the ground. On examining it he found a patch denuded of hair under the collar, which was causing pain. The legs were very hot. He told the man to tike the horse to the stable, and after- wards saw Mr. Connor, who said his foreman was to blame for sending the animal out. The foremau ad- mitted sending the animal out,but said Mr. Connor knew it was at work.. The horse was suffering from fever in the feet. Constable Wheatley gave ccrroborative evid- ence as to the horse being in pain. Mr. Browne denied that the horse suffered any pain,and called four witnesses in support of his statement. He said there was no evi- dence against Mr. Connor, and he applied for his dis- missal, which was granted.—Mr. J. Fairhurst, wheel- wnght„ Stockton Heath, and Mr. Charles Beaten, land- • lord of the Barry Arms, Walton, gave evidence as to being on the bus at the time the Inspector stopped it. They denied that the horse seemed in any way in pain. During the:evidence there was a little scene between Mr. Browne and the Chairman, (Alderman Harrison) in cen- sequence of something the latter gentleman had remarked on the bench. Mr. Browne said he heard the remark of the Chairman. Mr. Harrison: You have no right to hear 3:lr. Browne : You speak so loudly, I cannot help hear- ing it. Mr. Harrison: What did I say? Mr. Brnwne: You said you couldn't understand people coming to give this kind of evidenee. Mr. Harrison: I did not.—Mr. S. Wright: He said " conflicting evidence."—Mr. Barri : I said I couldn't understand people giving such conflict- ing evidence. I did not cast any reflection on your wit- nesses or any others.—Mr. Browne: lam exceedingly sorry I misunderstood you. The remainder of the evid- ence for the defence was then heard.—Mr. Burrows, blacksmith, Legh-street, contended that the horse was not suffering from fever in the feet, nor from any pain. and that it was quite fit for work. Mr. Fergusson, veterinary surgeon, said he examined the horse the previous day. It would not suffer any cruelty by being worked. Mr. Harrison said the evidencefor the defence bad been purely theoretical, whilst the evidence of the inspector and the police officer was practical. He con- sidered tbat the horse was unfit for work, but as there was a preponderance of opinion on the Bench that the case had not been proved it would be dismissed.</p>
        <p>MINOR GASES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT,</p>
        <p>FillDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Alder- man Wright, Councillor H. Roberts and Mr. S.</p>
        <p>Wright. I DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—For this offence John Rhooney, labourer, 75, Conk-street, Salford. was fined ss. and costs. It was his 23rd appearance in court. ASSAULT.—Mary Doherty, Blacks-yard, was fined ss. including costs for assaulting and beating Catherine Riley, Ship-yard, on the 16th inst. The complainant stated, an I two witnesses corroborated her statement, that the defendant struck her on the head with a clog. For the defence it was stated that the complainant pro- yoked the defendant by spitting in her face. SATUEDAY.—Before Mr. John Burgess (chairman), and Mr. S. Chandley. DRUNK AND DTSORDERLY.—Mary O'Reilly, who said she had come out of the workhouse the previous day in 'Order to have a day's enjoyment, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Dial-street, at 9.30 the previous night. P.C. Wood proved the case. She was committed to gaol for seven days hard labour. MONDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Mr. .Win. Pirpoint, Mr. S. Wright. DRUNKENNES9.—FOr this offence Annie Quinn, 24, Titley-st., was sent to prison for one month, Margaret McLoughlin, no settled abode, one month ; Frank Swan- ton, 89 Eldon-st., ss. without costs. INDECENT BEHAVIOUR.—John Frady, Robinson's-yard, was charged with committing an aggravated assault upon Theresa Jervis, and Eliz.tbeth Taylor. at the Old Craw on Saturday evening. The first complainant is the barmaid at the public-house, and the prisoner was alleged to have gone into the public house, and there indecently insulted the two females. Elizabeth Taylor gave her evidence very unwillingly, and said that she was afraid to tell the whole truth. She was, however, reassured by the Chief Constable, and then gave her evi (Jenne corroborating the other complainants evidence. P.C. Millington gave evidence to arresting the prisoner. Alderman Harrison considered that. it was intolerable that the prisoner could not go into a puhlic-hou,e</p>
