      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CCLLWICK'S SKIN OINTMENT AND</head>
        <p>CCLLWICK'S SKIN OINTMENT AND</p>
        <p>JT)LOOD IA B UP:TS. WORLD-WIDE RFPUTATION. Either olntmert Tabl,t' A fr,:m nil Chmists ;or post free Is. 31— from MAItTIN. Chemin, Sr)othampton. A ens BOOTS, CaAh Chemists, Wrirriogt.in. SPEAKIIAN 27, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>out behaving in such a disgraceful manner. Prisoner was sent to gaol for two months with hard labour. A CONVICTED THlEF—George Connolly,Percival's Fold was charged with being a convicted thief found fre- quenting Bewsey-at., on Sunday morning with felonious intent. P.C. Turner said that he SAW the prisoner loit- ering about in Bewsey-road early on Sunday morning. When the prisoner saw witness be commenced running away, but witness caught him and locked him up. He was dismissed with a caution. AN UPROARIOUS PRISONER.—Wm. Curley, 4, Jolley's- yard, was charged with being drunk and disorderly on Saturday night, also with assaulting Joseph Simmons, a coloured man, and also with breaking a window of the Leigh Arms, doing damage to the extent of 3s. 61. P.O Cooper said that he saw the black man running away on the previous night, and discovered that the prisoner had been abusing him sorely. He went to the prisoner and locked him up. The window of the Leigh Arms was broken during the scuffle. Joseph Simmons said that the prisoner wished to fight him, and because witness would not oblige him, he commenced knocking him about and the hat of witness was also lost in the scuffle. —Prisoner said that he was drunk and dii not know what he was doing. He was fined 20s. and costs, and also ordered to pay for the window, and the man's hat, or in default a months imprisonment. THEFT OF A PURSE.—John Me. Cone, 4 Friars Green, was charged with stealing a purse from the person of Mary Welsh, in Jolley's-yard, on Saterday night.—The complainant said that she was standing at the yard door when the prisoner went past. He struck her in the .mouth without any provocation, knocking one tooth rdown her throat, and loosening another. She then fell down to the ground, and prisoner pus his hand in her bosom and took a purse containing 3.3. 21d. away. A struggle ensued, and the prisoner threatened to cut her ! 'fingers if she did net let go. A policeman was sent for ! ! but none could be found. The prisoner at length got LI away, and leaving the purse behind, with the money I abstracted from it. He was arrested the follow- ing day.—A witness for the defence said that the complainant struck the prisoner before he did anything to her.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for 21 days. ' BREACH OF THE PEACE.—TWO brothers, John and Thomas Mitchell, 16 Hill-st., were bound over to keep the peace for six months, for fighting in Hill-st.,on JUEe 16th. They were also ordered to pay the costs, or in default seven days. P.C. Berry proved the ca3e.—Thos Common, Lythgoes-lane, was also dealt with in a similar manner, for fighting in Lythg,oes-tane, on June 16:h, with John Ireland. A warrant was issued against Ire- land. A Disonnennot YOUNGSTER.—John Burns, a boy of 13 years of age, 1 Turner-st., was charged with disorderly conduet in Winwick-st., on June 18. P.C. Bell stated that the boy had some newspapers near the Central Station, and with two other boys, was a source of great annoyance to the passengers. The boy was discharged with a caution, and a warrant was issued against the other two boys. STONE Tnnownsia.—George Robinson, 16 Nicholson-st. was fined 23. 6d., for throwing stones in Liverpool-road, on June 15th. P.C. Bell proved the case. PITCHING AND TOSSING.—Joseph France, 24, Watkin- st., was fined ss. for playing pitch and toss, in Jones Buildings on June 17. P.C. Meacock proved the case. —Thomas Highano, 66 Turner-st., George Delooze, 66 Turner-st., and John Sutton, 4 Knowles-yard, were also fined 2s. 6d. for committing the same offence on June 16 in Winwick-st. P.C. Bell proved the Case. CHIMNEY ON FIRE. —RobPrt Kirke, 3 Smith-st., was fined 2s. for haying his chimney on fire on June 14th, TUESDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman) Mr.E J. Guest, and Dr. E. Starkey Smith. DRUNKENNESS.—The following were charged with committing this offence on the preceding day :—John Costello, drunk in Church-st., ss. and costs ; Thomas Hoskins, in St. James'-st., 53. and costs ; Edward Gott, charged with being drunk and disorderly,a.nd assaulting his wife Catherine Gott. D.S. Monaghan deposed to see- ing the prisoner drunk and using bad language. P.C. Bell corroborated and said he also witnessed the assault. Mrs. Gott denied the assault. The prisoner was fined 53. and costs for the former offence and avitteci of the latter. Sarah Dillon was also charged with being drunk and disorderly in Fennel-street. She was discharged on promising to abstain.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough (chairman), and</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Geddes.</p>
        <p>DISORDERLY CONDUCT.—John Griffin, an Earlestown youth, and known as Thomas Stanton.. was charged with being disorderly and annoying passengers under the Central Station railviay arch, on Monday the 18th June. P.C. Bell said that about seven o'cl.ck on the night in question, he saw the prisorv.r in company with two other youths jostling people coming from toe station. He attempted to arrest them but they eluded him. 'I he prisoner who had been up 19 times be'bre,was discharged on promising to leave the town and behave himself for the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Messrs. T. Rigby, and F. J.Norman. A DANGEROUS PRACTICE.—Thornas Britland was sum- moned for furiously driving a horse and cart at Clifton on the 9th June to the danger of the public.—P.C. Morris said he saw the defenciadt driving furiously along the highway at Cliptorf and on reaching the top of the hill at Rock Savage he urged the herse on faster going down it. The animal was running at 4, rate of between 12 and 13 miles an hour. Several children were playing about the road at the time and some had narrow escapes of being run over. Defendant was ordered to pay the costs. MAINTENANCE OF A SON.—The magistrates granted the application of Superintendent Atherton for an order against Thomas Walker to pay is. per week for his son's maintenance. The boy is lodged at Bradwalt Reform- atory School, having been sent there for being gully of theft THE INTOXICATING CUP.—Thomas CoinollY • and Thomas Brannon were fined 54. and colts each, Within Walker and Thomas Myr), 2. 6,1. and costs for being drunk and disorderly at divers dates. Mary Finney St. Helens for a similar offence was discharged on promising to leave the town and and improve her behaviour. PROFANITY.—DanieI Ankers and Thomas Ainsworth were fined ss. and costs each for using obscene and pro. lane language. How DISEASE Is SPREAD.—MiCIiaeI Phillips was charged in custody with having beet a Felging-house for nightly lodgers, such house riot being regietered in accordance with the requirements of the Public Health Act. Mr. F. A. Lake, solicitor, who prosecuted on be- half of the Commissioners, said that the prisoner had already been before the court for breaches of tho bye- laws and keeping unregistered lodgineehouses. On the 12th inst. Sergeant Leo and P.C. Gregory went to the ' house occupied then by the prioner in Mersey-street and on visiting the bedrooms found two couples lodging there, who were subsequently found to be riielitly lodgers. The offence was made all the more serious owing to the filthy condition of the house. The spread of disease in the town was attributed, in a large extent, to such filthy dwellings being unlawlully kept (Ten. The fact that the prisoner had been in court six times Pre- viously on charges similar to the nature of the one that morning was sufficient evidence that a were fine of 30s. or 40s. and costs took little effect upon trim. The nia,t er was in the hands of the magistrates who could fine him the maximum £5. which was an amount more likely to 121 ring about the desired cure.—Defendant said be was so poor that he was compelled to keep the house open to money for food.—Fined 4)4. and costs! 15s. 4 1., or in fault of payment one month's imprisonment with hard labour. ASSAULT UPON A CONSTABLE.-1).,ni.1 Heeds was charged with being drunk and disorderly on the previ- ous Saturday night in Wellington-st. and with asssult- ing P.C. Richardson whilst executine his duty.—P.O. Richardson deposed to being called int) the theatre for the purpose of assisting to eject the prisoner who wee fighting with three men. When raking him into custody prisoner struck him twice at the side of the head.—Ser- geant Gill corroborated and the prisoner wets fined 2s. 6d. and costs for the first offence and 103. and costs for the second or three weeks' hard Is hour. RUFFIANLY ASSAULT UPON A WOMAN.--John Peers was charged with assaulting Alice Peers on the night of the 23rd inst. in Nelson-st..—Cimplainant stweil that the prisoner, whilst she was talkiest to his wife, came up and knocked her with great force to the ground arid kicked her. He went away but subsequently returned and repeated the conduct. He loft her in a state of in- sensibility and bleeding profusely from the injuries he he had inflicted. —Witnesses were called who supported this statement and the prisoner was sentsnced to gao for one month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>I get I defl</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WIDNES POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>WIDNES POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>MoNDAY.—Before Mr. 11. W. Deacon and Councillor</p>
        <p>W. w. GrNsage.</p>
        <p>DRUNIKENNESS.-11.Targarvt McCann, 9, Railway-street, and Annie Jones, no fixed abcde, were finel for this offence.</p>
        <p>BVV.ACH OF THE PEACE.—Catiwrint, Hynes, Marsh-3t., was houna over t.) keep the peac, for three months. GABDFN THEFTS.—.-Seven lads nained Patrick Tyrell (a'!ed 13), 35, Ca.therine-st. ; Thomas Tanse,), (15), 1. Railway-sr. ; James Cullen (14),33. Catherine-st. ; James Broadhurst (15),20,Catherine-sn. ; Thomas :.:rewart (19). Elizabeth-st. ; James Tyrell (15), 35, Catherine-st. ; and John Tansey (17), 1, llailway-sr., wcre charge.] with tealing a quantity of roses, P-ttur.n.s. :"nd googeberries, from the garden's of Mrs. Wilcock. Mr.R"ginald Calland, and others, at Ditton, on Sunday last. They all pleaded guilty. All the lads except Thomas 'l'ansey and James Cullen were fined ss. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>FRODSFIAM PETTY SE;!SIONS</head>
        <p>FRODSFIAM PETTY SE;!SIONS</p>
        <p>MONDAY. —II-fore Messy... A. Thotn.s and T. Rigby. INTOLICATED ON LICENSED PRE3TtsEs.—Davirl Aston, iros suntinoued for being drunk on the licensed pre- mises of the •' Belle Monte" Inn, Frodsham, on the 9th</p>
