      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>- . , , _ _ . - - _ -</p>
        <p>inst.—P.C. Davies deposed to visiting the public-house mentioned and to finding the defendant in a drunken condition in the taproom. He had no beer before him. When witness called the landlord's attention to the de- fendant's condition, and asked for an explanation, the landlord replied that the man had just that moment entered the house. He had asked for drink but had not been served. Defendant was forthwith ei•cted, and when the constable spoke to him outside the house he became very abusive.—Fined 53. and Bs. BJ. costs or an alternative of fourteen days' hard labour. REMOVED HIS FURNITURE TO Avow HISTRAINT.—JOS. Oakes. of FrOOSIIMM, was summoned at the instance of John Hughes, estate agent, for clandestinely removing , his furniture, not exceeding the value of £5, for the purpose of avoiding distraint.—John Hughes stated that the defendant had tenanted a cottage on an estate for , which he collected the rent. He fell into arrears to such I an extent that he was compelled to apply for an order to distrain. On the 16th April, however, the defendant removed his goods so that there was nothing to distrain. The amount of arrears which prosecutor claimed was 3.54. 3,l.—Fined ss. and costs or fourteen days' imprison- n2eut with hard labour. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ A STRAYING HORSE.—Joseph Ellams was fined ss. incluLlin,s.,!' costs for allowing his horse to stray on the public highway at Helsby on the 17th June. In default of pa merit he would have to go to gaol for seven days. SCIIOOL ATTENDANCE CASES.—For neglecting to send their children regularly to school the following persons were fine'd 6d. and 4s. 6/i. costs :—Mary Eilen Myth, Thomas Moores, Audrew Ross, and Thomas Ja.nnion.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 30, 1894.1</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 30, 1894.1</p>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Mr. W. C. Jones (chairman), Messrs. H. Nield, and C. H. Talbot. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—For this offence at Apple- ton on the 19:h inst. at Walton, George Lemon, a coloured man employed on the Ship Canal, was fined ss. including costs. CHARGE AGAINST A WARRINGTON FISH DEALER.---reter Lawless, fish dealer, Warrington. was charged with hav- ing on the 18:11 inst. at Walton, furiously driven a spring cart on the highway, and further charged with unlaw- fully and cruelly illtreating and abusing the horse by beating it.—P.C. Shetwell stated that on the 16:h inst. he was on duty on the Warrington-road, Walton Superior, when he saw the defendant driving a horse at- tached to a fish cart, at a furious rate. Defendant was standing up in the cart and flogging the horse, which was going as fast as it could. When the defendant got near Walton Church, lie broke the whip on the animal. He stopped to pick the whip up, and witness went to him and told him that he would report him. There were ten or twelve marks on the anima; where the de- fendant had struck it with the whip. The magistrates imposed a fine of 54. and costs for furious driving, and dismissed the defendant on the charge of cruelty to the horse.</p>
        <p>REFUSING TO QUlT.—Patrick Cunningham, labourer, Appleton, was charged with being drunk on the licensed premises of the Mulberry Tree Inn, on the 17th June, and also with refusing to quit when requested so to do. P.C. Brew stated that he was sent for to the Mulberry Tree Inn on the date named when the defendant was there drunk. Witness asked him to leave, which he re- fused to do, and witness then put him out.—Superin- tendent Atherton stated that the defendant took up a glass belonging to another persisn. The landlady refused to serve him on account of his thunkenr.ess. He was fined the costs of the proceedings. DOG AT LARGE.—Frederick Newall, tanner, Aston, for allowing his dog to be at large on the 16th inst. at Pres- ton Brook, was fined 2s. 83. WITHOUT A LIGHTED LAMP.—Frederick Staner, Bud- worth, was charged with having on the 2nd inst. at Antrobus ridden a bicycle on Warrington-road at 9.45 p.m., without having a lighted lamp attached thereto. A fine of 2s. 6d. and costs was imposed. Is Z,:EARCII OF GAME.—Timothy Brannon and Richard Dunn, both of Warrington, were charged with commit- ting a trespass by entering upon certain land in the pos- session and occupation of Mr. Samuel Horton, in search of conies on the 10th inst.—Mr. Samuel Horton, farmer, Cirappenhall, stated that he saw the two defendants on the date named, on his land, in company with the two dogs, i lumber and a greyhound. The dogs were run- ning where there were some rabbit burrows. He had cautioned one of the defendants before. Brannon was fined ss. and costs, and Dunn 53. including costa. TEMPORARY AIITHORITY.—Mary Ann Cowap, applied for temporary authority to sell intoxicants in a t.nt at Little Leigh on the occasion of the Druids anniversary from two o'clock until ten.—Superintendent Atherton obj,,cted and said nine o'clock was plenty long enaugh for the applicant to sell in a tent. The applicant agreed to close at nine o'clock and the application was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the 1131 lie of Commons on nmday evening Sir E. Grey informed Sir E. A. Bartlett that, in compliance with a request of the King of the Belgians, Her M,,jesty's G.vernment had signed an agreement with him under which article 3 of the Congo Agreement of the 12th May was withdrawn. In reply to Mr. S. Smith, Mr. Sillw-LEFFXRE said he hati.determined that there should be a toll inquiry into the management of the schools of the Hackney Union. He would defer his decision as to whether there should be an investigation into the conduct of workhouse schools generally until after tha Hackney inquiry. Mr. Asuurrn stated, in reply to Mr. Hopwood, that the cases of prisoners under long sentences were system- atically considerud by the Home Office at specii in terva</p>
        <p>The House agil went into eranmittee on the Budget Bill, after Sir Wifliain Ilare.urt had expressed himself hopeful that the C.,intsi:ttee stae vroutd be brought to a &amp;lie in the present week. Progre,,swa report,..d, and the buss aijourued at 1.2 35.</p>
        <p>In the or Lords 7,, id FIAWREFEURY stated that Local Government Board had decided to continue for another year the special precautions which had been observed at ports to prevent the introduction of cholera. The Crur4tv to Children Bill and the Notice of Accidents Bill passed through cominittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening SIR Witeeam HancoutiT, in accordance with notice, moved a vote of sympathy, with the French nation in the calamity whieli has befallen it. Mr. BALFOUR seconded the motion. He said the message ot that House was a national expression of a great national feeling. Mr. M'Carthy added the tribute of the Irish party, and the resolution was agreed to. Sir E. Gewit, ens% eling a questinn.said the diplomatic bedc at Pekin had informed the Caincese Government that they could not recognise the notification of the Shanghai Customs authorities restricting the importa- tion of machinery as in any way binding on foreign subjects. The House again went into committee on the Budget Bill. Clause 18 to 22 having been added to the bill, clause 23. which imposes an additional duty of sixpence on every 36 gallons of beer was considered. Colonel , LOCKWOOD moved an amendment that the extra duty I should he only threepence. Mr. WHITBREAD said that having as a friend of the Government made an appeal to the Chancellor if the Exchequer and got a promise that the impost wac only to be a temporary one.he could not now turn round. ME.GOSCHEN asked the Chancellor lof the Exchequer to say that the tax was onlyln he temporary. The House divided at midnight, with the result that the amendment was reje,cted by 289 to 271 —Government majority, 18. The clause increasing the I beer duty ey sixpence per barrel was then agreed to, land the House adjourned.</p>
        <p>In the 110 We of Lords the Earl of ROSEBERY moved the vote of sympathy with the French nation. In so cloing he rdferreu to the other great events which were r,corded simultaneously in the newspapers,and spoke of the lives of the unfortunate Welsh miners as being "each of them as dear to their own- kith and kin as any President or Prince can he to theirs." We did not, his Lordship went on to say, know as touch perhaps al was to be known of the strange creed of the Anarchists, but we might be certain that President Carnot was assas- sinated tr.- an Ishmaelite whose hand was against every man and who thought to strike a blow at the very heart of the State by striking at its head. The Marquis of Samsnurtv, in seconding the motion, agreed with Lord Rosebery that the atrocious opinions, professed by the Anarchists severed them from the community of any nation with which they wer- . naturally connected. This crime represented none of the known divisions of politi- cal opinion ; it was it mark of some strange madness which had thlcen hold of the more excited and irritable intellects of a half-educated class, and must in the future necessarily be the parent of precautions. The resolution was agreed tn. WEDNESD A Y.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday, in Commit- tee on the Budget Bill, consideration of clause 24, which places an additional •Int: on spirits. was resumed. Dr. MaeGnEoott moved Sr ainc.ndruent to exemp!: Scotland from the increased duty. He objected to the tax as a Scotch Nationalist, and al.o as a consumer, remarlciwz amid laughter, tnat the water supply of Se.Atiand was peculiarly suited to the of whisky. He said the people would have a nArcotie of some kind. Weill should he aimed at was regulation and not prohibition. ! Sat W. HARCOURT. in a humorous reply, hoped Dr. Mae- Gregor wou!d not; press his nrolon for a differential ditty between the two countries. Several other ameridtunts were withdrawn, and The clause was added to tip-. hi Snit NV.llAncorwr a ftprrvircis inceved that the nmin ques- t:Ml ht, now put. A division was rhanengell. The WAS carried by a majority (4 55, and there was also a Gorernm-ot majoriry of 55 for the clause taking spirits. whirl] was carried amid loud Ministeria: cheers. Progress v::es then reported, and the House adjourned. SEALSKIN JACKETS AND FURS of every ,iesclulion CLHANi.D or Re4-DYND and kLTERED. FUR 4r-Issol ant Trade up into rugs, Coog2, Manutactirine; Funr;er. 11, Upwr Iw lag- tout, nut.? 21, Renstrtw-atreA, Liverp,»l. EstaMishe,l 20</p>
      </div>
