        <p>Majority for Winningtou 113</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL SANITARY AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting was held on Tues- day afternoon at the 14itton Workhouse. Mr. J. Mclntyre presiding. The other gentlemen present were the Revs. J. W. N. Tanner, and J. Ihn Messrs. H. E. Heywood, J. Brandreth, S. Wood- ward, T. Baxter, CO., C. T. Whitley, J. Ashton, clerk ; G. Ashton, deputy-clerk ; Farrington, nuisance. inspector; and Dr. Adams, medical officer of health.</p>
        <p>FPODSHAM PAIIOCIIIAL COMMITTER.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Cotnrnittee was lt, let on the 22nd inst. under the presidency of Mr. A. •fltoinos.—The Clerk read a letter from the Local Government Board in answer to one from the Committee on the 26-h ult. with reference to the application of the Ituno‘,rn Rural Sani- tary Authority for the issue of an crier fixing a general scale of eh&amp; rges for the supply of water from the Frod- sham Waterworks to houses on premi,les in the townships of Frodsham and Frodshatn Lorciship.—ln answer to the query the Local Government Board stated that they would direct a local inquiry to be held in due course on the subject by one of their Inspectors.—The Clerk was authorised to reply to the effect that the inquiry could be held in the Town Hall, Frodsham.—The Superintend- ing Engineer submitted his certificate for the payment of £550 to Messrs. Ebenezer Tirmnins and Sons, on further account of their contract for the construction of works of water supply and the same was passed for pay- ment.—The Superintendent reported that the works had been so far completed, and that the contractors had that day commenced pumping into the reservoir.—lt was resolved that the Waterworks Sub-committee be re- quested to prepare a plan as to extending the water mains and other matters necessitating a further loan and an estimate of the amount required and submit the same to the next meeting of the Committee.—The minutes were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>REMUNERATION OF THE CLERK.</head>
        <p>REMUNERATION OF THE CLERK.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wr-,te with reference to the application of the Authority for sanction to grant their Clerk extra remuneration for his services in con- nection with the various parochial committies and ask- ing whether the Clerk was formally appointed to perform those daties.—The Clerk replied that he had not been formally appointed hut attended the meetings in his capacity as Clerk to the Authority. TYPHOID FEVER AT WESTON POINT. A latthr was received from the Runcorn Commis- sioners referring to a case of typhoid fever which had occurred on the schooner " Lizzie "at Westoe_point., within the district of the Authority. The Commissioners stated that if the Authority would pay them 30s. per week the man would be received into the Infectious Diseases Hospital. and suggested that it would be wiser. for the Authority to make such arrangoments with the captain of the " Lizzie."—The Clerk reported that he had pointed out to the Commissioner's clerk that the fee which the Authority had agreed to pay was Li is. per week for each patient. Dr. McKenzie had given a certifi- cate to the effect that the man was suffering from enteric fever. A justice's order had been obtained for the. man's removal to the hospital, and that order had been carried out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>HOSPITAL AT MOORE.</head>
        <p>THE HOSPITAL AT MOORE.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. M. Dunlop writing on behalf of the Manches- ter Ship Canal in reference to the hospital at Moore,said that the hospital was no longer used for the navvies and asked for a small sum to be paid for the privilege of allowing the hospital to remain there.—The matter was referred to the GPMMittee. MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams, in his fortnightly statement reported that two cases of scarlet fever existed at Appleton, Stockton Heath one at Sutton, one at Preston Grook, aim one of typhoid on a vessel lying in the Delamere D.-mk, at Weston Point. He had taken the usual precautionary measures for the prevention of the spread of the disease. He further stated that he had received a letter from Mr. Jones,s,...hool attendance officer. stating that a number of cases of whooping cough prevailed in his district. There were also several cases of measles at Frodsham Bridge.— The report was adopted. FINANCIAL STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brandreth suhmitted the report of the Finance Committee which showed that the balance in the trea- surer's hands amounted to £332 18. sd. The expendi- ture amounted to £7Bl is. 6d. which included £550 on account of the Frodsliam water supply scheme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>PLAN.</head>
        <p>PLAN.</p>
        <p>The plan of a further addition to premises situate at Netherton was submitted and approved in aPeordatice with the renommendation of the Frodsham Lordship Par- ochial Sanitary Committee.</p>
        <p>RUNNING WATER PIPE AT EINCSLEY.</p>
        <p>A tender —w-; ;11.--bmi&amp;d- for tta work of improving this pipe, and the same was referred to the local com- mittee appointed to consider the matter.</p>
        <p>'WATER SUPPLY TO PREMISES AT ASTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Farrington reported that certain houses situee at Aston were without a water supply within reasonable distance, and ti,tice was ordered to be served for a supply to be provided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>SHEFFIELD VACANCY.</head>
        <p>THE SHEFFIELD VACANCY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Liberal Association in the Attercliffe (Sheffield) division on Saturday, Mr. Alder-. man Batty Langley aceepted the invitation to contest the division in the Liberal intcrPst, expressed himself heartily in favour of Home Rule, and gave his views on other important questions. ills decision was received with much enthusiasm, and arrangements were made to carry on a vigorous campaign. The Labour party also held a mecting,and sat for five hours. In consequense of the acceptance of Mr. Batty Langley as the Liberal candidate, the candidature of Mr. Charles Hobson was withdrawn ; but the Labour party passed a resolution expressing its "surprise and disgust "at the action of the Lqoeral party in opposing Mr. Hobson. The Labour party at Sheffield have decided to bring out, a candidate of their own for the vacancy in the Atterclifie division. They have chosen as their repre- sentatine Mr. Frank Smith, of Lambeth, who wa,s for- merly associateJ with General Booth in the work of the Salvation Army, and he addressed a large meeting of workingmen in the constituency on Wednesday night. The writ was received in Sheffield on Wednesday, and the nominallons were fixed for Monday next and the election for Thursday.</p>
        <p>WORTH KNOWING.—WOODEND'S FAMOIJS NINE OILS. i boon to sufferPra and Lets like magic in all ca4eB of RHEUMATISM, SPRAINS, BRUISES, and ST! FF JOINTS. Price Is. 4d. oer bottle. carriage paid.—J.WoonExn. chemist. 22, Pitt-street. Liverpool.—AdeZ.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. R. Cha.dwick the well-known breeder of shire horses in Runcorn on Monday, at the Royal AgricWtural Show gained the first prize in the acrricultural horses class for a gelding foaled in 1890 or 1891.</p>
        <p>troyal Laundresses say Critchley's " S'rannti GLoAn Is unequalled for impartina a beantifnl finish to starched lin ,n, Focc., either eoid or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Corn- wa'l. says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without your "Starch Gloss." iron Mrs.—, 'Bradford "Dear sit . am pleased to inform you that I have tried many 0(1-e-tiled Starch Glasris and Polishes, hut find none equal to yours." It should he used in every house. Sold at 14,, 34., and 64.„hy most stArch selle7s. If not in stodc will procure it. T. Ca mom, i; 7. Btselchlirn, P:oprietor.— id?.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DINNER 'lO IHE RU.NCORN RUGBY FOOIBALL CLUB.</head>
        <p>DINNER 'lO IHE RU.NCORN RUGBY FOOIBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>-- PRESENTATION OF THE LEAGUE MEDALS Mr. Phillip Speakman, J.P., C.C., president of the club, occupied the chair at a dinner given on Fri- day evening at Wilsou'a Hotel, Runcorn, to dr players in both teams on the occasion of the bution of the medals won by the second team Ir- the competition for the championship of the Liver-, pool and District Junior Rugby League. The even:, irate • been organized by the president Dr. McKenzie, Messrs. G. Shone, T. Whinyates, and others who wished to en-e courage the growth of the institution. The compaDY numbered fiityetwo, amongst whom were taose gentle- men already named, with Captain Easeley, Richard Abel, ,Aithur Lightburn, Henry Mason, "''• Jones, J. S. P. Sunpson, Win. Carroll, J. E. Long6baw, secreu:try ; and J. assistant secretary. Tt4e repast was of a most sumptous nature, and was artreir- ably served by Mr. Dyer. The cloth having been removed Mr. Speakman pro-.. posed the usual loyal toasts which were heartily respondec' to. Mr. G. Shone then proposed "The Commissioners coupling with it the name of Mr. S. Taelor, Chairman ot the Board. In the course of his observations Ore proposer said that probably this would be the last ocea- skin upon which their local governing body would hn toasted, and it would die a natural death on the ar: November when the Local Government Act woule begin to operate. During the forty-two years ss'llic" ttie Board bad existed, much good work. had been done by its members, a.uti the town had rapidly progressed in many respects. (Applause.) 'Tile Board had a vise': weight of responsibility resting upon their shoulders, and they bore it notay despite the aspersions toe fre- quently cast upon them. In the absence of Mr. Taylor, Mr. Richard Ate', re- sponded to the compliment saying that he for one hae always dune the best he could in the past, and wenill contiuue so to do in the future. He understood thee the club was in a little oirliculty financially- and he - would be only too pleased to help them out if a can ..vas- made upon him to that effect. (Loud applause.) Mr. Whinyates io proposing " duccess to the nuacera Footisall Club" said he was sure it was the wish of _every: loyal Runcorniau Was, the wearers of the green , meet with equal, if nut more, success in the future then in the pa›t. It was a gratifying rtfleeion that the Hurl- Dern Football Club had W Oil its way from the botteint!st. • ring of the ladder to the top. lucre were few cluD3 1; any, which could rival the performances of their owil club for the past five or six yeass, and considering the limited area from which they had to draw their players. those performances were surprising when compared 'e) Biadford, Liverpool, Oldimin, Manchester, and sues) places, Runcorn was a mere village, but it proudly belei its own in the footbali world and he liopal it ever would do so. (Applause.) The club, he was sorry to say, had' a financial balance on the wrong side winch had beets caused by extraordinary expenditure in various improve-- inehts and additions sorely no ded for a long time. Tbise . Was not, however, any cause for alarm, and if the mere- hers would only prove loyal to themselves and the cletY then that incubus would disappear and its place be , occupied by a balance on the 'eget side. (Applause.) Indeed, so iong as they bad at their head as president a „- gentlemen poseessiiig such ability ar.d influence as Mr- 1 Speakman no anticipation of failure need be entertained by either the members or general public. With the , toast he was very pleased to conneA the name of their president, Mr. Spaikman. (Applause.) Mr. Speakman in response thanked them for thele kicd references to him and added that it was a great gratite. ca.eon to him personably to belong to such a club ". With regard to the financial position ot the club, he evas not at all dismayed at their adverse balatice,and theught • that with the hearty co-operation of players and rbe public they would soon rid themselves of it. Then,witil their increased freedoin,their power would daily increase. ' (Applause.) He was very glad to find that the Cricket Club had been associated with the Football Club. It liad forcibly struck him, a few days recently, when reading.' s• an account of a comical football match, that something of the same nature might be arraeged for Runcorn, onIF that the cricketers should be aetors and not the foot- bailers. He felt sure that those present would take the matter up warmly and manage to make something oat. • of it for the benefit of the Football Club. (Applause.) Dr. McKenzie submitted the toast of the "A" Teams and connected with it the name of Mr. G. Robinson. He remarked that he had taken great pride in the doings of the team, as they had been called "'rho Decter-5 Boys." (Applause.) Their achievements during the past season had been most extraordinary, when it evali considered what a number or heavy calls had been made upon theirs by the first team. So long as he was in corn so so long would he look after their interests. (Ap- plause.) 'The Chairman intimated that the most important' event of the evening was this disribution of medals to the 2nd team, which had won the rare reputation of not having tasted defeat that season. The all-impertant object that night was to do honour to those to who!honour was due, in recognition of their wonderful pr.- formances. It was known to the world what vietorree the Runcorn Football Club had achieved in the field a - sport, and with such a splendid second team to draw from, the club should continue its triumphant march until football ceased to he a sport. (Applause.) fie advised tiv members of both teams to preserve their good characters, for in the football field such a charac- ter was always particularly noted as a sign of stabilityHe was pleased to find that they hada good ceintnittee, and with such a capital secretary and treasurer their finances ought surely to improve. With such efficient teams they would not only seen wipe off the debt upon the club, but would hold their wwn in the future as je the past. (Applause.) Otlier toasts were submitted and honoured in hearty' good fashion, the club committee and the gentlemen- responsible for that enemy's proceedings all coming for a fair amount of praise,to which Messrs. J. E. Long- shave' and Carroll responded. . In acknowledging the thanks of the members for hiO presence in the chair that evening Mr. Speakman said that it was always a pleasure to him n to preside over their gatherings. He had always been closely allied with the noble game of football and there were not Incl. institutions in the town which had kept their colours so fresh as the football club had kept theirs. (App'ailso• It was very pleasing to see that so much good feeling existed in the ranks of the football community, vcn'' appeared extremely anxious to assist the club out of itS financial difficulties. The remainder of the evening was spent in a spirit conviviality,the proceedings taking the form of an enter taininent, songs being sung by Messrs. Whinva.tes. jollYt Warder, Mason, and Itillingsverth. The tnedas are prettily designed, being the work of Messrs. Alexander , Bros. Liverpool, On the front of them is inscribed )he. word "Runcorn," whilst on the back are the initials ot the Liverpool and District Rugby Football League diam'. pions, and the name of each recipient.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I. I Jo</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS,</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Mr. John Mclntyre presided over the usual fort- nightly meeting of the Guardians held at Duttos Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon. There were also present the Revs. J. W. N. Tanner, Dr. Gastaldi,and J. Hanby, Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P., J. Brandretb. E. Heywood, S. Yarwood, T. Handley, T. Lea, AY- Norman, C. T. Whitley, T. Baxter,C.C..S. Woodward. T. Lewis, J. Ashton, clerk, and G. Ashton, &amp;pal ' clerk. THE PAVING OF THE GIRLS YARD AT THE woaKHousr.,, The Clerk read a letter from Messrs. Dunbar, and Co., Liverpool, asking if the question of paYini-' the yard at the Workhouse was still open. They eluded by saying that they understood that the Bog' "r had decided to use the granite concrete flags, and on tr.. strength of this they had made a large quantity to 5"3" the order.—Mr. Norman asked how this twitter present. Had the suggestion of the Ihard been carriet; out regarding the repairing of the yard ? The substitc; ted plan mentioned by the Board had apparently ro,,j been adopted.—Mr. Heywood s,iid they had decided ty asphalte the yard, but last week the yard was in tir same condition.—lr. Woodward said in his opinion tn: boys' yard also required repairing in a similar manne'' —the Chairman hoped that the members of the Corl; mittee would take notice of the matter and get the WO one.</p>
        <p>THE LADY-VISITING COMMITTEE. The Clerk reported that the Committee of the 130aral constituted to consider the advisability of eleCting committee of ladies to visit certain parts of the W°lll., house. had met that morning and passed a resolution 1`; favour of the proposal. They had drawn up a nurnber,- of rules which were similar to those of the Stoeltr,:g' Union.—Mr. T. Loa Proposed that the report of t 73. committee be adopted —Mr. Samuel Yarwood seconde and it was agreed to.—Dr. Gastaldi then mnvod th" a the number of the comini,tee he seven, which v 7.1 seconded by Mr. Lea.—Mr. Woodward brought forwa4'4. an amendment•that the number be nine. This v° seconded by Mr. Fleywood, and after a S,VoVeffit coona- siderable discussion, was carried.— dr. HandltlY nrthe- that the committee consist of Mrs. Wood. wife 01,,,ir5. Rev. Canon Wool, Runcorn; Mrs. F. J. Norman. Mangan, wife of Dr. Mangan, Miss Dotton. Mooro Frodham ; Miss Rest, Aston L-pige, Baxter, Dutton Hall ; Mrs. Marsden, Aston s • Iteuton, Fredsham.—These names were agree(' tO. FINANCE. Mr. Brandreth stated that the accounts for Ply- 'op amounted to £494 104 101. 'Having a balance in the. treasurer's hands ot £4.633 134. 91. He read os',ertheir names of those townships whioh had not. cPt pai" „to- calls which were duo on the 19`.it May,and it was eve”tilic; ally decided that letters be sent to them reque9 immediate payment.</p>
