      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>LIFE'S TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>LIFE'S TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>BY MR. H. WHITHABf.</p>
        <p>In the article of last week I endeavoured to draw attention to, tne great amount of unrest and tills- quietude existing among the masses. 1 have made nu attempt to drew this blacker than it really is. l'ne difficulty lies rather in selecting trdin the vast amouut of matter to hand. Judging from what one reads in the daily press, either tile cheap daily, or the more expensive monthly this unrest is not con- fined to the American continent, but pervades the whole of the European continent. It uues nut even stay here, but from the very complete reports 1 hate near me, 1 gather that in the manufacturing dis- tricts of India it is very much more serious and is likely at no distant date to give We British Govern- ment the largest problem to solve it ever had. As a socianst, I. should like to Rime our friend .%Ir. Peers to consider the situation calmly, and to put down in black and white the features in the social programme he condemns, as also, the charac- teristic features of individualism he SO frantically supports. It 1 understand my Spencer aright " lieneral happiness will only be gained by a juet blending of altruism with egoism." It was only in primitive states of socieiy that jas- ti.le meant a balancing of aggression uy counter aggression, in the developed Imuuern justice men recognise such limitations of conduct as exclude aggressions altogether. Absolute morality, demands I that couduc flall be regulated in soca a way as I not to inflict pain. " Lai men as they are and society as it is, WO dictates of absolute moraliky are impracticable." If this is true, and I take it that no one will deny the position, are not Socialists• justified in feeling their way towards better- things ? It is a true saying, that Sociaiism seeks to rob no man of that part of liiinseit wincli makes true manhood,any more than true Liberalism would deprive the State of that Conservatism which gives it its true glory. A very apt illustration may here be given from Spencer, " Pure Altruism is like a cluster of bodies generating neat, each of which will have, so long as it generates beat for 'welt, a certain amount of proper heat and a cenainamount of heat derived from the others, whereas the whole cluster will become cold as SOOll as each ceases to generate tor itself, since over-indulgence destroys pleasure." I hope I have made it clear that Social- ism seeks only to destroy that which is worthless, and will reinstate that which is best in 'man. Of course, I am aware of the possibility °I difference as to what is worthless and what is bast.</p>
        <p>We should agree that the Micawber ' state of mind is worthless. We should agree that the "Golden Rule," Whoever its author may have been, is best. Mr. Vanderbilt is reported to be worth £20;000,000. Part of this was inherited, the rest may be toe results of his own great energies,mental, and physical. It is suggested that by his keen busi- ness talents, and a fair duration of life, he may yet become the sole controller of the whole of the American railwey system. I would ask those who defend unrestrained individualism, or shall I say, pure 'selfishness to put in a delence here. I can- not.—A German millionaire recently said :—" No man can can become very rich wittiout brushing his sleeve against the penitentiary."• Business men and professional men speak with an air of cynicism about all the men of really great wealtu. We may reasonably ask, What man of great wealth is there who could persuade the community as a whole that his Christianity is genuine ? Mr. Chamberlain some years ago put before us the new idea of" unearned increment." This may in its turn suggest the idea of the proprietary rights of the people in natural monopolies. Then what about railroads, street cars, gas works, water works telegraphs, &amp;c., &amp;c. If us Warrington it is granted that some of these belong to the town awl some to the state, then the principal contended for by the socialists is conceded and we say that the Individ- ualists, like Home Rule, bloc;:s the way. If the Christian Church as represented by Mr. Barleton glories in not taking money from brevvers, so it , ought to put the question to every large beuelactor " Where did you get your money "? People wonder how it is the christian Church does not achieve pore, may not the answer be, that it depends upon support derived from sources that are accursed in the sight of Jesus, tile son of a carpenter. We can- not gather figs from thistles to day any more than they could eighteen hundred years ago. The whole wage system is covered by the ques- tion of surplus values and profits, and these have all been challenged by fair-minded men who had no idea of posing as socialists. Even- the simple , question of lending money, and, getting interest I has received. the attention of such men as Moses, I Aristotle, and Jeremy Bemtharn, with a result not I comforting to the mind of the money lender. I People shiver in their shoes when they hear tell of labour riots, or of Anarchists. • Do. tney ever I inquire deeper and search for the Cause. L)ok at the province of Quebec when English enterprise is taxed remorselessly, and wages are at the very lowest, yet the Church of Rome has over 100,000,000 dols. worth of untaxed property. It is the opinion of the President that in 1900 the untaxahle church Droperty in the United States will amount to the astounding sum of 3,000,000,000. All this untax- able property whether in England, Italy,or America is a dead weight upon the shoulders of labour, and demands the attention of the individualist unless he is lost to all reason and common sense. I do not, by any means, exempt labour from its share of blame. Indeed, lam disposed to lay the chief part of the blame on its shoulders. We see now, how needless was Robert Lowe's dread of giv- ing the masses the suffrage... They have done worse than nothing with it. • Mr. Bryce, in his book on the American Commonwealth - says:—" In the United States, although the legislature is largely com- posed of lawyers,many. of these have little practice, little knowledge, comparatively little professional feeling, and he further. admits they have " no hold on politics." No wonder he has to say further on, "They say, there are rich and powerful corporations willing to pay vast sums for questionable services." Referring more directly to the question of labeur, the refusal, or inability of Trades Unionism to move along on the lines marked out for it by the spirit of the age renders it some- thing like a• broken cog in machinery. The low wage earner has to pay the wages of the high Wage earner. The Park Commissioners of New York re- cently offered to pay bricklayers 14s. a day for eight hours' work, this was refused and Ws. was the lowest sum that would be accepted. The power of the union is absalate consequently a p .Htir labourer earning only ss. or 6s. a day must pay for a couple of rooms 10s. and 12s. a week rent. Those who run may read. Unionism must become uni- versal and international or skip. It has become rampant Individualism. A writer in the "Chicago Times" writes :—" I have witnessed scenes of pitiful *ant, of brutal treatment, of ignorant ferocity, besides which. the wildest stories of slavery in the squth were tame and insipid. . I have seen men Working ten hours, their only repast being crusts of stale bread. I have seen them driven from place to place like dumb, driven cattle at tire muzzles of Winchesters. • I have seen them driven with their, wives and babies from their rude shanties into the storm, there huddling together with mute misery in their eyes. I have seen tender children thrown into jail and left for a whole day wailing with hunger, thirst, and fear. I have seen them hunted like wild animals, and driven like runaway slaves to prison, all because they were not, content to work like slaves for 50 and 75 cents a day, paid at the truck stores, for the glory of Carnegie and protection to American labour.'" •</p>
        <p>• Let it not be forgotten • that 'I am pointing out I some of the causes for the social unrest we see around 11P, I see in unrestrained Individualism the demon that is robbing matt of his personality. Car- negie said he would as Soon leave his son his curse as leave him a rich man. A writer in a representa- tiveChrtstian paper says, " Christian men should scrutinize the sources of their income under the eleirest light which the Spirit of Christ and eco- nomic science can shed upon .them, and should re- linquiSli whatever is not begotten of justice," We are getting into line whatever our opponents may thinkpf it. A tnan is indged at once as shift- less and not worth consideration if he fails to rustle around and get a living. Twelve months' experience has taught the present writer something about it. In the.town I write in there is scarcely a foreman to be found whether On the street or in the factory who will give a jab without, being first. bribed. I have knnwa as much as ..Cl5 to</p>
        <p>be paid ,by a novice for the more right to work al-. a Wire block. The man who took it had taken hun- dreds of pounds to introduce Armenians and Turks to the same work. This foreman got at lengt) into a shooting bother over it and was discharged. His successor, a regular :Mender at the communion table, managed it differently. He serves in one of the dry goods stores,has a good commission on his sales and always knows those who work under him. Na- tionalities in some cases subscribe weekly sums to- wards a fund for the purpose of helping into a lob their £riehdB Ss therhrrivei •</p>
        <p>" Lives of toadies oft remind us' We may to high places climb, And by suction leave behind us Those who've served a longer time. Suction that perhaps another Nobler spirit would disdain, And to save his own grandmother To employ it would not deign." Socialists may well go on their way rejoicing. The company they journey in is ever on the increase. Help comes from every side,even though solitary and ill-considered attempts at communimin may in certain corners of the world fail. If I find time before I leave this part of the world, I will attempt to show how even nature hArself is at work helping man to help himself. In conclusion I may give an extract frora an essay by the Rev. Professor Shuttleworth, entitle,i "The Problem of Poverty." "Poverty and the conditions which make for poverty are shown to be the prim- ary causes of enormous proportion of the sin of London and England, and that the Church cannot hope to deal with the sin without attacking the cause."</p>
        <p>DR, GORNALL AND TEE WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The" British Medical Journal "of June 16th,says with regard to the treatment Dr. Gornall has re- ceived from the Warrington Board of Guardians, " We cannot refrain from expressing our mingled astonishment and regret, at the treatment which Dr. Gornall has received at the hands of the guar- dians of the Warrington ,Union. The local press states that the guardians have determined Dr. Gornall's contract as public vaccinator, on the grounds that he is health officer of the borough, and has a population of some 60,000 persons to cater for in the way of gratuitous vaccination. He has been a servant of the Poor- law body for upwards of twenty years, and has in that time, vaccinated a number oE people nearly equal to the present population of the town. Why then have the guardians put an end to his contract? Seemingly on grounds altogether untenable as rea- sons sufficing to account for the step taken. Dr. Gornall as health officer, is looked upon as a person dangerous to the public health .on account of the fact that he will of necessity have to come across infectious places, -which might lead to til?, spread of disease through the medium of his office as pub- lic vaccinator. Illas ever such a paltry excuse given? As well decline to allow a medical attendant to vaccinate a child beca use be has a wide practice. Indeed, the reason would be the more cogent in this latter case,since a private practitioner is daily going direct from house to house on his rounds, and at- tending all kinds of complaints. The one and the other have to take all reasonable care that disease is not spread by their action. There is too, an utter absence of evidenee that disease has ever been spread by Dr. Gornall in the discharge ok. his public ditties. But still more unreason- able is the second point which has been brought.l forward by a guardian as tending to show why Dr. Gornall should go. It was stated, quite erioneously, that the Local Government Board did not advise districts being held by one man for vaccination purposes which had more. than 25,000 people in them. How far this is from the truth will be at once seen by reference to the mem- orandum issued by that Board or vaccination m- rangements, in which it is distinctly laid down that those districts are the best which have up- wards of 50,000 population. Birmingham has but one public vaccinator under the sanction of the guardians, and the London 'Board, and the district- beside which, the population of Warrington sinks into small proportions are not few ; but even so, Warrington with its 60,000 persons is a typically, favourable district for purposes of lymph choice and we trust that the attempt to divide it, which is in contemplation, will not meet with sanction at the hands of the central authority, the more especially when the reasons for its division are made known to them. Nay, we will go so far as to hope that the Board will require to know why Dr. Gornall is to be dismissed in this summary manner after his long and faithful service .0 War- rington, and will refuse to allow his contract to be determined in the irregular way in which it is reported to have been done, in direct opposition to the ruling of the legal adviser of the Guardians." A'notice has been sent .to each member of the Warrington Board of Guardians by Mr. J. C. Sutton, the clerk, to the effect that at the Board meeting to be held on Wednesday, the 27th inst., Mr. S. Davies will move the following resolution : " Unit the resolutions passed .at the Board meeting on May 30th lastaipon the subject of determining the appointment of Dr. J. U. Gornall as public vaccinator and of advertising for candidates for the office of public vaccinator, be rescinded, and that the contract with Dr. Gornall be continued as hitherto."</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</p>
        <p>HOMING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>This Society held the first of this seaslon's con- tinental races from Valognes (France) last week. Distance 274 miles in the straight line 16 memberz. sending 53 birds. Mesera. Hartle 3 and Collinge convoyers to the Lancashire Federation liberated the birds at 8 o'clock a.m in the light S.-W. wind. Unfortunately these circumstances did not prevail very long, as a thick haze came over the English channel and continued the whole of the day in England. Out of the 53 birds only four arrived home the same day. Mr. Edward Burrows being the fir:t to wire in at 5.42 p.m. The next race is from Avranches 355 miles. - -</p>
        <p>Velocityyds. per minute. I—E. Burrows 2—A. Buckley 3—J. .Houghton.. 723 H. Woodward does a velocity of 659 but is disqualified from the fourth place for telegraphin,, wrong. The'.tollcwing arrived day after. 4—John Tim perley 5—M. D. Woodhead 6—H. Clare 3so* 7—J. Burgess 349 B—H. F. Greenough 9—Wm. Healey 10—F. C. Dixon 330 11.—J. Naylor 329 • 12—R. Barnes *Woo:lhead beats Clare fraoti m of a yard.</p>
