      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WARRINGTON FIRIt'S LONDON PREMISES DESTROYED BY FIRE. •</head>
        <p>A WARRINGTON FIRIt'S LONDON PREMISES DESTROYED BY FIRE. •</p>
        <p>One of the largest fires which has occurred in. London for a long time broke out shortly before. nine o'clock on Thursday last in the congested district of Finst,ury. The scene of the outbreak was Agnes's (cabinet-inakers) Factory,Tabernacle-street, and within an hour a block of buildings fitty yards by thirty deep resembled a furnace. The property involved con- sisted mainly of cabinet and domestic ,furniture factories, and was in the occupation of some eight or ten firms. The full Strength of the Metropo- litan Brigade was in attendance—no . less than twenty-eight steamers and three manuals, several drawn from the outlying districts, being engaged ff)r several hours in preventing an extension of the Confia- . gration. This proved no indonsiderable task, as half. a I gale of wind carried the sheets of flame and showers Of sparks to a considerable distance, whilst the involved area, being situated in the midA of a labyrinth of nar- row streets, -was difficult of approach for the firemen. Toe centre block, which was in the form of a triangle was impossible of salvation, .while the hack of the con- flagration was not broken until. several buildings oppo- site to two aides of the figure had been gutted.' The firm largest involved by the outbreak are Lebus and Sons, cabinet makers whose show- looms and steam factories occupy the greater .part of the devastated area. In Whitefield-street, where occurred one of the subsidiary 'outbreaks, was situ.4ted their , largest factory. which had only rec.ntly been erected, and not yet even tenanted. It occupied the site ot l dozen small cottages which bad been demolished for the purpose. Among the premises destroyed are those belonging to Messrs. Fletcher, Rus.ell and Co., gas enginetr4 of War- rington, Lebtis and .Sons and Agnes, cabinet manufao hirers ; G. Summers, stationers; Barnard Barnard, furniture manufacturer; Smith, bent wood manufae- turer ; Westlake, fancy wood merchant; C. F. Marriner, bedstead manufacturer.; and R. L. Cresbie, bedstead manufacturer. •</p>
