        <head>PERFECT</head>
        <p>A PERFECT</p>
        <p>BOY HEROES OF WAR. - In our Arany we find, says "Chums" that many drummers and buglers h)ve covered themselves with tflory. Some boys will perhaps know that affecting at :ry of. the Ponins,•l3,r War. A little fellow wanieri-d trout his regiment, aid, beinl taken prisoner by the French, was brought b?fore Napoleon, who looked upon him as a spy. A:ket his rank in the British Army, the boy said he was a drummer, whereupon the Emperor ordered a drum to he brought, and commanded him to beat the "charce." Toe lad co mphed with much alacrity. -"Now beat a retreat! " said Napoleon. " I can't," replied the bey. "No' such thin!! is ever known in -the British Army. We never .heat a- retreat." "Very good! " exclaimed the Emperor. "You are a brave boy, and you shall jan your regiment." He then ordered some of the soldiers round him to conduct : the little hero to the E ,glish lines, which the drummer reachmi in safety. He was killed at Waterloo.—During the A'eyssinian expedi!ion a bug ler whose duty it was remain at Lord Napier's side, slipped away as our army approached Magdala, reached the gate before the storm- ing party, and was tile first to dash into the enemy's , stronghold. On returning home he received his reward —he was decorated with the Victoria Cross. Another bugler great:y diStinguiehrid himself at the blowing open of the Cashmere Gate, the grandest exploit- in the Siege of Delhi. his lad formed one "of the party which accomplished this memorable feat, and he was obliged to sound the " charge." three times before it could be heard above the firing of the rebels. He richly deserved the Victoria Cross that. was conferred upon him. —Splendid, too, Was the deed of a (hummer in the Crimea. On evening, in broad daylight, he was observed to leave the trenches before . Sebastopol with a can of tea in his bend. Heedless of the shower of shot and shell ftom the Russian batteries which poured around him, he picked hie way among the wounded gi vine our brave soldiers a drink. vk, hen his tea was all gone, he threw the empty can towards the Russians in a 04;ntemptuous manner, and then walked i aek to the trenches. The noble generosi' yof this lad enabled many to endure their angulidi until alter dark, when parties or our twohs brOUght in the wounded; Sevs-lel, prob- ably, were by hi S timely aotier, saved from death. This fact alone was doubtless sufficient reward for the wonderfully heroic lad ; but he also received the Victoria Cross. which was fastened to his breast by the Queen herself.—Another case in point from -the Crimea, and we have done. In one of the attacks on the Redan youthful lieutenant named Massey stood in a terrible fire, with a calmness that astonished even the enemy, to ii‘duce the soldiers to follow. This teroic courage, in shawing- which he was Very badly wounded, won him the titite of "fl-elan Massey," and when he left the serviceond resumed his studies at the Dublin University his fellow-students presented him with a, magnificent sword, a well-earned treaeure:,</p>
      </div>
