      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WORMS IN CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>WORMS IN CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>WORMS IN CHILDREN. ,01 ere easily, surely, arid with perfect safety got rid of bY -01 KVATING'S WORM TABLETS. Nearly all childreo 9 from Worms. If suspected do not wait, you can witll;,, cure the child (has no effect except ou warns). Olld Cheraiste, in lino, 13. il. oath.</p>
        <p>ITHE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>We are now told that some influential coalowners are again disposed to insist upon a substantial reduction of Wages, and if the Scotch masters suc- ceed in the present contest doubtless the appetite for " reductions " will be still further whetted. In that case another disastrous strike or lock-out is almost inevitable. This is an event which, to say the least, cannot be faced with equanimity in this part of the country. The coal trade, though in the hands of private owners, is, in a stricter sense than most other industries, a national concern. It is the Use upon which the vast manufacturing in- terests of this country rest, and therefore the mil- lions of people who are dependent directly or indirectly upon these manufacturing interests are fairly entitled to put in a claim for consideration. If another crisis is threatened by another attempt to enforce a reduction of wages on a large scale the coal-owners will be under the necessity of clearing up a few points which are now hazy before they can claim justification in the eyes of the pub- lic. It is alleged, for instance, that the coal-owners as a body are net by any means so unanimous in moving for a reduction as they were last year. It is insinuated, too, that the present movement is engineered or promoted by a few colliery pro- prietors who have all along been particularly implacable in their desire to bring down miners' wages. One of the chief arguments used last year orl behalf of the coalowners was the "ruinously low rates" at which contracts had been taken, but it is asserted that that argument does not now apply to the same extent as contracts have lately been taken at higher figures. But supposing this not to be the case, it may reasonably be asked why the coalowners after the experiences of a few months ago, have under- taken in advance to sell coal at a price which will not permit a "living wage" to be paid. We know that a praiseworthy attempt was made some time ago to restrict the vicious system of "under- cutting." Has this attempt failed ? If it has failed it would be interesting to know who are considered to be most responsible for its failure It is well known that the miners' leaders have offered to co-operate with the colliery proprietors in put- ting an end to excessive undercutting. These overtures it would seem, have not been accepted, and if, as a result of their rejection, another general stoppage of industry is brought about, it is not at all improbabl that the system which places the mineral wealth of the country in prac- tically irresponsible hands will be very critically ; reviewed.</p>
        <p>MR. BALFOUR was the principal Nonconformist speaker at a meeting of the Noncon_ Unionists. formist Unionist Association in London last week, but it is question able whether the tone and matter of his address would be altogether agreeable to those who listened to him. Mr. Chamberlain and some other Liberal Unionists are disposed to maintain that they are still as good Radicals as over they were in all things but the Irish question. But Mr. BALFOUR treats any claim of this sort with scant respect. He takes it for granted that the Unionists have been finally incorporated with the Tory party. The union having once been formed he believes it is not deAined again to be dissolved. The Unionists having broken with their old party on one point are, in the esti- mation of men like Mr. BALFOUR, to be considered as committed to the whole gamut of Tory doc- trine. Mr. BALFOUR, in addressing them, does not even condescend to speak respectfully of the political work in which they were engaged prior to the differences on Home Rule. Liberal Union- ists are supposed to be proud of the great reforms which have been affected by the Liberal party in the course of the last half century, but Mr. I3ALroun choses this particular opportunity of all others to tell them that the improvements in the political and social conditions of the country are in no way due to what they were pleased to call progressive legislation. He, in fact, treated his Nonconformist hearers to a strong dose of undiluted Toryism, and the surprising thing is that his speech is freely punctuated with the "loud applause" which .signifies assent. Even Liberal Unionists are generally assumed to approve of the extension of the franchise as a great act of politi- cal justice which has been fraught with beneficent results. But no, says Mr. BALFOUR, that is a "naive . and rather simple-minded view" which was really blown to the winds so far back as the French revolution. What the people wanted was not franchises but leaders. This is really the old, old doctrine of Toryism that the "Common people" are entirely unfitted to govern themselves, and must trust their fortunes to the Salisburies and the Balfours of the day and be 1 thankful for the small mercies tliat are vouchsafed them. So far as Mr. BALFOUR himself is con- cerned there is,of course, nothing to cause surprise in all this, but it is remarkable that it should be addressed to those who parade their Nonconfor- mity and their " Liberal " Unionism without evok- ing a protest of even the mildest character.</p>
        <p>A criminal case that has been Cruelty in a Pub- lic stitution before the courts lately illus- In</p>
        <p>trates in a very painful fashion how easy it is even in these days for officials of public institutions to abuse the trust that .is reposed in them. A woman named Gillespie was placed on her trial before Mr. JUSTICE DAY for cruelly ill-treating the infants under her charge at the Hackney Union Schools at Brentwood, and as a result of the trial she was sentenced to five years penal servitude. And richly she deserved the punishment. She had originally.advertised herself as of "middle- age, single, early riser, and fond of children," yet during the last eight years at least her "fondness for children" had displayed itself in the most abominable cruelty to the little pauper children placed under her charge. She delighted in dip- ping the heads of the children in buckets of water, marching them about with only a shirt on and with baskets of clothes on their heads for an hour at a time, and striking them with broom-handles, frying-pans and bunches of keys. Her love of tor- ture was shown by keeping th 3 children so thirsty that when they could find an opportunity they would drink from the puddles in the schoolyard ; and, in another way, by sending the children out to gather nettles with which their own backs were stung. The most remarkable part ef the affair— which was strongly commented on by the judge —is to be found in the fact that all this was done in a public institution,and continued over a period of at least eight years. Even now the inhuman methods of this unnatural woman would not have been laid bare, had it not been that one of the children died under circumstances which seemed to clearly show that its end had been accelerated by cruel treatment. Justice DAY might well say that he "hoped that the revelations would tend to induce those in authority over public institu- tions to have some little care, conscientiousness, and diligence in the discharge of tkeir extremely important duties."</p>
      </div>
