      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>SATURDAY. JUNE 23. 1894.</head>
        <p>SATURDAY. JUNE 23. 1894.</p>
        <p>AT the present time the mining Miners, community of these Islands de- mands, in more ways than one, tion qte and sympathy. The dreadful disaster itll4°llth Wales illustrates in an appalling fashion ql6,l3erils to which the collier is exposed during Qf 'is working hours. More than three hundred , Workers who on Saturday descended the in Taff Vale, full of life and vigour have been will have been by the time the search parties IZPlete their work—brought back to their *a:" as mangled corpses. in their case death for' ket in its most sudden and its most hideous th 41' This is the special infliction under which ;Tilling community of Wales is now suffering, we turn to the North we find that the 44-'4l miners are face to face with a trouble of 41,()ther, and of even a more wide-spread charac- -1411., T'racticallv the whole of the miners of Scot- r;llave this week ceased work in order to resist ltion of wages. If this struggle be pro- aNfeet it will probably bring in its train as much, \VQ terhaPs more, suffering and misery than the q 1,48". calamity, Englishmen will watch this thate with keen interest, owing to the fact or may, according to its results, either hasten "laY another cessation of industry in Itry similar to that of last year.</p>
      </div>
