      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>SUCCESS OF WIDNES BANDS.</head>
        <p>SUCCESS OF WIDNES BANDS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday a brass band contest was held at St. Helens. The test piece was the selection " Elixir of Love," and Mr7tT. 0. Shepherd, of Liverpool, was the adjudicator. The Widnes Subscription Band succeeded in gaining the fourth prize, and Gossage's Soap Works Band, Widnes, were awarded the fifth prize.</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S BLACK BLENDED TEA, ald. per quarter lb.—Advt. OAKLAND-ST. WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE, WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The children in connection with this society had their annual treat on Saturday. They proceeded to a field off Lowerhouse-lane, preceded by the Widnes Subscrip- tion Prize Band. The usual games and other amuse- ments were entered into very heartily. A nice tea was provided after which games were again indulged in. The party reached home shortly after nine o'clock, thoroughly pleased with the day's outing. COLLIER'S sell everything for the smallest possible profit.— Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>CORRECTION.</head>
        <p>A CORRECTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas J. Holt writes as follows :—" In your report in last Saturday's " Examiner " of the meeting in connection with Brunswick Sunday School anniver- sary held in St. Paul's Chapel, you make it appear that my grandfather, the late William Holt, began his con- nection with the school early in the present century and died in 1886 at the w/o of sixty years. Now, what Mr'. Simpson said was that he died at the age of eighty years, which was his exact age at the time of his death, as a glance at his tombstone in Bethesda Chapel grave yard will show."</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S FRESH IRISH DAIRY BUTTER, 10d. per lb is excellent,—Advt.</p>
        <p>1/VIDNES IRISH NATIONAL LEAGUE AND THE WESTPORT DISASTER.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of the. T. P. O'Connor (Widnes) Branch of the Irish National League was bold in Vic- toria-road on Sunday afternoon, Councillor O'Donnell in the chair. the Chairman said it would be a fitting thing on the part of the branch to move and ask the Irishmen in the town to co-®perate with them in raising a fund to assist in relieving the distress caused by the Clew Bay . disaster in Mayo. A small committee was appointed to wait upon the clergy and obtain signatures to a requisition to the Mayor asking him to calla town's meeting to consider the matter.</p>
        <p>BUNCORN OCCASIONAL POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court on Tuesday morning, before Mr. F. J. Norman, Michael Mokre, a youth seven- teen years of age, who failed to appear on Monday at the Sessions, was sentenced to seven days' hard labour for making use of obscene and profane language in Lonslands-road on the 19th inst. It was stated in court that he had been convicted of various offences no fewer than 89 times.—George Friar and Henry Stephens were charged with sleeping out in a shed on the Ship Canal Works, Runcorn. After the hearing of the evidence both were discharged with a caution.</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S is. BUTTER is the gem in the market.—Advt. ACCIDENT TO AN OUTWARD BOUND VESSEL FROM</p>
        <p>RUNCORN. About half-past six on Tuesday evening, the schooner "Jessie" of Fleetwood, outward bound for Runcorn to load coal at Cardiff was endeavouring to crcss the bows of the Allan steamer " Laurentian," lying at anchor off the Woodside stage, when theitide carried her on to the bows of the steamer. The schooner lost her bowsprit and also sustained damage to her hull. The tug " Weathercock " took her in tow and beached her between Egreniont and New Brighton where an inspec- tion was subsequently made as to the damage done to her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>FIRE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At about nine o'clock on Wednesday night consider- able excitement was occasioned in the neighbourhood of Egerton-street by a fire which occurred on the premises occupied by Mr. Joseph Tickle. As this gentleman was lighting the gas in the window of the shop (No. 31) the flame caught some fancy grass which immediately ignited, the'flames spreading very rapidly owing to the highly inflammable nature of the articles in the window. With the assistance of other persons, Mr. Tickle suc- ceeded in extinguishing the fire but not until damage to the extend cf £33 had been done. The plate glass win- dow was broken which was valued at £l5; and the damage to the building amounted to £8 ; thus leaving £lO, which was estimated as the amount of damage to the fancy goods, &amp;c. This is only partially insured. Information of the fire had been sent to the fire brigade station, and the" buzzer" was blown. The hose cart, however, did not arrive on the scene until the fire had been put out, consequently thr services of the brigade , were not required. Inspector Hicks, Sergeant Woolley, and several constables were present and with much Idifficulty maintained order amongst the onlookers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MEETING OF ODDFELLOWS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>MEETING OF ODDFELLOWS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The members of the Loyal William IV. Lodge N 0.1085 Independent Order of Oddfellows held their half-yearly meeting in the lodge room, Devonshire Hotel, Runcorn, when Grand Master Bro. John Peck was presented with a very handsomely-framed past officer's emblem and certificate on the completion of his services (for the second time since he has been a member of the Lodge) of filling the respective offices of elective secretary, vice grand, noble grand, and grand master to the entire satisfaction of his brother members. Bro. Geo. Burrows, P.P.G.M., in presenting the emblem on behalf of the lodge, said it was not necessary for him to speak as to the willingness and attention which-Bro. Peck had given in promoting the best interests of that lodge. He was quite sure that every member present was heartily pleased to show in this slight manner the appreciation of services so heartily rendered to the lodge by Bro. Peck. Amongst other business transacted was the election of officers for the next six months, appointment of auditors consideration of laters respecting district nurse fund and Technical Institute fund, and the formation of a juvenile branch lodge. COLLIE'S BREAD is the best money can buy.—Advt. A RUNCORN BOAT OWNER IN TIIE SALE POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning, at the Sale Petty Sessions, Eliza i Martha Benson, of Runcorn, was summoned for using two canal boats named Martha and David,the cabins of , which were not in a habitable condition. Mr. Arthur I Linaker,solicitor, Runcorn, appeared for the defendant, the summonses .being taken out at the instance of the Sale Local Board. The case has been twice adjourned owing to the an-appearance of the defendant.—Mr. D. Llailiyrell, assistant inspector under the Canal Boats Acts, said on the Ist February he. inspected the David and Martha boats as they passed through Sale. There was no fault to be found except that the roofs of both of the cabins were rotting in one or two places. He pointed out those defects to the captain, who said that in web weather the rain came in on to the cabin floor and on to the beds. Several notices were sent to the defendant to put the boats in repair, but no notice had been taken of them. For the defence Mr. Linake.r said the boats had been at Runcorn tied up for many months previous to the Ist February. Trade had been bad, and his client had not tilt money to spare to put the boats in proper repair. An old customer of hers asked her at the end of January to take a load of macadam for him Ito the wharf near Sale. That she consented to do, and I that was all that the boats had been used for. Since that time they had been tied up altogether. The defendant had no intention of infringing the Canal Boats Act at all. The Chairman said the Bench accepted the expla,na- :ion offered by the defendant's solicitor,and would only fine her 10s. including costs in each case. SMART CAPTURE OF A LIVERPOOL TFIIEF AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court on Friday morning (last week) before Messrs. P. Speakman and F. J. Norman, a young nian named Henry Stephenson was charged with stealing a silver watch and guard valued £5 10s. the property of John Davies, of 19, Bridgewater-street, Runcorn.—From the evidence adducted it appears that on Thursday evening shortly after seven o'clock he went to the house mentioned and arranged for lodgings there for three weeks, saying that he was the mate on board the vessel " Genorer Homer" then lying at Weston • Point. He left the house at about eight o'clock and returned at ten. Alter a few minutes he pulled off his boots and said he would go to bed. lie went upstairs to his room but shortly afterwards went downstairs say- ing as he did so that he wanted some tobacco to have a smoke before going out in the morning. As he did not , return the landlady become suspic'ous and on going to his room she found that the watch and chain had gone. The articles belonged to a fellow lodger named Davies, 1 who had left them on the dressing table. She at once I communicated this fact to the police.—P.C. Gregory stated that he was on duty in Ashworth-street at six o'clock that morning when he saw a man, whose appear- ance was ideditical to the description given by the. land- lady, going up Runcorn Bridge. Witness followed him and during a conversation the latter told witness that he was going to Liverpool. The officer then asked him what he had in his pockets, and commenced to search him, whereupon he found two watch chains in a waistcoat pocket. and the stolen watch in his trousers pocket. Upon making enquiries he discovered that the two chains belonged to watches which the prisoner had stolen from Liverpool. He took him into custody and upon charging him at the Police Station, Davies replied "I took them. I don't know what possessed me to do so."—The magistrates sentenced the prisoner to two months' imprisonment with hard labour without giving him the option of paying a line.—At the conclusion of case the Chairman, Mr. Speakman, complimented P. C. Gregory upon his vi!!ilance and intimated that he would himself draw the attention of Superintendant Atherton to the smartness he had displayed in capturing the thief.— It is understood that a communication has been sent to the Liverpool Police explaining that the prisoner had had articles in his possession, which, it is supposed had been stolen from Liverpool, and for which they might , find owners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 3O, 1894.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BURIAL BOARD: ELECTION OF MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>On-Thursday evening, a meeting of ratepayers took place in the Bethesda schoolroom' Runcorn, under tlit chairmanship of the Rev. Canon Wood, vicar of Run- corn. The business consisted of the election of three ratepayers as members of the Board, in the places of Messrs. E. Imison, W. Whittaker, and W. Mellor, the retiring members, who were eligible for re-appointment. Finally, it was proposed, seconded, and carried, that the retiring gentlemen named be re-elected.</p>
        <p>SIR JOHN RIGBY AND THE RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that some time ago at a meet- ing of the committee, who have the work of building the institute in their hands, it was decided that Sir. John Rigby, the attorney-general, and an old Runcorn- lan, should be approached for a subscription towards:the fund. Mr. P. Speakman J.P.C.C. was entrusted with the mission and accordingly interviewed Sir John Rigby with the result that the latter has sent a cheque for £5O.</p>
        <p>A MAD DOG AT FEARNHEAD.</p>
        <p>A. case of rabies occurred at Fearnhead a few days ago. The dog which belonged to Mr. William Caldwell, and, which was 'kept fastened up in the yard, was noticed to be very savage, but it died before a gun could be procured to shoot it. The body was taken pos6ession of by the police, who have since had it examined by a veterinary surgeon, and he at once pronounced that the dog died from rabies. The Muzzling Order in this district will now be most stringently enforced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>RUNCORN CYCLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The mid-week riders of this club mustered in strong force in Devonshire Square at 2-15 on Wednesday after- noon and favoured with delightful weather started on an excursion to Lymm. The journey was completed in an hour and a half, the spin being extremely pleasant. The members shortly afterwards resorted to the Plough Inn where an enjoyable meal was prepared for them and to which they all did ample justice. The evening was spent in divers ways: dancing, singing, Szo. The return journey was begun just before eight o'clock, the members arriving home at about 9-15. WIFE DESERTION AT WIDNES. On Thursday, at the Widnes Petty Sessions, before Councillor W. W. Gossage and Alderman Timmis, a labourer named John Corrighan was brought up under a warrant charged with neglecting his wife and three children on the 24th of May. Mr. Woods, one of the relieving officers, said the prisoner took his family to Glasgow at Christmas 1892. Be deserted them there, and the wife and children were transferred to the Pres- ent Union. They again became chargeable on the 24th of May last, and the cost to the guardians had been £54. The prisoner was found working for his father, a farmer, in county Monaghan, Ireland, and was brought over from there on Wednesday.—Prisoner pleaded guilty, and was sent to gaol for one month with hard labour. RUNCORN WESLEYAN METHODIST CIRCUIT; QUARTERLY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MEETING.</head>
        <p>MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. Wilkes, superintending minister,presided on Monday evening over the quarterly meeting of this circuit, which was held in the Brunswick School. The remaining ministers present were the Revs. J. Hanby (Balton) and W. Arrowsmith (Halton-road), and also a number of other laymen from the circuit. The business transacted was of a purely formal character. The super- intendent presented his quarterly report, showing that the membership had slightly increased. The stewards confirmed the action of the Invitation Committee in in- viting the Rev. Lile Stone to take over the district of Halton in the place of the Rev.J.Hanby. On the pro- position of Mr. E. Taylor, seconded by Mr. Rawlinson, a vote of thanks was accorded to the retiring ministers. SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARIES AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The anniversary sermons of the St. Paul's Sunday School were preached on Sunday. The pulpit was occupied in the morning by the Rev. T. Oliver, D.D., vicar of Garston, and in the evening by the Rev. Canon Robson, A.K.C., vicar of Claughton. In the afternoon the Rev.W. Stanton Jones delivered a special address to the children. The collections taken in aid of the Sunday School amounted to £l9 12s.—The anniversary of the Lacey-st. Wesleyan Sunday School was also celebrated on Sunday. Special sermons were preached, morning and evening by the Rev. J. Bourne Jones, of Accring- ton, and in the afternoon a special address to teachers, scholars, and friends was given by Mr. Cadwallader Raby, of Manchester. Special hymns,&amp;c., were sung at each service by the choir and scholars. The collections realized over £75.</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S sell the best quality goods at lowest possible prices.—Advt. RAILWAY FATALITY NEAR ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The eight o'cl.pck express from Lime-st., Liverpool, to Manchester was on Thursday night proceeding at a speed of about fifty miles an hour between St. Helens Junction and C.Alin's Green, when George Clark, aged eighteen years, a daywageman at the Bold Colliery,was knocked down and killed. It appears that Clark lodged in Parr- street, Sutton, and was going on night work at the colliery, which is on the opposite side of the Manchester main line. How he came to be on the line is a matter of perplexity, as the footpath is separated from the railway by a high fence,and there is a footbridge near t 6) the junction of the colliery sidings. He was apparently struck on the back of the head and hurled a considerable distance. As soon as possiole a stretcher was procured, on which he was being removed to his lodgings, when he died on the way. A COSTLY '6 LITTLE SEINE " AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>• At the Runcorn Police Court on Thursday morning before Messrs. G. L. Davies (in the chair), and F. J. Norman, an elderly man named John Howard, was charged in custody with being quarrelsome and disor- derly on the licensed premises of the Fleetwood Arms, Church-street. and refusing to quit when requested by Mr. Alfred Chaplin, the landlord. He was further charged with breaking a plate glass panel in one of the front doors of that house, valued 18s.—Mr. Allred Chaplin asserted that the prisorser came into his house on Wednesday night about half-past eight. Some time afterwards he became abusive, using very obscene lan- guage. Witness asked him to go out but prisoner refused, whereupon he was compelled to eject him. Having done so prisoner rushed at witness as he was again entering the house, and in attempting to hit him broke the glass panel in the door,—Questioned by the Chairman, witness said the man was not drunk and was served with a pint of beer.—P.S. Lee stated that when the prisoner was brought to the station the prev- ious evening he could scarcely stand. He was quite drunk.—Fined ss. and costs 7s. 4d. for the first offence, and for the second ss. and 235. costs, making a total of 40s. 4d. In default of payment he was sentenced to twenty-one days hard labour. AWARD TO A SCHOOLMASTER.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire County CORUCiI have awarded Mr. Cawthorne, headmaster of the Croft Board School, an exhibition of £lO on the result of the recent competitive examination in chemistry. CO-OPERATIVE BAKERY AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The bakers of St. Helens have a movement on foot for the formation of a union bakery for that town on the co-operative or limited company principle. HUSSARS BILLETED AT WA.RRI NG TO N.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon, a detachment of the 14th Hussars, to the number of 109 officers and men, arrived in Warrington, from Manchester, and were billeted in the town during the night. They left on Wednesday morning for Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>W.AILRINGTON BANDS AT ST. lIELENS.</head>
        <p>W.AILRINGTON BANDS AT ST. lIELENS.</p>
        <p>The third annual brass baud contest arranged by !kir. Thomas Boardman, of the George Hotel, St. Helens, was held on the ground of the St. Helens Football Club, on Saturday. The following bands from Warrington were amongst the. unsuccessful competitors :—Warrington Borough (Mr. R. Rim- tner), Warrington League of the Cross (An J. Mul- lins). The test piece was IL Round's " Elixir of Love," and the prizes were awarded by Mr. Shep- herd, of the Royal Court Theatre, Liverpool, who officiated as judge, as follows:—!, Pemberton Old; 2, Skelrnersdale Temperance; 3, Ashton Temper- ance; 4, Widnes Subscription; 5, Widnes Gossage's. THE CROFT BOWLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>About 40 members of the Croft Bowling Club, held at the General Elliott Hotel. played their opening game for the season on Saturday on the green attached to the hotel, and competed for prizes given by the club. The members were handicapped by the committee, the limit given being four, and the game thirteen up. The green was in good con- dition, and after a sharp contest the following were the prize-winners: —l, copper kettle, H. Hankinson ; 2, metal teapot, P. Unsworth ; 3, half-dozen knives and forks, R. Sanderson. The members sat down to a good knife and fork tea at the hotel at six o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>TRIP TO SOUTHPORT.</head>
        <p>TRIP TO SOUTHPORT.</p>
        <p>The choir and their friends of the Risley Presby- terian Church Culcheth, had their annual trip on Saturday, the place selected this year being South- port. The party, numbering 36, went by the seven a.m. train from Padgate to Southport in special carriages, and arrived there at 8.35 a.m. They then visited the principal places of interest until noon, when they partook of dinner. Afterwards they went to the Botanical Gardens, Churchtown, and witnessed the crowning of the May Queen, after which they returned to Southport for tea. They left by the seven p.m. train, and arrived safely at Padgate Station about 9.30 p.m., having spent an enjoyable clay. Mr. D. L. Dunn carried out the arrangements satisfactorily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FUNERAL AT WHITLEY CHURCH.</head>
        <p>FUNERAL AT WHITLEY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Miss Smith of Rose Bank Whitley Superior and Bryngwyn, Llandudno, took place at Whitley Church on Tuesday, the ser- vice being 'performed by the Rey. J. J. Bryant, vicar of Whitley. The principal mourners were Miss Kerr, Miss Julia Kerr, Masters William and Robert Kerr, Mr. and Mrs. J. B. Brooks, Mr. and Miss Mason, and Miss Ella Smith. The wreaths were beautiful and numerous and the fallowing is a selection from the names upon the accompanying cards—The Misses Kerr, the • Masters Kerr, Miss Ella Smith, Mr. and Mrs. Brooks, Miss Edith Holmes, Miss Steelthe Misses Wynne, Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Tunstall, Mr. and Mrs. J.L. Tunstall, Annie (nurse at Bryngwyn). Emma (cook at Bryngwyn), Henry (coachman at Bryngwyn). The funeral arrangements were most efficiently carried out by Messrs. J. and W. Dutton of Sankey,itreet, War- rington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BELL-RINGING AT GRAPPE.NIIALL.</head>
        <p>BELL-RINGING AT GRAPPE.NIIALL.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, the ringers rang a peal in the Grappenhall Church tower of 720 Plain Bob Minors in 25 minutes, 6th being the Observation Bell. This peal has the fewest calls in having only 8 Bobs and 6 Singles. The following is the order in which they stood.-1A James Mainwaring,, 2nd Frederick T. Spence, 3rd Thomas Burrows, 4th A. Oxley, sth T. Brammer, 6th E. Edwardson, coneuctor. Weight of tenor 10c. 2q.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>ANNIVERSARY SERMONS AT CROFT.</head>
        <p>ANNIVERSARY SERMONS AT CROFT.</p>
        <p>The anniversary sermons in connection with Christ Church, were preached on Sunday. Three services were held, and there were large congrega- tions. The Rev. R. Tebbs, vicar of St. Thomas', Wigan. preached in the morning, the Rector in the afternoon, and the Rev. Canon Penrhyn in the even- ing. The choir rendered the anthem" 0 taste and see "in the morning, " God be merciful " in the afternoon, and " 0 Worship the Lord "in the even- ing, alsol,the Magnificat and Nunc Dimittis from C. Simper's service in F. Mr. Cawthorne was at the organ. The collections amounted to £l3 19s.</p>
        <p>40- A NEW MAGISTRATE FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Duke of Westminster has approved of Mr. Thomas Handley as a new magistrate for the Run- corn Division and he will on Monday next attend at the Chester Quarter Sessions for the purpose of being invested with magisterial powers. Mr. Thos. Handley is a gentleman so well known both in Run- corn and Widnes that any information immediately concerning him is superfluous to a native of either of these towns. It may, however, be stated for the benefit of others, that Mr. Handley is the youngest son of the late Mr. Thomas Handley. and resided at Fern Cliffe, Higher Runcorn. He is 47 years of age, and at present is the head of the firm of Messrs. Handley and Beck, ironmongers of Runcorn and Widnes. For more than twenty-five years he has been actively engaged in public work in Runcorn and district during which time he has held many responsible positions. In conjunction with his brother, Mr. James Handley, J.P., he owned and carried on the Weston quarries until the formation of the Runcorn and Helsby Red Sandstone Company, which took over these quarries, and of which company Mr. Thomas Handley and his brother are directors. He is at present the chairman of the Runcorn Burial Board, a member of the Runcorn Board of Guardians, and Runcorn School Board, a director of the Runcorn Gas Works, and for nine years was an active member of the Widnes Local Board. He has proved him- self a man of integrity, possesses an amiable though retiring disposition, and is eminently fitted to the posi- tion of magistrate owing to his sound practical judg- ment. Mr. Handley is by persuasion a Congregational- ist and is a Liberal by politics.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESrONDENT,</p>
        <p>The recent gathering of our local footballers at the Wilson's Hotel has been acknowledged, by all who participated in the proceedings, an immense success. The objectof the prime movers in the event was to do honour, in right royal fashion, to the members of both teams, but more particularly to the second team, who, it will be remembered, was not once defeated during the past season. They were, moreover, successful in winning the first place in the Liverpool and District Junior Rugby League Competition, and the gold medals which they thus secured were presented to them by their worthy president, Mr. Philip Speakman, J.P. C.C. The result of the gathering mast certainly have de- lighted the originators equally as much as it did the players, and the latter should feel somwhat stimulated when they remember the many expres- sions of lively satisfaction and admiration which were made on this occasion concerning their past performances.</p>
        <p>The prospect of the club so far from being gloomy as some folks state, is .very bright. They are sur- rounded by willing friends who will readily res- pond as far as their means will allow them, to any appeal that might be made to them on behalf of the club. Then they have in Mr. Philip Speakman a president who has given them valuable assistance, and who is still very anxious for their welfare. He has repeatedly expressed his intentions of support- ing them through all their difficulties, and will not allow them to be handicapped owing to their low financial condition. Lastly, they have such a com- bination of skilful players that they should not ex- perience much diffiL:ulty in attracting big "gates ' next season. It is to be hoped that the players will be fired with more zeal in the future, and that they will succeed in not only retaining their pre- sent exalted position in the football world, Ilia will climb still higher.</p>
        <p>The Presiaterians of Runcorn are not wasting any of the precious time in their endeavour to raise sufficient funds to conclude the building of their proposed new church in . Victoria-road. Already they are busily:engaged in making arrange- ments for the holding of a bazaar on a large scale in the new chapel schools which have only recently been opened. The date has not definitely been fixed but it is expected to take place some time in October. The various committees have been formed and the members of these are putting forth prodig- ious efforts in order to make the event a decided success.</p>
        <p>The magisterial bench of Runcorn will very shortly have a new and thoroughly worthy occupant in the person of Mr. Thomas Handley. head of the well-known firm of iron merchants, Messrs. Handley and Beck of Runcorn and Widnes. For fully twenty-five years Mr. Handley has been closely associated with the governing bodies both of Runcorn and Widnes. In the latter town he occupied a seat on the Local Board for nine years during which time he was known as an active and useful public man, jealously guarding the interests of the community which he represented. In Run- corn, where he is possibly better known, he holds various positions of importance. He is chairman of the Burial Board, a member of the Board of Guardians, and of the School Board. He is a gentleman, who in every respect, is likely to fulfil the office with dignity and justice; and he has been the recipient of many congratulations on his having acquired this additional post of honour.</p>
        <p>The ratepayers of Runcorn must be very satisfied with the gentlemen who represent them at present on the Burial Board, judgiag from the small num- ber present at the meeting on Thursday evenitig when three persons wore ta he elected in the places of Messrs. W. 5 hittaker, B. Imison and W. Mellor, retiring members. Canon Wool presided over the assembly which numbered about four persons, and these were unanimously of the opinion that the gentlemen who were eligible for re-appointment, should serve for the ensuing year. They were accordingly re-elected. It is a gratiication to know that the ratepayers are 99 content with the objects of their choice, and that these gentlemen discharge their duties so much to their satisfaction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WEST LANCASHIRE COAL DISPUTES.</head>
        <p>WEST LANCASHIRE COAL DISPUTES.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. PEARSON AND KNOWLES AND THEIR EMPLOYES.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, a deputation consisting of Mr. T. Aspinwall, Mr. T. Glover, and Mr. Isherwoed, and a number of workmen waited upon the management of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles Colliery, Lower Ince, re- garding the disputes which led to notices being sent in. About 2,000 employees were affected and they stopped work on Wednesday. There were a number of griev- ances, the principal being that the men wanted the " scorage " (an allowance on each score of boxes of coal) added to the field price. The questions in dispute were fully gone into, and an arrangement male. The men afterwards decided to resume work for a fartnight, and if in the meantime they have any complaint the agent will take the matter in band. Following upon this set- tlement comes the question in dispute at the Ashton 's Green Colliery, St. Helens. W understana the matter will come before the Lancashire and Cheshire Miners' Federation to-day, and probably notices will be sent in next week.</p>
        <p>WATER POLO AT ST. HELENS. In consequence of 800z1,4 having withdaawn from the Liverpool and District Water Polo League, St. llelens arranged a friendly match with Liverpool Gymnasiman. On Tuesday the teams met,and after a pretty even gany?. St.Helens came out victorious by 6 goals to I-. The senior's monthly handicap was won by Peter Ripley, the winner of the last hand:cap, while Willie Slater won the juniors'. Both handicaps were very exciting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <p>THE POST OFFICE CONTROVERSY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>DEPUTATION FROM NEWTON.</head>
        <p>DEPUTATION FROM NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Following on the heels cf the deputation from Earlestown, consisting of representatives of the Commissioners, and a number of ratepayer, which waited up Mr. Rich, Postulaster, Liverpool, in reference to the postal arrangements at Earlestown, a deputation from the ratepayers Association of Newton waited upon the same gentleman on Friday, to explain matters, as they had reference to Newton. The deputa- tion comprised Messrs. F. J. Wood (chairman), R. Stuart, G. Clarke, J. Adams, R. Barton and A. Syme. On arrival at the General Pcst Office, Liverpool, these gentlemen were at once admitted to the presence of Mr. Rich who received them very courteously. Mr. Stuart acted as spokesman for the deputation,and said they represented the ratepayers of Newton, and from the local press they noticed that the ratepayers of Earlestown were bringing before him the question of postal facilities, and that deputation wished to put their views before him. _</p>
        <p>• Mr. Rich said before going any further he would like ' to tell them that there was no bias in the post office against Earlestown in favour of Newton, or against Newton in favour of Earlestown. What the post office wanted was ample facilities for both places. In fact, they wanted a collective and distributive centre in one place, it could not be in both places, and one place must have the central office. There were two points to con- sider—the office at Earlestown was extremely defective. and at Newton it twas extremely defective, and there was no doubt whatever that considerably more accom- modation was necessary for Newton, and he thought for Earlestown also. They ought to have a proper sorting room for postmen and others, and accommodation for about 20 officials, there ought to be a retiring room for the nvn, and accommodation of other kinds. The office at Newton certainly answered the requirements so far, and they had not got the accommodation at Earles- town. If they had a sufficiently large office at Newton of the kind which he explained was going to be erected at Widnes, he did not think they should want a similar office at Earlestown. Now the first thing to do was to provide a.suitable office. So far as the post office was concerned, it did not much matter to them if the head office was at Newton, they would certainly have a proper office at Earlestown, but not a head office, yet an office which would have amply accommodation. At Earlestown they would now see postmen sorting letters in the shop, which they, as business men, would say was not a right and proper thing. Letters were con- sidered a sacred trust and as such they should have a proper place in which to deal with them. Mr. Stuart after thanking Mr. Rich for that explana- tion said they wanted his consideration. He (Mr. Rich) was now dealing with the question from a departmental pint of view, and he (Mr. Stuart) was assuming that the offices at Runcorn and Widnes (which Mr. Rich had referred to) had been provided by the ratepayers. He wanted to point out f)r his consideration that Earles- town and Newton were contiguous places, managed by a Board of Commissioners, elected by the inhabitants. Up to now that Board had been elected by the people of Earlestown, as that end having the largest part of the population they had been able to select the greatest number of Commissioners. In November, by a similar agitation to this it was arranged that the members should be elected in wards,and not all together,and thus the representation would be more equally divided. If 1 Earlestown got what they wanted now, it would be manifestly unfair to nwton,as Newton would probably have to pay as much out of the rates as Earlestown. Mr. Rich said that as car as he knew the ratepayers , would not have to find either one place or the other. , The post master has to fine. the post office, and he goes to a man and says" will you fit it up ?" At Earlestown or Newton there will not be a Crown 'office, which is a place provided by the Crown, as the townships are not , sufficiently large. Mr. Stuart said they wanted the whole of this ques- tion deferring until November when the new Board would deal with it, and they expected to get a fair pro- portion of representation all round, and it might be beam. for EArlestawn, and certainly for the department If the ratepayers spent their money on a central office it would be very much better. Mr. Rich said they did not want a very expensive offiee, say one about £lOO a year would be quite suffi- cient. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Stuart said that from the figures they had been getting out, to have a head office at Ear:estown would necessitate a great amount of extra work on the officials. He then gave some statistics, showinz the distances of the various suh-offices from Newton and Earlestown, the sum total being the extra distance to be traversed if the head office was removed to Earlestown, would be over six miles, _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Rieh observed that while he attached importance to those points, he did not think they were of the very first importance. In all probability he should send rural postmen over that ground whether the office remained at Newton or was removed to Earlestown, and it was probable they should do it on bicycles, as it would save labour to the men, and accelerate th 3 delivery, but they inust not attach too much importance to that statement. Assuming they had Earlestown as a distributing centre, how would that affect them? They would still have a proper office at Newton, with telegraphic facilities, and practically from a postal point of view they would be as well off as at present, if not batter. His opinion was that the Newton people would be very sorry to see the old postal business transferred to Earlestown. As regarded the postal service, whether the collection or delivery of letters or parcels, it must be understood that the accom- modation must be better. There could be no alteration that would place Earlestown in a worse position than at present. Mr. Wood : That is over the counter. Mr. Rich : In every way. Mr. Stuart said there was an article in a newspaper a few weeks since headed, "Wanted, a post-mark." The Newtom pemole were somewhat in fear and trembling that if the head office went to Earlestown they should lose their post-mark of Newton-le-Willows. Mr. Rich said he had seen the article and the facts were hardly quite as they were put out. The post office at Earlestown, where letters were posted, had, marked upon the back, the post-mark of Earlestown, but they had never yet had the usual post-mark on the face, and then it was only the letters which were posted at the Earlestown office. These that were posted in the wall boxes were taken to Newton, the distributing and des- patching centre ; and seeing that they were extending the postal facilities, the public would be expected to post their letters and subqoit to their being stamped Newton-le-Willows. There was no objection to their being stamped "Earlestown" that he could see. Mr. Stuart said they did not want to sink Earle3town, but if Newton understood they were not going to lose their post-mark entirely, they were all right on that head, they would, however, prefer that the considera- tion be left over until after November. What he wanted to point out was that supposing now somebody at Earlestown entered into competition with Newton for providing suitable building, which was he going to take?</p>
        <p>Mr. Rich : Obviously an answer to that question. could not come from me. It is not for me to give an answer on that point, as it is an important one. s Mr. Stuart said it would be muck) better to decide the question, if all the people decided to unite, instead of, as they are at present disunited, to provide a proper postal station. Mr.. Rich said if he decided to defer the question until November, the understanding must be that the people at Eirlestown and Newton would not press unduly for extension of the facilities. He had received a deputa- tion from Earlestown and hoard what they had to say, but he had not committed the department in any way, and so far as he could see,he did not think their demand was unreasonable.</p>
        <p>Mr. Adams said the doputation which waited upon Mr. Rich did not represent Newton at all, as the people of Newton had not a voice in putting them on the Board. _ _</p>
        <p>--Mr. Rich said as far as he understood it, it was sup- posed that therewould be a change in the representation on the Board in November. The deputation : Yes. Mr. Robinson: But you are represented on the Board at present. The deputation No. Mr. Stuart explained it was the custom, that when an occasional vacancy occurred on the Board, for the Com- missioners to elect some gentleman to fill it. At the present time there was a gentleman belonging to Newton on the Board named Mr. Smith, who was elected by the Commissioners and not by the votes of the people of Newton. With the ballot in November the votes would be about equally divided, and what they said was that they could not induce the Commissioners to take this question up, and build a suitable building instead of tinkering at it in this manner. Mr. Rich : What you wish is that whatever arrange- ment is made, should be satisfactory to the people of Earlestown as well as to the people of Newton. The deputation: Certainly. Mr. Wood asked if the requirements • of the Newton post office were much below what the authorities at% the present time required. Mr. Rich : Very much below. Mr. Clarke here produced a map showing the distance of the outlying villages or sub-districts from Newton and Earlestown respectively. Mr. Rich after look;ng at the map said if Earlestown had any proposition to make, and no doubt they had, he should be very glad to consider it,and submit it to head- quarters, and he would say the same to Newton. There was no doubt Eirlestown was advancing, and Newton was not advancing to the same extent, arid if they were going to le.7,islate upon the district then they would leg- islitte for the place where the largest population was. Mr. Adams and Mr. Clarke pointed out that in the case of new property which was being erected, the direction of the building was all towards Newton, and land which had remained unoccupied for many years was now being applied for. . _ _ . _ _ -</p>
        <p>Mr. Stuartart said if there was any proposition for build- ing a post office, it should be bugt in a central position between the two places. After some further conversation. Mr. Rich said if Newton was made into a sub-office tiley would not feel it, or even know it. The only thing would be that</p>
        <p>Newton would not be the head office, and the natural consequence was that the head office must be where the distribution of the lctters took place. Whatever arrange- ment was made, no man or firm would be placed in a worse position. What was wanted was to simplify the work, and make the arrangements more complete. If the people of E frlestown were in a position to offer a suitable building for postal business, it would be for Mr. Morley to say whether it would be convenient for all business. He had no doubt a large amount of sentiment entered into the question, but he had heard all that the people of Ea.rlestown had said, and he did not hear a single word antagonistic to Newton. What he gathered was that Earlestown intended to take care of itself. The plan was left in the nossession of Mr. Rich, who thanked Mr, Clark for the trouble he had taken, and for the excellant plan submitted. ... .. . . _</p>
        <p>After thanking Mr. Rich for the courteous way he had received thew, the deputation withdrew.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WESTON.</head>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF A SULOR FROM TYPHOID FEVEII.—JOIIII Sims, aged about 37 years, of Falmouth, and engaged as mate on board the schooner " Lizzie," at present lying in the Delamer° basin, Weston Point, was found to be suffering from typhoid fever when he arrived with his vessel. He was attended by Dr. McKenzie who ordered his removal to the Runcorn Infectious Diseases Hospital. The order was.complied with but notwithstanding all the skill an attention bestowed upon him by his attend- ants he sank and died on Tuesday evening at about half- past six o'clock. Information of his illness had prev- iously been sent to his wife at Falmouth, and she hurried to Runcorn reaching the hospital a few hours after his death. She was much shocked at hearing the painful news of her husband's somewhat sudden death. On Thursday afternoon the deceased was buried in the newly constructed " Sailor's Grave " in the Runcorn Cemetery which makes the first interment therein. The coffin containing the remains of the deceased seaman was followed by a short procession of seamen, some of whom were captains. Mr. Shaw, the local missionary, was present among the number. After the burial ser- vice, which was performed by the Rev. C. T. Horan, chaplain of the Mersey Mission, Liverpool, the party returned to the Seamens' Institut,e where tea had been provided for their convenience.</p>
        <p>WIDNES PETTY SESSIONS. TIIIIRSDAY.—Before Councillor W. W. Gossage, and</p>
        <p>Alderman T. S. Timmis.</p>
        <p>DOGS.—Michael Burnes, Victoria-street; NVil:iam Rox- burgh, Hough Green ; John Edwardson, Lacy-street ; Lloyd Jones, Halton View-road ; William Atherton, Oakland-street ; and Joseph Wood, Wellington-street, were each lined for allowing their dogs to be at large insecurely muzzled. CRUELTY TO ANIMALS.—H. Bartley, and Peter Dennett were charged with working a horse in an unfit Henshaw St. Helens, was also charged with working a horse in an unfit sate and J.Matthews was charged with causing the same to be worked. Also James Bold, and John Lovelady, Irwell-st., were charged with working a horse in an unfit state. Each of the defendants were fined.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>COAL TRADE. MINERS' MEETING AT ST. HELENS. THE WAGES QUESTION AND THE DEADLOCK.</head>
        <p>THE COAL TRADE. MINERS' MEETING AT ST. HELENS. THE WAGES QUESTION AND THE DEADLOCK.</p>
        <p>A meeting of St. Helens miners was held on Sat- urday to hear an address from Mr. Glover, J.P., the St. Helens agent, on the present position of the Scotch miners,the wage question, and the condition of trade generally.—Mr. P. M‘Kiuley was voted to the chair.</p>
        <p>Mr. Glover then detailed the event leading up to the decision of the Scotch miners to come out in resistance of a reduction of wages. He said that now 31,030 Scotch miners were federated with the Miners Federation of Great Britain. That had come about since the late strike. In Scotland they had amalgamated and formed a federation on the principle of the Lancashire and Cheshire Miners' Federation,4nd then they became amal- gamated to.what they knew was the most powerful and successful organisation that had ever been tried in Great Britain—viz., the National Federation of Miners (ap- plause.) Mr. Glover continued to say that if the Eng- lish miners allowed the Scotch miners to suffer 25 per cent. reduction English coalowners would say they also were entitled to a reduction in wages. English miners must therefore, for their own protection,support Scotch- men. The English employers were evidently wanting a meeting very badly for the purpose of considering a change in wages, and it had been broadly hinted by some employers that they were entitled to 20 per cent. reduction. With regard to the to the state of trade, Mr. (Auver ssid no doubt the coal trade had been passing throegh some depression ,but he did not admit that the trade was very much depressed at the present time. If the whole of the coal-producing parts of the country could at the present time work over , four days a week, he did not call it bad. He knew that some collieries, in some parts of the kingdom, were only working two days a week, and in some places perhaps only three days a week ; but alongside with that there were collieries working six days a week. He quoted statistics from the "Labour Gazette," showing that at 994- collieries, where 256,409 men were employed, the average time worked during May was 4.71 days per week ; while in April the average was 4.61,and in March 469. Fifty per cent. of the miners employed made twenty-two days per month. In the face of increasing working days, and in the face of the masters having secured an advance of 6d. to 91 per ton on , some of the large contracts, they came and asked for "a change of wages." The state of other trades was not good generally ; but still, at the present time, the coal trade was not so very had; and so far as St. Helens was concerned, the miners had worked as well this summer, or even better, than last summer or the summer before. In conclusion, Mr. Glover read and put the following resolution :—" That this meeting of St. Helens miners, after hearing the agent's report of the Scotch miners' dispute, and also the request of the employers forming part of the Board of Conciliation for a change in wages, consider that the men's representatives have done right in not meeting the employers on the board until they comply with rule 9 also that this meeting pledges itself to support the Scotch miners by paying whatever levy may be called by the Federation of Great Britain."</p>
        <p>The resolution was carried unanimously. In reply to questions, Mr. Glover said it was not true that Mr. Ashton, secretary of the Miners' Federation,met the masters in London last Tuesday. As regarded the Scotch strike the English miners could not strike to help them during the term of the Conciliation Board, because the Scotch were not federated with England when the board was formed. He would not predict what they might do after November.</p>
        <p>THE SCOTCH MINERS' STRIKE.-ONE HUNDRED</p>
        <p>THOUSAND MEN IDLE.</p>
        <p>The strike of Scotch miners began on Tuesday, and that no fewer than 65,000 men are idle. The stoppage is the greatest in the history of the trade in Scotland, and it is calculated that with other trades affected 100,000 men are out of work. The coal masters regard the general stoppage as favourable to an early collapse of the strike, as they argue that had some of the men continued to work the struggle would have been longer drawn out. The nurnly,r of blast furnaces to be extin- guished is increasioa, although foreign coal agents offer to deliver coal at Glasgow at the same prices as those obtaining in the city market. Large contracts are being made with Tyne firms for coal. On Change on Tuesday tho feeling was one of surprise at the general character of the strike, and merchants seemed to realise that pros- pects are serious, failing an early change in the position of affairs. Another correspondent telegraphs :—Cornplete effect has been given in the Lothians to the strike resolution, the only colliery in the two counties in which work pro- ceeded on Tuesday being the House of Muir Colliery, near Ormiston. The supply of coal is limited, there being practically no stock on hand. The greater part of the colliery owners in the Lothians are outside the Masters' Association, but a thorou.h understanding exists. •A w.neral rise has been made in prices. In Fifeslaire 9,000 men are idle, The stoppage of work is complete, with the exception of two small collieries which can have no effect upon the situation and at which no reduction was enforced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ALARMING ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>ALARMING ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>About eleven o'clock on Saturday morning an alarm- ing accident happened in the Old Market Place, St. Helens. Mrs. Fletcher, of 23, Corporation-street, was shopping and left a perambulator with two young chil- dren outside the chemist's shop of Mr. R. Smith. The perambulator appears to have run off the pavement and in front of a horse attached to a vehicle belonging to Colonel Gamble, C. B. The horse reared, and the per- ambulator was knocked down .unner the horse's feet. The children were, happily, rescued, but the perambula- tor was smashed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ALLEGED NIGHT POACHING.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED NIGHT POACHING.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens on Monday, before Alderman It. Pilking- ton,a collier named John Littler, of Billinge,was brought up in Custody charged with night poaching on land be- longing to Colonel Gamble, C. IL. early on Sunday morn- ing. Mr. J. 0. Swift appeared to prosecute,and Mr, H. L. Riley defended. Mr. Riley asked for bail for the pri- soner. There was urgent special reason why he should have bail. On the side of his head there was an ex- tremely ugly wound, which was still bleeding,a,nd there were marks of wounds on his legs which he alleged he received on that night. At all events, he was in urgent 'need of surgical treatment, and he (Mr. Riley) had two respectatile men pre3ent who would answer for bail. Mr. Swift offered no obi?ction.—Prisoner was bound over in £lO and two sureties in .£5 each, John Grundy and Thomas Naylor being sureties, and the case was re- manded until Friday.</p>
      </div>
