      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE, - Very large fancy rustic straws are the latest models. Flowers of the season—pink and v ()lot stocks, roses (she fall cabbage rose particularty)— are seer everywhere, with ivy and nt-iy. Featnere are placed in the crkzi attitudes that recall the days of the Empire. They nod anyhow, and wilt look sublimely absurd in windy weather. The bonnets are perfeot ; real, close-fitting " baby " shapes of rose pink or turquo:se straw, trimmed with black tulle, jetted tips and ornaments, are very pretty. Scene ire enlrely made of creamy guipure, drawn ever 'gold wire and trimmed st the pales 4 velvet forget-rne•note and wi:d potpies, or, composed of branches of litlics of tits valley and turquoise velvet. Rosettes cf shaded chifiOn, mAkie, or ribbon arc much used for millinery awl shot effects, produced in Lows of d ff.rent cc/oared tulles mixed together. The Spe.niwlt bo!ern hats ire worn with a tiny border of spacg'es, a t-tand ot rtbhon round the crown, and the thteo full pompons and aigrette on the left side, with a twist coloured silk tied on the hair. A stylish hat is sailor shape of a obampagne-colraurt,”l trimmed with a band of riboon comfo,tably' I:ned with padded satin,</p>
        <p>Now is the period when the white piqu6 prise flourishes once again, and, by the way, we have borrowed this fashion from our babies. Piqt:6 is obtaining amongst us, but be it understood it is generally pique glorified with black satin ribbons and chiffon vests. It is a material that pleases me but little; it is too stiff, yet I think it may be need most successfully to form facings to drill gowns, and .it may be relied upon to make very smart little coats to be worn over serge skirts for yachting; these looking their bPst, perhaps, when adorned with gold buttons. With serge skirts, too, coats of coloured linen have an excelleet effect. A cornflower-blue serge skirt and a holland coat, although it perhaps suggests the undress costume of a page, may be very successfully worn with a black shirt and a black tie, and crowned by a Panama hat trimmed with black taffeta ribbon. An orthodox yachting gown suitable for eithet shore or sailing wear is arranged in •fine Isle ol Wight serge of dark blue, this being used for the short naval jacket and plain, skirt. A waistcoat of plain red cioth, close fitting in shape, and well moulded to the figure, opens to show a Capriott vest of white cloth, barred with lines of red and blue. The deep sailor caps is also of white cloth, edged with red and blue. at of regulation shape in burnt Panama straw, trimmed with red and blue,</p>
        <p>Cotton bate and bonnets trimmed with white embroidery may be bought for children of all ages, and the little ones also look well in boat-shaped Panama.' trimmed with a white ribbon tied at the side, and feather pompons may be called upon to do their decorative duty on hats of this kind. How easy it is to buy a smart hat for a small girl, but how desperately difficult to suit a little boy. The stiff, ban', manly sailor hat is perhaps the best for any little boy over four, but before he has arrived at this wealth of years the hat question is really a very troublesome one, perhaps best answered by the Jack Tar hat, which turns straight back from the face, and either bears unblushingiy the name of the ship to which its wearer does not belong, or is more simply trimmed with a black ribbon. For our infant girls the close cap lined with si!k, made of innumerable rows of lace and soft frills round the face, is almost universally voted the most becoming, as it is unquestionably'</p>
        <p>the most comfortable bonnet, This reminds one of a little girl's pretty frock I have just seen. It Is in blue black crepon, of full bodice, with col!ar, yoke, and wristband of embroidery. The sleeves are also trimmed with embroidery. The hat in white fancy chip has bows of cream ribbon and white heather. Yours lovingly.</p>
