      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>EXAMINER. SAT URI'A Y. JUNE 30, 1894 THE DOUBLE LIFE;</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SAT URI'A Y. JUNE 30, 1894 THE DOUBLE LIFE;</p>
        <p>The " Pall Mall Gizette " says the following letter was received by one of the examiners at a recent examina- tion for a medical diploma, al(' was written by a feminine sta lent of medicine :—" Sir,—Don't you dare refuse me again in physiology when you know I know all about physiology; [ very likely know more than you do. I shall write to Mr.— if you do about it. Very soon doctors will be drawn only from we pure noble. minded women, • and you vile drunken filthy men expelled for ever.—(eigned)—." Mr Ellis, whose interesting and exhaustive work on Secondary Sex Characteristics has just been published, says that man is more easily attracted to a woman with abundant hair than to one whose locks are scanty while woman is more easily won by a big, manly voice than by a weak or high-pitched one. As to brain, h.; does not admit superiority or inferiority,but merely diff- erence. Woman's brain more nearly resembles that of the child; man's more nearly approaches that of the ape. OPOod Housekeeping. It is the heart of keeping a house clean, not of making it clean, that characterises good housekeeping. It is a disentanglement of the threads of work that wears out time and patience, and breaks down the body and nerve. The average woman, who has not learned to manage her work, wastes about one half of her strength in useless energy, in unmethodical ways, taking ten steps where she need but take one. When work is once reduced to system, and each follows properly the other, when the housekeeper has learned the true economy of time and strength, we shall hear little of broken down hou le- keepars unable to withstand the weight of the daily toil which comes to their lot.</p>
        <p>How to cure damp walls. Sylvester's treatment for damp walls, if effectual, Is, says" Work," simple enough. Two separate washes are made; one composed of Castile soap and water—Dh. to the gallon—and the other composed of 'alum and water-2,z. to the gallon. The walls to be treated must be perfectly dry before applying the washes, and the air temperature at the time should not be higher than 50deg. F. The soap wash is laid on first, boiling hot, with a large fiat brush, and rubbed in so as to produce a lather. Twenty-four hours after, the alum wash is applied at a tetnperture of bet ween 60degs.and 70.1egs. F. In another twenty-four hours a coat of soap wash may be laid ; and a on, till the walls have formed on them a film of the comp mod which is quite insoluble.</p>
        <p>Cie oici talon', like me, and you dare not face lain after your flight from home. Cheer, up child. We won't see the old gentleman until your mother. has made your peace with him. Our first step mhst be to find her. I believe I have a plan to discover her, even if she does not use her own name in theatres," he concluded, with madden</p>
        <p>energy. " -IVhat is your plan, my lord ? " asked Giralda, faintly. "Simply to write several notes addressed to the ; Countess of Arevalo, and leave them with the I theatre managers for deliverance. The names will i be spoken of at the rehearsals, and your mother cannot fail to hear it. She will apply for the litter at once, of course, for no doubt she is very anxious . about you, and will connect you with the letter. • The result will be that she mid come to you with- out delay. Giraida making no objections to this proposi- tion, the waiters were summoned to remove the debris ot the breakfast, -Wigby was cated to bring his lordships writing-case, aid the marquis him- self dashed off the notes, wor 1n them cau- tiously, and addressing each to a ditlereet theatre. " There," he said, giving one to Giraidie which he had discarded because f a blot. "If your mother receives this, she cannot fail to understand • it. If any one else reads it, no halm is done and nothing is betrayed. You see that I have said simply that 'the Countess of Arevalo may find Giralda ' at our present address, adding, Please come at once.' I have said nothing of myself, or my wishes. I will leave that till we see her." .kin sealed the various epistles, and summoned ' Wigby from the inner room. While he was occu- pied in commissioning the valet with their de- - livery, Giralda glided from the room, hastening to her own chamber with the copy of hie letters which his lordship had given her. Hastily writing a few lines upon the back in ex- planation of the note, and asking for directions as to her own course, Giralda inclosed the scrap of • paper in an envelope, addressing it to the Lady Beatrice Hampton, at Hampton House. Having stamped it, she sent her trusty little maid, to : whom writing was an unknown art, to drop the missive in the letter boy at the corner of the street, and returned to the little parlour, almost before her brief absence had been noticed. The morning wore on, and Wigby returned with the declaration that the name of the Countess of Arevalo was unknown in any of the theatres, but that he had left the missives as directed. "Now we have nothing to do but wait," said Lord Trevelyan, in increasing spirits, his gouty foot having become easi2r. "I am sure we shall hear from the countess. I hope she will not refuse to give you up to my guardianship, for you have already become very dear to me. I am sorry," he concluded, "that my disease should have taken a turn for the worse at this time. I fear I shall not be able to escort you about London during our stay." " Trevelyan Park is much pleasanter to me than this gloomy, foggy London, my lord," replied Giralda, from her seat at the windew. "I would willingly go back to-morrow, if you were willing." "I shall be delighted to go," said the-marquis, "provided 1 can have seen your mother, and pro- vided also that my foot is well enough. My busi- ness will be completed this evening. Next time we come to town, my dear, I hope and trust that I shall be well enough to show you the sights. By the way," he added, "you can still see the sights of London, if you wish. My favourite and friend, the Lady Beatrice Hampton, will no doubt be • glad to take you about." "No, no," said Giralda, with a shiver. "I do not wish to see anything, my lord. I would rather go back to the country—l would, indeed." I The marquis did not urge his idea. In truth, he felt pleased and flattered that this lovely young creature seemed to shrink from all society save his own, and that she ever preferred his companion- ship to all the gay sights of the great town. - ! "She begins to cling to me already," he thought delightedly. "She sees something in me, no doubt —gruff iend gouty and irritable as I am—to love. She is the first person in th is world to love me with a pure and disinterested affection. Perhaps, after all, I am to find a peace and rest in the affection of this young stranger of which I have never dreamed. I begin to love her as I loved Geoffrey, • and she will never repay my affection with base ingratitude as he did. .The hours passed slowly, and Lord Trevelyan began to listen to every step in the hall, and to wonder aloud if the countess had received his message, and if it werenot time for her appearance. Giralda grew nervous and troubled, seeing no outlet to the diffidulties in which she had become involved. Soon after luncheon had been served, and Giralda's secret tremor had almost declared itself in outward signs, a waiter made his welcome ap- pearance, bringing three letters which he presented to his lordship. "Here's a note from my business man," said the marquis, when the servant had withdrawn, "and this square white letter is from Adlowe—at least it bears his crest. And this—why, Giralda, here is a letter for you ! It must be from your mother !" He extended a dainty little missive as he spoke, and the young girl hastily took it and retreated to the window-seat, which she had occupied nearly all day. As she expected, and as his lordship had sug- gested, the letter was from her mother. It contained but a few lines written in a dis- guised hand, and was signed "B. Arevalo." Its contents were to the effect that the writer would call that evening at eight o'clock, circumstances preventing the visit until that hour. On turning the leaf a tiny scrap of paper fell out, and on this was written a warning to Giralda to exhibit no surprise at any disguise her mother might wear. Slipping the loose scrap of paper unobserved into her pocket, Girak a exhibited the letter to the marquis, who read it aloud, "'the countess will be here at eight;" he com- mented. " Adlowe has written to say that he will be here this evening between 'eight and nine. Ilumph ! He don't exert himself to be civil to his old uncle ! That conies of having entailed pro- perty. If I could leave toy title and estates at will, he would be the most affectionate and attentive of nephews " The strong possibility that the Lady, Beatrice and Lord Adlowe Might meet in Lord Trevelyan's parlour that evening, gave a new turn and a greater impulse to Giralda's fears. The business agent of the marquis called upon his lordship and departed before dinner, which was served at six. After the repast, Lord Trevel- yau, with true delicacy of feeling, ordered Wigby to wheel him into his chamber which adjoined the parlour, under the plea of donning evening attire, but, in reality, to leave Giralda, to meet her mother alone. "If you will kindly knock upon the door be- tween the rooms, my dear, wiitn your mother arrives," he said, between his twinges of pain caused by the removal, " Wigby shall wheel me out !"</p>
        <p>The door was closed, and Giralda was left to herself. The time passed, and the clock on the mantel- piece struck the hour of eight: At almost the same momenp catne a knock upon the door. The young girl started up from her crouching posltion .before the glowing fire, where she • had been struggling with a host of homesick thoughts, and flung back from her white brows her dusky waves of hair, and looked with an eager, startled glance at the door. She bad barely uttered, in a faltering voice, a summons to enter, when the door opened 'and a lady swept into the room. Girabla made a convulsive little spring forward, uttering a glad cry. "Altuunid, !" hue cried, and then paused, stir- prked and disappointed as the lady flung back her</p>
        <p>7 v- ail. The face revealed to her .yearning, wondering n. ,m,Tsig. tender face worn in her !secret home by the Countess of Arevalo, nor was it the cold, proud, statuesquely beautiful countenance peculiar to the belle and beauty, the Lally Beatrice Hampton! The visitor was tall and stately, seeming much stouter than the Lady Beatrice, with a less haughty carriage. Her face was wondrously white, except in the cheeks, which looked flushed. Her hair was of a golden hue, and two long yellow curls fell on either side of her face from out the brim of her bonnet. She wore a pair of gold-bowed spectacles, and there were a few lines under her eyes and about her morth, indicative of the approach of age. Her, attire was of rich black silk. Altogether she had the appearance of :an aristocratic middle-aged , lady, with decidedly foreign looks and minners. Giralda stared at this apparition, as if fasci- nated. The lady swept a cautious glance about the apartment, and then quietly , removed her spec- tacles.</p>
        <p>One glance into the wondrous hlack eyes, so softly glowing, so strangety industrious, was %lough tQr • Gjojda,. Pts_pite tke clever_ disguissi</p>
        <p>Ult</p>
        <p>—.A A-A,' and sprang to her</p>
        <p>•1t iv powder, a few painted lines, a wig, deceived even you my darling " • 140,y Beatrice, softly, as ;he embraced the figure with a mother's passionate "How pale you are, my precious ,g;rl Has your courage given out?" " n:), mamma " replied (Arabia, in a whis- per. " 'dy courage is strong—but what can I do? tlw am 1 to prove dear papa's innocence? The way seeitH dark—"</p>
        <p>4lu4,ed Lady Beatrice drew her daughter to a low couch in a shaded corner.</p>
        <p>" In the first place," she said, replacing her speetacLs, "if my disguise has deceived you, who expected me and in disguise, Lord Trevelyau will not dream of suspecting my identity. Yet, to make all sure, I will tone down the gas-</p>
        <p>le proceeded to subdue the light, and then sat do4vn on the couch by Giralda's side, taking both of the young girl's hands in hers. "Von have been homesick, darling?" she asked. A grieved little quiver of Glralda's lips was the only response. ' "I knew you would be. Is Lord Trevelyan still kind to you ?" _</p>
        <p>"As 'kind and gentle as if I were his own daughter," replied the maiden. "Oh, mamma he, loves me already as if I were his child ! I had a guilty feeling to-day, as if I were winning his affection under false pretences. He would turn rue out if he knew whose child I am. It seems as if I were deceitful—and he trusts me so."</p>
        <p>"My darling," said the Lady Beatrice, gravely, "do you not believe your father to be innocent of the crime with which he was charged, and which to-day threatens him with ignominy and a fright- ful and undeserved punishment?" " Yes oh, yes, mamma ! " cried the girl, eagerly. "You believe, then, that Lord Trevelyan has been deceived, and that he would welcome your father back if he knew him to be innocent ? "</p>
        <p>"I know he would, mamma ! "was the fervent reply. "Then why not dismiss all your doubts and fears and give yourself wholly to this grand purpose of clearing your father's name ? " said the Lady Beatrice. "Oh, if he could only once again assume his rightful position among men 1 If he could only walk the earth fearlessly and undisguised ! If I might only present my children to my father and to the world ! I am sick of all this mystery ! I am growing fearful of detection, too! Oh, Giralda; if you can only reconcile your father with his uncle, I will bless you till I die ! My darling, you will do it?" "How can I, mamma ? " asked Giralda, sadly. "Last night I feltistrong to do it. To-night there seem 80 many difficulties in the way. "We have changed moods, then, dearest. - After you left me last evening it seemed to me that some loving, pitying angel must have led you to Tre- velyan Park. I believe that you will gain for us all a great happiness. You can humanise that miserly, misanthropic old man. You can 'tad him to talk of poor Geoffrey, and you can suggest in many ways Geoffrey's innocence and Adlowe's guilt—for, in fny heart, I know that Adlowe brought about that midnight assault on the sleep- ing marquis. How he did it Ido not know. He might have frenzied Geoffrey with a drugged liquor, and then set him on to murder his uncle, working up the poor boy's mind to a perfect hatred of Lord Trevelyan. I cannot explain how it happened, but I will stake my life on Geoffrey's innocence of intended crime. Adlowe always hated his cousin. Giralda, can you not make all this plaiu to the marquis ?" "1 will try, mamma." • "My love, think how much happiness you can bring to us all! Think of the safety poor papa longs for ! Think of Rupert, of Fay, of me, who am condemned to a false and hollow existence which I loathe ! "</p>
        <p>The Lady Beatrice's voice was tremulous with her earnest pleading. A strong faith in her daughter's power to unravel the mystery of that long-ago attempt to murder Lord Trevelyan had taken possession of her soul. "And, darling," said her ladyship, as Giralda raised her mother's hand to her lips and kissed it, 'there is one thing more for you to discover. In those old days, Lord Adlowe had a valet some years older than himself, a base sort of fellow, who pandered to all his master's vices. He was not .a bad man at heart, your papa thinks, but Adlowe corrupted him. That man might be able to clear your father's name. He was in the adjoining room on that fatal night when Geoffrey drank the drugged liquor that stupefied his senses. Geoffrey remembers ,seeing him through the half- open door at the moment of raising the glass to his lips, and he thinks that Adlowe was unconscious of the valet's proximity. I have advertised for him again and again. I have sent a person into Wales to search for him, but he could not be found. He is dead, or has emigrated, in all probability. In the latter ease, s me servant at the park, or some villager, might know his address." " What was his name ?."</p>
        <p>" Negwyn. He was A, WelAmatt. Oh, if you could hut get trace of him, Giralda! I think he could be bribed to toll the truth. He overheard many conversations between the cousins, and he could say much to clear Geoffrey. He disap- peared a few months after Geotfrey,fled to South An terica."</p>
        <p>" I will try to find him,. or his address," declared Giro.lda, with a resolute look. "I feel strong for the work before me. I will be brave and cautious, aud avoid Lord Adlowe as much as possible, as you told me, last night. Al if I last, mamma, I will go home to papa and my brothers. But I shall not fail," she added, noticing how the suggestion pained her mother.. "1 will win success by hard work an'd unfailing courage." The Lady Beatme caught the girl to her breast, kissing the pure, spirited,. lovely face in tender piuiionateness. _</p>
        <p>"Remember how much depends on your success," she whispered. There was a brief silence between the two; a silence more full of meaning than many words, and tlieu Gmraids. getiLly dLen,s,raged herself and whis- pered : " And new, mamma,. shall I not announce your coming to I.4;:rd Trtwelyan ? Your disguise is per- feet. He cannot recognise you." The Lily Beatrice assented, and Giralda hast- en,,d to knock upon the door of the marquis's chamber, and to, announce the presence of her inoLher. A moment inter his lordship, in his easy. chair, was wheeled out into the dimly lightly par lor by his valet, who then retired in obedience to a gesture from. his master. ( To be e,ontinued).</p>
        <p>TuE trouble is with .you young fellows now- 4-days, you Want to begin where your.. fathers left otTi" said an Old •chap out West [don't want ft:begin. where my father left. off," replied one of the young fellows present. "No? And where did your father leave off?" "He was hung." " iSIA; did the wicked children say, Go up, old bald head,!. to good • Elijah?"' " Yes, dear." " And did the bears, come and at them?" •" It is told us, my 'son." "And would they come and elt, me iLI said, 'GO up old bald head,' to pa?" "Possibly." ' '" Wotild they you?" . "Probably not." • '"TF en,- you say it to him and I will give you a penny, to seepie bears."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Care of Knives and Forks.</head>
        <p>The Care of Knives and Forks.</p>
        <p>If it should happen that the steel of knives and forks become rusted, dip them in sweet oil and let them stand for twenty-four hours, then rap them with powdered quicklime, and the stain will be removed: The handles of knives, no mater what the mwerial, should never be allowed to stand in water. The water, particularly it is hot, loosens the handles from the tang and also dulls them. A tin or granite-ware pail or pitcher shout dbe kept exclusively for knives. When used it should ha ye some water in it, but not enough to come up to the handle of the knife. Ai soon as the table is cleared, the !calves should be put in this, to remain until the time for washing them. If you have no regular case for the knives, they may be kept in one made of flannel. To make this, take a piece of flannel about three quarters of a yard wide and cut off twenty-oce inches. Fold over eleven and a halt inches of the selvedges end, leaving a single thickness of about four inches at the other end Baste the double part together; then stitch into twelve compartments. Bind the bag with tape, hnd sew tapes on the single flap at the centre. Of course; the flannel i on the inside.. Then steel cutlery is to be put away for any length of time, melt pure mutton suet, and dip the :1-!el part of the 'knives and forks in it. When cool,' wrap in tis Sue piper, and then in thicker paper or flannel.</p>
        <p>Rest in the Simmer Holiday.</p>
        <p>But Jane thing is all-in portant about a sumrner holiday for all parents who are j tiled and worn out, arid, in •st ,sf all, for the bread winner of the family. The chief end of it is to be rtal re-creation, in the good old sense —that the worker shall go back better and stronger and more cheerful for it. Observation teaches us, however, that this is often prevented by mistakes so common that they pass almost unnoticed, but so evident in their effects that hven " Punch " has embalmed them in jests, about people being almost as well as before thes! went away, after being a few weeks at home again - they may, says •` Cassell' Book of the Household." be summed up in the one general error of attempting to crowd so much into the time, that the result is coming home again fairly wearied and tired out. If it is a Con- tinental tour, the body is tired by long and constant journeys, and the mind by a constant whirl of sight see- frig,which isnot given tirm really to refresh and interest the mind. If It is a cycling er.walking tour,people who have for 19ng months been unused to exercise, plunge at once into an amount they cannot possibly take without lassitude. This is easy to understand ; but people do not seem to understand that they may do the very same thing at the seaside. From close confinement to deskwork they will rush at once into long swims (if good swim- mers) in the morning, with long walks or other excur- sions all the day afterwards ; or they will perhaps play tennis all day,with little intermission. They can hardly have too much of the fresh country or sea .air, but there is not enough of gradation in their rush from sedentary. life into active exercise which their flaccid muscles Can- not bear. ,There is not enough all through of idleness, of mere rest, in the holiday of many people ;.and there is a great deal to be said for that lazy beach lounging which is the superb scorn of some superior folks. These do not understand that men and women tired out by real work require such rest, and enjoy it ; but these latter themselves should see to it, that they take enough of the precious blessing whilst within their reach, and do not lose it by crowding too much of sight seeing or hard physical exertion into their holiday. It is a great thing to be ahie to " idle " on proper occasions ;.it often does a world of good, and the man is ereatly to be pitied Who has lost the enviable power of enjoying simple existence under the blue vault of heaven,.with the song of the birds or the whisper of the sea sounding in his ears. We have rdr,gnised that for some change and excitement are a neeMity.; but it is a very sad necessity. And even for them, a measure of both physical and mental exertion is essential. Even in so trifling a thing as reading, which is more or less generally a part of seaside occupation, novels of such a class as compel the reader to contiom into late hours, and so excite the mind as to banish sleep even then, sheuld be carefully avoided by all whose health is in need of improvement.</p>
        <p>ACCOUCHMENT o THE DUCHESS OF YORK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>BIRTH OF A SON.</head>
        <p>BIRTH OF A SON.</p>
        <p>The Duchess of York gave birth to a eon at ten o'clock on Saturday night attbe White Lolige, Richmond. Tne QueQ,n was made acquainted with the birth of her great- grandson within a few minutes of the event; in fact, a quarter of an hour had not elapsed when the first tele- gram of congratulation reached the White Lodge. The Princess of Wales proceeded early on Saturday morning to the White •Lode, where she remained, being joined late in the afternoon by the Piince of Wales. The Home Secretary was also summoned, and by virtue of his office was present when the birth took place.</p>
        <p>THE PRYER OF THANKSGIVING. At a meeting of the Privy Council on Tuesday, the Archbishop Canterbury was ordered to prepare, a form of prayer and thanksgiving to Almighty God for the Duchess of York's safe delivery Df a prince, to be used in all churches and chapels in England and Wales on Sun- day, theist of July. All the ministers of the Established churches in Scotland are also exhorted to offer up I prayer and thanksgiving.</p>
        <p>The Czar of Russia plays on the cornet. it is said, with such utter disregard to time and place that someone once.: hinted that it entirely acc)unted for the existence of Nihilists, •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>DESECRATION OF NATURE.</head>
        <p>DESECRATION OF NATURE.</p>
        <p>Although the Homoca Co. have had ono of the greatest schemes in rural advertising put before them, they have declined to entertain it on account of the widespread disgust existing in the minds of all who love their woods, dales, fields, streams, rocks and hills in the glory of their natural beauties, undefiled by the modern billposter. They (The Homocea Co.), are Content,witli the legitimate channel of the Press, and by Pamphlets containing Testimonials. They can roint to cures, far more convincing than mere assertion. TESHMONIAL rum BISHOP TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>KM BARNET.</p>
        <p>My dear old Friend,—l distributed a variety of your remedies. among afflicted. natives, and among nfissionaries in Sierra Leone, Liberia, Coast of Guinea, Congo Ccuntry, and Angola. South of Congo, I have not been over those different and distant fields since, and have, no definite information in regard tf) their curative power. I have used HomocEs. and have proved its healing virtue both for severe bruises and flesh wounds, and also to kill the virus of mosquitoes and chiggoes (jiggers). Yours very truly. (Bishop) Wtx. TAYLOR. American Methodist Epihcopal Mission.</p>
        <p>LORD CARRICK saws HOMOCE&amp; cured him of bleed- ing piles, when all else failed; that he Rare some to a labourer who was lamed by a stone falling upon him, whom it cured. A woman had a pain in the elbow and could not bend it for a'year, and it cured her, and another used it for scurvy on her leg, and it was doing her good—one letter closes from him with the words, "It is the most wonderful stuff that I ever came across.' Remember that EIOMOCEA subdues inflammation and al!ays irritation aimust as soon as applied for it</p>
