      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>On, THE ,HAMPTON MYSTERY.</head>
        <p>On, THE ,HAMPTON MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>By the Author of " DARIEWOOD:"</p>
        <p>-CITAPTER XIX, FORTUNE FAVOURS GIRALDA'S PLANS.</p>
        <p>It is scarcely necessary to say that the remaining hours .of the night on which she had made her secret visit to the Lady Beatrice Hampton brought no sleep to the eyes of Giralda. She was excited and bewildered by the story she had heard from her mother's lips. It seemed to her utterly incred- ible that a ge.y.and wild Geoffrey Trevelyan—the object of his uncle's bitter and undying hatred—the youth who had been caught in the attempt to mur- der his .lordship—him whosee.ause she had promised the old housekeeper at the Park to advocate— was after all her own father—her grave, noble, student father—the being of all others in the world she most honoured and revered.</p>
        <p>"He was never guilty of that attempted crime !" she repeated to herself vehemently, again and again, as she sat in her dimly lighted howl cham- ber. "Never .l never : Papa is incapable of harming a living creature! Poor papa! VI, hat if Lord Trevelyan wen to suspect my parentage! What if Lord Adiowe were to discover mamma s secret ! " • •</p>
        <p>Her face paled at the idea of such a possibility, and she shuddered with a slow-creeping horror. "Lord Adlowe is as cruel as death," she whis- pered, crouching by her window. "He knows no pity. And he loves mamma, white he hates with an awful hatred poor papal. If papa should be dis- covered by eitber of his two bitter enemies, he would be transported for life. It would kill my mother—my proud, beautiful mother! It would cover my brother Rupert with disgrace—poor Rupert, who is so proud and high-spirited! And Fay would die—dear little, tender, poet, Fay! Oh, what a fearful doom seems impending over us all !"</p>
        <p>She shuddered again—a long convulsive shudder —and looked through the window up at the gloomy sky with a wikt and questioning gaze. After a long silence she spoke again, still in a low, horror-strieken whisper. "Between us and all that terror and death, what is there? Only a veil of secrecy and mys- tery which may be rent at any time. An incau- tious word—even a look—may precipitate the ava- lanche which will overwhelm us And who is there to stand between papa and all this peril? Only me I." She repeated the words, as if they gave her strength. • Then, growing calmer, her sweet young face grew resolute with a holy purpose, and her eyes glowed like shining lamps with the determination that was growing jn her soul. " Providence sent me to Trevelyan Park," she thought, "and it was Providence that has made Lord Trevelyan like me. I have gained a place in his affections, and I must use it for papa. From this moment • I consecrate myself to the task of making clear, my father's innocence of the crime of which he is accused.! I will know neither joy nor rest until his name is cleared, and until he and Lord Trevelyan are friends again ! " She uttered the two last sentences half aloud, and with the solemnity of a vow. She was not insensible to the difficulties and dangers in hei way. She remembered that Lord Trevelyan intended to seek for the ,supposed Countess Arevalo at the various 'theatres, believ- ing her, as Giralda had innocently declared, an ac- tress on the boards. What would he say when he found his quest useless? Would he insist on a visit to The Evergreens? And if he did, what excuse could she make in refusing him? These and lgndred questions occupied the mind of the young girl until long after the dull light of the London morning had struggled into her cham- ber.</p>
        <p>At last, however, she arose, summoned her little Welsh maid, and proceeded with her toilet. • , Having attired herself, and banished the sad and troubled, look which seemed so out of place on her lovely young features she descended to the little private parlour, where Lord Trevelyan was already waiting her coming. His lordship was seated by the bright hearth, his gouty foot wrapped in shawls and reposing upon a softly cushioned chair. His cynical face wore a gloomy, discontented expression, and his broWs were es;Ttracted with pain. His countenance brightened at Giralda's en- trance, and he welcomed her with a fatherly smile.</p>
        <p>"You are. pale• this miirning, little sunbeam," he said, kindly, as she came up to him, and in- quired solicitously after' his welfare. " Last night's dis4ation did you no more good than it did me. I have hurt my foot by using it so ranch, my dear. Ah ! that horrible twinge ! " and he drew his breath hard. "I wish we were back at the Park, child ! " "So do I," Giralda exclaimed, with unconscious earnestness. " Oh, my lord, can we not go back to-day ? " The marquis looked at her flushed and eager face in some surprise. " To•day ?," he said. "I could not be moved to- day. lam as helpless as alog. My foot is swell- ed, and," he added, with a groan, "that con- founded Vtrigty 'seems to have lost all his skill in caring for it. There's a great fold in the shawl just under *my foot—oh, dear! "and he groaned again. Giralda. gently lifted the bandaged and afflicted member in her arms, and deftly smoothed out the offending fold.' The marquis drew a long breath of relief. "It's a blessing to have somebody about that knows something !`" he observed. To think of my being a prisoner at the very time I have so much to do! I meant to have visited all the leading- theatres to-day, besides transacting the bugness which brought me to town. I have written to My business man to come to me this evening, so that is disposed of. But how are we to find your mother ?" _</p>
        <p>Giralda paled and flushed in what his lordship thought a most unaccountable manner. He had not time to remark upon it, if such had been his intention, when Wigby, his valet, op- portunely entered the room. . The sight of his sleek and silent servitor seemed to irritate Lord Trevelyan's unquiet temper. • "Just pull the bell for breakfast, Wigby," he said in a dictatorial tone. "There I that will do. They'll ,think there's • murder going on. here. Pity you can't put a little of that superabundant energy of yours into your service to me. Now 'push me up to the table. ° Blazes man! Do you mean to kill me outright? Stop! Let me be ! " • His lordship's progress to the table was checked midway, and he leaned back on .the cushions of his chair, his grand, rugged old face convulsed with agony that Giralda sprang to his side in Bud- den terror. She sileiatly motioned Wigby to relieve and soothe the,;pramped and tortured foot of the inva- lid, while she bathed his lordship' brows with strong cologne water, and caused him to inhale a a quantity of sal volatile. in a few moments the spasm of pain had passed, I and an exquisite sense of relief succeeded. "Since it is so clearly proved that Mahomet cannot. go to the mountain," said his lordship, ' "the mountain must come to Mahomet. In other words," he. ,added, marking the b.milderment of his servitor, "you can push the table to me Wig- by." . The valet obeyed, and was then dismissed. . ' He had scarcely disappeared when the break- fast was, brought in by a couple of white-aproned waiters, one of whom spread the cloth. andiaid the table in an incredibly short space of time. When they had retired Giralda took her place behind. the coffee urn, and proceeded to wait upon the. table with a very pretty air of housewifery. "After .all," said the marquis, as he took the cup of fragrant coffee from her hands, "it seems more civilized, and in every way more pleasant to see a lady pour the coffee. I've been living like a heathen all these years, with that clumsy Wigby to wait upon •me. I am impatient to see your mother, my dear, and to assure myself that she will not take you from me. A sudden tremor passed over the young girl's figure, but she did not look up. "I have been thinking of the best way to get at the matter, since I cannot go out," continued the marquis, not noticing her emotion.. "I have thought of a personal in the first column of the Thunderer.' But that won't do. for it would not' a,Olt tatalia.:, a t „ pras I.4„‘a,atunj JaoS loam O PiJ oxe BOA,,</p>
