      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>GREAT COAL STACK FIRE NEAR ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>GREAT COAL STACK FIRE NEAR ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The large coal stark at the New Boston Colliery. Hay. dock, belonging to Messrs Richard Evans and Company, Limited, is on fire. The firm, in consequence of the depressed state of trade, have been compelled to stick coal at all their collieries, and at the New 11-iston pit some 7.000 or.10,(X)0 tons have been laid down wince the strike.ot last year. On Friday last smoke was noticed issuing from the top of the stack, about ten yards from the side nearest the road. This denoted that the st/lek was on fire internally, and gangs of men com- menced to remove the coal. t Lines of rails have beep laid from the side of the stack on to the pit brow, and as the coal is filled into boxes' it is being removed and placed in railway wagons. Bose pipes have been laid from the reservoir adjoining the colliery, and water is being paured on the burning caal. The tire is apparently burning at the base of the stack, and as the men remove the coal from the bottom, flames occasionally burst forth. These are .promptly subdued, and he fire is b ing kept within as small a radius as-possible. A lar e number of men, in relays, have been continually at wort( since Friday, and their efforts have been, largely success- ful. Oil reaching the seat of the fire on Tuesday, the workmen found that tons of the coal had been reduced to cinders. This is now being removed, and will prob- ably be used by Messrs. Evans at their colliery and other furnaces, and the other, which has been filled up in waggons, will be sold as slick. This will involve con- siderable loss to the firm. "TOBACCONISTS commencing" 111 d. guide (2E9 pages) 3d. TOBACCONISTS' CO.-186, Easton-toad, London. Manager. HY. MYERS. Eat. 1866.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>WARY DUNNING'S MEMORA.NDUM. We were talking about getting lost, and various ~lemhers of the little company had same experience to tell on that point. "A fellow may get lost in London, an know,' said one, "but he's a fool if he 4ays lost ; here's always a policeman or somebody else to pat Yod "Well," chimed in a long-legged American, who was eprawled out on the couch in a corner, "the worst time, I ever had in that line was in the Catskill Moontains 013 the Hudson. When you get fairly in 'em there's no end ta those mountains, and they are just thatched with woods—some of the trees almost as high as St. Paul's. I was crossing the main range, afoot and alone, and nissed the path. For thirty-six hours I wandered round tnd round hoping to find it again. At last, tired, fa,int, Frightened, and famished, I stumbled upon a spot, free •.4 bushes, under a great oak, that looked as though 1)eople had camped there not long before. There were • marks of a fire at the base of the tree and some scraPs of paper lying about. Among them was a lady's memorandum book with the name 'Mars' Dunning old letters on the red cover. The book was soaked with but the writing was legible.. On the last page was Ate following entry, dated just one week earlier : We re a party of five crossing these wild mountains for a (eager none of us ever having been on them before. Al we have to guide us are brief directions from an old meuntaineer, which I have written down in this book. 'this spot is described by him. We are all right so far. ft-morrow should bring us to our destination. The course," as they say at sea, is due west on the tap .f this ridge, and the distance eight miles. I kissed ' that dainty book as though it had been a wayside cross. It was better than meat or drink. It put new life into me, and the next night I had the happiness of restoring a to its fair owner, who gave it back to me as a keep- sake on out wedding day one year afterwards. A. great c.hing is a book of the proper sort when you are dying or lack of kuvwledge."' Exactly. " I read in a book," says a Leeds lady, "01 what Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup had done for .nany people, and it cured me as it had cured them.. Lad been weak and ailing from girlhood. I had no real .r relish for my foad, and always felt tired and languid. Uter the simplest meal I felt a kind ot uneasiness at the• '.• chest and sides, with palpitation of the heart. I .elfered much with pains in the head, in my back, and etween the shoulders. In time I got so weak that I hardly had strength to walk, which made it difficult for me to attend to my houcehold duties. Indeed I wao ..ften obliged to lie down on the couch and rest. " I tried different kinds of medicines, but obtained no. celief, until one day I came upon the book I have alreadY" AludEd to. Having read the book, I bought a bottle of the Syrup from Mr. Davies, the chemist, in Park Laney, . and after I had taken its contents I found that nay food agseed with me, my appetite was improved, and I had 7 gradually gained strength.</p>
        <p>" Of course you will understand that I was so pulled ' elqwn wi:h all these years of suffering that it took time 04 o bring me round ; but after having used the SyruP for nine mPnths I felt better than ever I.did in my IVe.. Since that time I have been in good health. Calling , fle day at the chemist's, after my recovery, Mr. Davies . said : ' Mrs. Crosby, you do look well. When I last saw Vou I thought you would never be strong again.' I told' him, as I tell others, that Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup had put fresh life into me, and made me a new woman. .• li.,u are at liberty to publish this letter if you think, proper. Yours truly (Signed) ELIZADETH CROSBY, 1.3, Rutland Terrace, Burley Road, Leeds, July B:.h, 1892." '- Millions of copies of such hocks as this lady mentions are scattered abroad in hope thwt they may fall into the' : hands of those who greatly need them. That they are . ike seed sown in good ground is proved by the grateful .icknowledgments which reach us from all sides. Indigestion and dyspepsia, the one disease under rnanl, .; names, which the Syrup cures, is everywhere producing - Rs a.a.ftil crop of misery and death. Therefore what- ever indicates where the remedy is to be found is surely ~ akin to the valuable memorandum discovered under ace' foreA oak when he was bewildered and ready to perish,'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
