        <p>NOT QUITE SURE. There would be less damages for libel if one always remembered that it is one thing to make a statement, 4nd another to prove it ; that it is one thing to know, and another to prove, either good or bad qualities, c0n- ,.....49.110t1y it is best to make no statements, especially in writing, unless one is" quite sure" and can easily prove them.</p>
        <p>When :Ars. Snell, of 40, North Street, Horsham, made the sweeping statement, which we give below, with t2ference to Warner's Safe Cure, she was careful to know Kils could prove every word of it, and no one can gain- what she asserts: even the proprietors of Warner's afe Cure are content to allow her statement to co un- thallenged. She asserts : " For fifteen years my life tlas a daily burden. I suffered from inflammation of bladder and indigestion; burning pain in groin and excruciating pain in the pit of the stomach, ',leadful aching of the eyes, coldness of the extremities, u.l6tressingflatailencv,pain and burning during urination, i'oncl bad taste in the mouth. I was extremely nervous, lr,w'sPirited, and gradually growing weaker, with great f;nsl of flesh. After all these years of suffering and ess attempts to better my condition, I was cured arner's Safe Cure. I can walk long distances with- Pain or inconvenience. Have regained my flesh, lifea' natural life-vigour has returned, so now I enjoy enjoy.d ase."follow my domestic occupations with comfort '4°l e</p>
      </div>
