        <p>tol.t,,haa been rumoured for some days past that fr,,tu Randolph Churchill contemplates retiring tie" 111 Public life, and that the announcement of his toParture on a prolonged holiday is only a prelude n„an authoritative statement to this effect. I can- b.".ascertain, however, that there is any definite 4318 for the report. Lord Randolph's friends are tifrit,°`lBl3' uncomfortable in regard to the condition i th_ 9 health, and they do not shut their eyes to ,"_u Possibility that unless it shows some improve- heZent may hardls find it possible to continue his 'vork • th.„ in Parliament. But it is practically certain •• he leaves England without having come to any %,,clsion in the matter, and with the hope, in which 48e,rYbody will join, that the prolonged change will 14)re his energies. T.P." in the "Sun,' saw M. Carnet once, uad a short, conservation with him. It will be ,4erabered that at the time of the opening of the Prench Exhibition Lord 'Salisbury was guilty of to„ftt English Liberals thought a wanton act of clis- L'rtesy to the French Republic; and accordingly resolved that some manifestation should be. k;nus, to undo an offensive snub to the free govern- Of our great neighbour. About this very time, to, as announced that M. Carnot was to visit Calais Den the splendid new docks which have been ,rec, Iltilt`eu there ; and some of us resolved to take ad- age of the opportunity to go over to France pateresent to M. CaTnot the expressionof our sym- Aft,,Y fur himself, his 'Government and his country. t'kr a great deal of rushing about from six o'clock morning we were presented to the French tticit_ident, said a few words, and received a kind ktro."rier reply, delivered with an extremely plea- Itotf,,-Tindeed, delightful—smile, and the courtesy bred%istinction of manner which belongs to a well- ' r renChttlan.</p>
        <p>At thl•a -- , moment and several times throughout the 'g ad d n opportunity of closely studying M tic,lL°tiand he produced a most favourable impres- -11/44.11P0n me. He was a small man, with a slight 'tiltB`e, and though he looked keen and clever—and )3(41,1.111 those things as a matter of fact—there was ri4,c'eloß distinguished or striking aLout his exterior tl3t his wondrous, untiring and incessant cour- t Vi His face was long, thin and sallow; he wore • k beard and moustache—and altogether eu like a 'quiet Civil servant rather than the Ititc, of a nation. But throughout all the terrible tilm'ctlea of along day—the smile—patient, amiable bettlcTen—never once left his face. He must. have Lity Lerriblfy fatigued, and in his place I should e been. bored to extinction. But though there Itizt lines on the face of suffering, tension, and t,„l,etY, the smile was always there. It was only re Pore, exalted, and singiilarly sweet and th 51% h that•could have given such an example of 10/;earfol Performance of tiresome and even distress- He looked what he was—an amiable, 14htle, h: Jrzh-minded, incorruptible citizen—the ta Aeris. tor of sacred and lofty traditions of devotion ""tY, honour and country.</p>
