      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <p>st. adier influences than either M. Dupuy or M. isson would have been. Be is conscientious has tie courage of his opinions, will not drift, and pos- sesses great powers of work. Though M. Brisson has a large Socialist follawing, it is not from any personal magnetism in the man himself, and even his follwing were not unanimous in his favour. Of the twelve or thirteen names which have been mentioned in connection with the presidency, the sekcti mi which has been made will unquestionably command the widest approval throughout France.</p>
        <p>An interesting comparison between the water supplies of Loudon and Paris has recently been made. It appears that Paris is certainly far ahead of London in the purity and freshness of its water supplv,as also in the lowness of charges to the con- sumers generally. The total supply daily in Lon- don in 1892 was 173,500,000 gallons. or 33 gallons per head on a population of 51 millions. The total available supply in Paris now that, the Avre gravi- ta' ion scheme is completed is 162.750,000 gallonA, or 65 gallons per head on a population of 21, millions. Toe net income per head in hondon is 4s 4.i.whilst in Paris it is only Is.lld. There is no longer a necessity for the supply of unfiltered Seine or Marna water for domestic consumption in Paris.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>JUBILEE OF THE WARRINGTON TRAINING COLLEGE.</head>
        <p>JUBILEE OF THE WARRINGTON TRAIN- ING COLLEGE.</p>
        <p>The Jubilee of the Warrington Training College was celebrated on Saturday, and the occasion was also taken advantage of to hold a bazaar in sup- port of the fund for the extension of the college. Tne College was founded in 1844, during the rector- ship of the Hon. Mr. Powys, who manifested great intereA in the scheme. He first of all built the Clergy Daughters' school which was intended for the orphans of the clergy of the old diocese tof Chester, and it was his wish that when the students attained the age of eighteen years, they should by passed on to the train- ing college. This scheme, however,did not succeed,and the two institutes are now separate. Originally there were only ten or twelve students in the Training College, but the number has gradually risen until there are at present 113 under dairy tuition. The original cost of the building was £6,131. The first principal of the col- lege was the Rev. H. C. Stubbs, who held that position for over 30 years, and resigned in 1883. In January 1884, the Rev. Morley Stevenson succeeded to the office which he still hAds. The results of the tuition at the College have been very gratifying, and since its founda- tion, 2,500 students have been educated inside its walls. Several useful additions have been made during the Rev. Morley Stevenson's period of office. In 1887, Sc. Kath- erine's Chapel was opened, and is in daily use for divine service for the students, and the practising schools in Hamilton-A. In recent years, the need of increased ac- commodation has been felt by the authorities, and an effort has been made to raise £4,500 in order to make the necessary additions and alterations. The Mount schools and the adjoining land were purchased for £3,000, and furnishing, SLI , involved the expenditure of another £1,500. £3,000 has already been raised, so that the amount reqdired for furnishing is the only item now requiring to be obtained.</p>
        <p>The proceedings on Saturday passed off in a very satis- factory manner, and large numbers of old students availed themselves of the opportunity afford,3d them of visiting their old school, and meeting their old friends again. An excellent programme was provided which took up the whole of the afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>LUNCHEON.</head>
        <p>THE LUNCHEON.</p>
        <p>At one o'clock a luncheon was provided in the school I drawing room, presided over by the Rev. Morley Steven- son. The folliwing 'received invitations to be present : Lord Bishop of Chester, Dean of Manchester, Archdeacon Woosnam, the Rev. Canon Rountree Penrhyn (Winwick), the Rev. Canon Heron (Moore), Canon Heywood, Canon Kelly, Canon Holmes, the Revs. F. W. Willis (rector of Warrington), F. L. H. Millard, A. Ma-kharn (Aughton, OrFnskirk), A. A. Markham, R. A. Whalley, B. Fair- clough, El. G. Crews, G. B. Thurston, E. J. Wilcocks (headmaster of the Warringtcn Grammar School), and A. Bater ; Mrs. Jayne, Mrs. Willis, Mrs. Maclure, Mrs. R. Ireland Blackburne, Mrs. Penrhyn, Mrs. Morley Stevenson, Mrs. Wilcocks, Mrs. Marknarn, Miss Ferri- man, Miss Cole, Miss Beal, Mrs. Bater, Mrs. Whalley, Miss Goddard, Mrs. Arthur Edelsten, Miss Webb, Miss Watkins, Mrs. Sharp, Mrs. Heywood, Miss Crew. Mrs. Atkinson, Mrs. Markham, Mrs. Hodgson, Miss Smith, Miss Clifton, Mr. Robert Pierpoint, NIX., Mr. Ireland Blackburne, Captain :Buckton, Dr. Watkins (Newton-le- Willows), Mr. Haworth, Captain Edelsten, Dr. Guest Gornall, and Mr. Radcliffe. Later in the day, Dr. Rity- ston (assistant bishop of Liverpool) was amongst those who took pait in the jubilee celebration. The luncheon was an admirable one, and was provided by Mr. speak- man of the Saracen's Head Hotel, Wilderspool, and there was an excellent staff of waiters. After the usual loyal toast had been honoured, Mr. R. PIERPOINT, M.P.., gave the health of the Lord Bishop of Chester. He said that he had found that the Bishop and himself must have been at Orcfnrd at about the same time for they took their ,degrees at the same year. He was proposing the health of a very distin- guished man who, after serving his time at his original college,went to Jesus. College, thence to Keble,and after- wards to Lampeter. He then obtained the vicarage of Leeds. He had worthily filled the position of Bishop of Chester, and it was with great pleasure that he gave his health.</p>
        <p>The toast was heartily received, and the Bishop of CHESTER in responding said that he could not help think- ing of Lord Salisbury's speech which was reported in the "Times" of the previous day. Lord Salisbury was speaking somewhat severely of the examination tests, and pointing out how when a senior wrangler was made, one generally wondered twenty years afterwards, how ever they made such a mistake. (Laughter.) Mr. Pier- point had evidently read Lord Salisbury's speech, and was evidently under the impression that persons who were prematurely anxious for University distinctions deteriorated not only intellectually but morally. (Laughter.) With regard to Warrington, he considered that they were going forward in every respect but one, and in that they were going backward. The greater part of the Warrington cabs were quite in a position to keep up their jubilee. (Laughter.) He hoped that those who had any superfluous energy would do their best to facilitate the visits of those coming from other parts of the diocese to the College, and produce a more vigor- ous, better ventilated, and on the whole a cleaner race of cabs. (Laughter.) The DEAN of MANCHESTER then proposed "The Train- ing College," and the Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON in reply said that he had received great kindness during the period he had occupied the position of principal of the College. They were on the eve of important changes. The alterations made to the College would, tie believed, tend to increase its efficiency and usefulness. They must have teachers who were thoroughly good in them- selves and thoroughly religious in the best sense of the word as well as being thoroughly capable teachers. He believed that the College would continue to send forth teachers in the future as it had done in the past, who would be a credit to the College, and a help to the Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev. CANON PENturvN propnsed "The visitors," to which the Rev. A. MARKHAM responded, and the pro- ceedings then terminated. SERVICE AT THE PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>A service was held at the Parish Church:at 2.15 p m., at which the Lord Bishop of Chester (Dr. Jayne) was the preacher. His discourse was based upon Nehemiah 6c.. 3 v.. " A great work." He said that the training of those who would be trainers of children was without controversy a great work. great even numerically, for during the last half century,2,soo teachers had gone forth from the Training College. Let them try and imagine what the range of their responsi hilities had been. It was great from whatever point of view they looked at it— from the point of view of culture, of patriotism, and above all of Christian faith, hope, and love. The teachers fulfilled an important office, and he would have them remember the corn mission given by Jesus Christ to His disciples to he endued with the -Holy Spirit. It was the cintv of the teacher to keep up the dignity and import- ance of his office. There was a story told in the Talmud which illustrated what the Jews thought of the teacher. It was said that on a certain oneasion, there was a great drought and the rabbis prayed and shed tears that they might brine dawn rain, hut the heavens were locked against their prayers. At length there came forward a man who prayed, and the heavens were covered with clouds, and the rain fell. When asked why his prayers were answered, he replied that he was a. teacher of little children. Dr. Arnold, the prince of educators, said that the methods of government depended upon the education of the young. The teachers and pa.rents alike in training lin their children must have touch private prayer, and he was glad that they had a college chapel in connection with the institution. He honed that the chapel would he the centre of their work. They should approach God every morning, and bring the day's work to Him every Proninu. There must he no onesidedness in their education. A 5. ta the College whose jabilre they were celebratine that day, he was glad to say that it was complete in its many sided. rwss. He was glad to know that the usefulness of the College was now to be largely increased, and it pleased him above all, that they would he ronre comfortable,a.nd that thase who came there at a critical time of life would find it a. real home, as there was e,mry appliance and convenience of home. By the purchase of the Mount Schools, the Colieae would have a harmonious equipment, and it would have the anplianee=„ the need</p>
        <p>of which had so severely embarassed them heretofore. He therefore hoped that they would have a still greater enthusiasm for their work, and that they would train a still greater number of teachers in the principles of the doctrines of the Church. MEETING OF PAST S ITDENTS AND FRIENDS.</p>
        <p>After the service in the church was over, a meeting of past students and friends was held in :the grounds adjoining the College. A large number gathered to- gether, and, as the day was beautifully fine, the meeting was very much enjoyed. Mr. R. Ireland Blackburne presided, and he was supported on a raised platform by the Bishop of Cliest,T, Dean of Manchester, Rev. F. W. Willis (rector of Warrington), Rev. Morley Stevenson, Mrs. Ireland Blackburne, and Dr. Watkins. Mr. IiLACKBURNE said thht he felt highly honoured at the invitation of' the Rev. Morley Stevenson to preside at that meeting, on the occasion of the celebration of the jubilee cif the college. He need hardly tell them that he had the welfare and success of the College at heart. He was clad to see that during the last ten years, the per- centages at the examinations had increased enormously. The percentages in first class religious knowledge had increased from nine to 82. The government examina- tion had also been very satisfactory, as also had the ex- amination in music. One of the most interesting fea- tures about the college was the way in which it had endeavoured to keep in touch with its past students, and he maintained that this was an essential part of the development of a training college of that description. In consequence of the very great success of the college, it had been found necessary to enlarge it by the acquisi- tion of the adjoining building, Mount school. The cost .of that property had been defrayed by the kind dona- tions of many friends and suppmters, including many old students. The property however required furnish- ing, which required a further sum of £1,500, which was absolutely necessary. At the bazaar which was to be ' held that afternoon, he hoped that they would try to make it the succesq it deserved. (Applause.) The DEAN of MANCHESTER then addressed the meeting. He observed that there was no " old " students of the college as they were of the feminine sex (laughter.) The past students were however heartily welcome at the jubilee of the Warrington Training College. He earn- estly hoped that the next 50 years of the College would be as successful, or even more so, as it had been dur- ing the past 50 years. 'Die Rev. 11101tLEY STEVENSON said that they were cele- brating that day their fiftieth birthday. During the past four years, they had had considerable additions and changes, he hoped .for the better. He felt proud of the enthusiasm of the students of the College, and he hoped that that afternoon they would raise funds sufficient for the furnishing of the building they had recently ac- quired.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
