      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>111 E XAMINER SA TURDA 1, JUNE 30, 1894.</head>
        <p>111 E XAMINER SA TURDA 1, JUNE 30, 1894.</p>
        <p>TilEcr BAZAAR.</p>
        <p>Before the reunion of the past students and friends was over, advantage was taken to formally open the bazaar. MRE4II.TRELAND BLACKBURNE briefly declared the bazaar open, and a vote of thanks to her was proposed by the RIXTOR of WARRINGTON. He -said that they were greely indebted to Mrs. Blackburne in that town. Fifty years ago, Mr. John Ireland Blackburne had given them the site of the parochial schools in Church-st., and about the same time he practically gave the land for the Mount School. Just lately, Colonel Blackburne gave them the site of the new Fairfield schools. He expressed the hope t h it on July 'l2th, Mr. and Mrs. Blackburne would grace with their presence, the opening of the Fairfield schools. That was an occasion of great moment a.nd deep pathos to him and to the parish. They were parting with schools in which for 50 years they had worked under the shadow of the church. The Mount schools had been built by his revered predecessor Mr. Powys, the late bishop of Sodor and Man. They were deeply indebted to th,- authorities of the Training College for the large sum they had paid to them for the schools. He hoped that still more useful work would be done by the Train- inv.!' College in them, under the tuition of their most excellent principal. ' Dr. Watkins seconded the vote of thanks to which Mr ISlacburne replied on behalf of .his wife, and the bazaar was then open for business. _</p>
        <p>There had been great preparations made for the bazaar and the schools looked •charming with the enormous amount of decorations which had been bestowed upon them. Almost every room was occupied by stalls upon which innumerable useful and ornamental articles were placed to allure the passer-by to part with some of his spare cash. The following were the stalls and stall-holders : 1, Fancy and plain work, Miss Goddard, Miss Ferriman, Miss Time- well, Miss Perry, Miss Etta Timewell ; 2, China, glass, wood-carving, bric-a-brac, Mrs. Morley Stevenson, Mrs. Bat-r, Mrs. Sherratt, Miss.L. M. Rowley ;3, Sixpenny stall. Masters Clement Whalley, Cecil and Harry Wools; 4, Books, pictures, photographs, &amp;c., Miss lina Ferriman Miss Bell, Miss Humphreys, Miss Baldwin ;5, Flowers, Miss Beatty, Miss Wa.rrener 4.6, Refreshment stall, Mrs. W. S. Woods, Miss Eatough. Miss Smethurst. There were a large number of entertainments, includ- ing theatricals, maypole dances, kc. In the dining hall a charming comediatte in one act, entitled "Withered Leaves," was performed, and the characters were very ably taken by Miss Sehotleld,Miss Collins, Messrs. Arthur Browne, W. H. Cookson, C. H. Wood, and G. T. Brund- rett. A iarce in one act entitled, "Freezing a Mother- in-law," was given, which tickled the risibilities of the audience considerably,and the following were the actors —Miss Schofield, Miss May Pierpoint, Messrs. A. Woods, G. T. Brundrett, and C. H. Wood. A display of "Mrs. Jarley's world-famed living waxworks," was given, which was much enjoyed. The characters were Sus- tained by the senior students, and at intervals appro- priate tongs were sang by a number of the junior stu- dents. Two grand concerts were held during the after- noon and evening, in which some very high-class music was given, including -selections from The Mikado, Pa- tience, Olirette, Gondoliers, and Pirates of Penzance. Mr. W. S. Woods performed the duties of conductor,and the various pieces were undertaken by Misses MacCarthy, A. Ridgway, R. Perry, M. Bell, and Averil Lane, and Messrs. R. S. Teague, Price,and R.T. Fairclough. During the afternoon a maypole dance was given on the la.cyt, by twelve elves from the Clergy Daughters' School. There was a photographic studio in the Mount; Schools, and Mr. Birtles was kept well occupied during the after- noon in taking groups and individuals who wished to see ' themselves as others see them. The band of the First Volunteer Battalion South Lancashire Regiment was in attendance at the lawn, under the conductorship of Mr. Thomas Armstrong, and enlivened the scene by going through an excellent programme of music. The pro- ceeds of the day amounted to £136. The bazaar was opened again on Monday.</p>
        <p>m.....•••i1ie.m.............•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WHO IS RESPONSIBLE FOR BAD ROADS? ACTION AGAINST WIDNES CORPORATION.</head>
        <p>WHO IS RESPONSIBLE FOR BAD ROADS? ACTION AGAINST WIDNES CORPORATION.</p>
        <p>On Friday at the Widnes County Court, before his Honour Judge Shand and a jury, an action was .heard in which Robert Broome, residing in Rain- hill, sued the Widnes Corporation for damages for injuries sustained to his horse and cart on the 20th of October last, alleged to be caused by the negli- gence of the defendants. Mr. Horridge (instructed by Mr. R.W. H. Thomas. appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. T. Swift (instructed by Mr. H. S. Oppen4eim, town clerk) represented the Corporation. The case for the plaintiff was that on the date named, about six o'clock in the evening (when it was almost dark), Mr. Broome was returning in a gig from Widnes to Rainhill. He had with him a hoy. Just after they had passed the man- hole and before they came to Cowley's house the horse got its feet among the loose slag. The animal fell and broke its traces. The plaintiff and the youth were both thrown out of the gig. The horse bolted, and it was afterwards found that both its legs were broken. The trap was damaged beyond repair, and the harness very much damaged. The Corporation had at the date ot the accident taken up the road in question for the purpose of laying down sewer mains. The work un to a place called Three-lane Ends had been properly done by contractors (Sayco and Randle), hut above this point the work was done by the Corporation. Loose slag and cinders had been put on the portion of the road where the trench had been made, and no lights were provided at the point where the accident happened, hut the following day a lamp was lighted near the scene. For the defence, Mr. Swift submitted that in the case before the court the defendants were under no liability what- ever. They were the municipal corporation of the borough of Widnes, and in that capacity they were the local authority under the Public Health Act of 1375. In that respect the defendants were the surveyors of the high- ways and it had been abundantly shown by anthoritioa from the earliest times under the Highway Acts of 1835 down to the present time that a highway authority was not liable for any negligence. Mr. Swift quoted several cases in support of his contention. After evidence had been heard, his Honour put several questions to the jury, who, after a short deliberation, , found that the accident happened cn the slag portion of the rend ; that the defendants were guilty of negligence in -making and leaving the road in the state in which it was ; that the defendants were negligent in not provid- ing a light or fence on the slag portion of the road ; that the plaintiff did not contribute to the negligence ; and that if the plaintiff was entitled to succeed they feuncl for him with -P.30 damages</p>
        <p>fli 110110tIr tk,liti he would reserve the question of I.w, and would deliver his judgment in Liverpool.</p>
