      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>The industries of Warrington by no means 'show any signs of diminution in variety and numbers. Two new works are about to be started in the Bank Quay district. The Mersey rubber works situated on the river side at Bank Quay—the buildings of which comprise a goodly pile--are now just getting to work, and in a short space of time it is expected that they will employ at least 300 workpeople. The works are lighted throughout with .electric light which is far more advantageous than gas in the making of such an inflammable commodity as rubber.</p>
        <p>The other new works at present being erected are for the chemical trade. They are to occupy the site formerly taken up by the Sunlight soap works. Large tanks for use in the making of chemicals are in cowrie of erection. The proprietors are Messrs. H. D. Pochin and Co., Ltd., who have factories in Salford, Miles Platting, Newcastle-on-Tyne, and Bristol. These works will to some extent fill the breach caused by the removal from the town of the chemical works of Niessrs. Roberts, Dale and Co. A jetty is being erected on the river side for the despatch and receipt of goods. When in full swing the works will find employment for a large number of workpeople.</p>
        <p>There was quite a variety of summonses at the Wairingtoa Borough Court, last week, and the youths of the town must hemn great consterna- tion at the inroads made on their liberty of playing in the public streets to the annoyance of passen- gers. There were two who were brought up for playing at pitch and toss, and, by the way, it is a curious thing, that almost all these cases are offences committed on a Sunday. Mr. Hunt had to report loud complaints received from all sides con- cerning this pernicious practice and he did not consider that previous offenders had been punished sufficiently. The magistrates decided that that morning they would not be so very severe, but hoped that everyone in Warrington would remem- ber that the maximum penalty for this offence is 40s. and costs or a month's imprisonment. Let us hope that this announcement will have a good effect amongst juvenile offenders.</p>
        <p>Another couple of lads were summoned for play- ing a game bearing the mysterious name of "tip- cat" in the public streets. The public who were in the court were enlightened upon the mysteries of the game, and it was then discovered that the game was simply the well known game of " peggy." This is a very dangerous amusement in the public streets and should certainly be stopped. The third game against which the power of the law was brought to bear was " rounders," which is a very harmless game provided it is played in a field, but it is obviously not a pastime to be playei where there are many houses about. The magis- trates did not impose any fine on the eleven youths although they cautioned them against the practice. They considered that public recreation grounds should be provided by the Corporation at various parts of the town for the youngsters to amuse them selves as much as they wished.</p>
        <p>This question of public recreation grounds is a very old on,i,and has been on the election addresses of 'candidates for the Town Council time after time. The Council however, has as yet made no move in this direction, and we appear to be as far off as ever from having these " breathing places." In the meantime the youths of the town amuse themselves in defiance of the law, and will continue to do so until they have places provided for them.</p>
        <p>Should any county ratepayer have any doubt as to the "economising" power of the representatives of the Warrington and district Highway Board, a visit to the Board meeting, especially when tenders are being discussed should suffice to more than satisfy him of the very zealous exertions made by the waywardens to keep down the rates. The meet- ing, last week, was a case in point. Spades were a fruitful topic for debate. One member was sorry that samples were not placed in the room, as he, being a practical man, would have been able to judge by the appearance and proportions of the qpade whether the labourer would "take to it "and do more work than perhaps might be the case with a spade supplied without the members' inspection. The very few engines belonging to the Board re- ceive, according to one member, a too plentiful supply 3f oil, which in his opinion should only be applied when the machine "squeaked." The tend- ers for these two articles were accordingly deferred for further deliberation.</p>
        <p>Mr. William's Diggle's efforts on behalf of the distress in Warrington are not appreciated in the least by the inhabitants of the town,and one would almost think that the old gentleman would be gett- ing tired of crying in the wilderness, with no one to hear him. Last week, he announced that he was going to deliver an address on the fair ground upon the poor laws,showing that as they are at present administered, they are" harsh, cruel,and inhuman." The unemployed, and those in distress were invited to attend. The unemployed and dis- tressed persons were however not disposed to spend their Saturday night with Mr. Diggle and when the time for meeting arrived, there was an audience of one. Mr. Diggle waited for about three quarters of an hour for the meeting to come to him, but as by that time his audience had only increased to four, he sorrowfully withdrew,no doubt meditating upon the apathy of those whom he wished to help.</p>
        <p>I have been favoured with a copy of the 43rd annual report of the Amalgamated Society of Engineers which has just been issued. The report is a very bulky one and contains very useful infor- mation of the doings of the society in various parts of the world. The Secretary, Mr. J. Anderson, reports the society to be in a flourishing condition, and he gives a very interesting summary of the various questions affecting this branch of industry. The Warrington branch is reported to contain 202 members, who have the satisfactory balance at their disposal of £5BO is. 1011. During the year five of the members, and two members' wives have died, two have been superannuated, and three have received benevolent grants. The meeting place of the local branch is at the Green Dragon Inn, Bridge-st., and the secretary is Mr. John Berry. That the society generally is in a Very flourishing condition is self evident, when it is stated that in December 1893, there was a total of 73,526 mem- bers, and that there was a total balance of £185,854 24. 11d., or an average amount per member of .C 2 103. 61d.</p>
        <p>I notice that a supplementary catalogue of the lending department of the Warrington library has been issued by Mr. Madeley, the librarian. It is a book of 100 pages, and contains some useful infor- mation with regard to the borrowing of booxs, and the technical institute. Should the borrower not, know the author of the book he wishes to obtain, he will be able to fini it in its proper alphabetical place. Readers who do not find what they require in the catalogue, are requested to apply to the librarian. A suggestion book is also kept for the entry of books recommended for purchase, and is laid before the Museum, , Committee monthly. Some interesting details with regard to the news room and reference department are given. The news room is supplied with 100 daily, weekly, and monthly periodicals. The reference and lending departments each contain 12,000 volumes, and are constantly receiving accessions of recent publica- tions.</p>
        <p>The united scholars service held in Bold-street Wesleyan Church last Sunday week, inaugu- rated by the Warrington Sunday School Union was a decided success. The church was crowded with Sunday school scholars, and the address which the Rev. Carey Bonner, of Sale, delivered will be long remembered by them. The rev, gentleman gave his hearers a symbolical address, calling to his aid a number of magnets, with which he illustrated Christ's remark "Ancl I, if I be lifted up, will draw all men unto me." A feature of the service was the reading of scripture by five of the scholars whom Mr. Bonner jocularly ;eferred to as his curates. The success of the meeting should en- courage the SuiV.ay School Union to hold more ser- vices of a similar kind, and as they do not interfere with the ususl Sunday school hours, good attend- ances should be easily obtained. MERLIN.</p>
        <p>EPPS'SI COCOA.—GRATEFUL AND COMFORTING.— "By a thorough knowledge of the natiirahlaw• which govern the nuetittions of digestion and nutrition and by R careful applica- tion of the flue properties of well-selected Cocos., Mr. epos has provided for our breakfast and supper a delicately flavoured beverage which may save us many heavy doctors' bills. It is the judicious .ise of such articles of diet that a conatitutiien may be gradually built up until strong enough to resist every tendency to disesso. We may escape many a fatal shaft by keeping ourselves eon fortified with pure blood and a properly non ished frame "—'• Civil Service Gazette."—Maiie sim;-lywith boiling water or milic.—iold oniy in packets, by Grocers. litiv!lled—•• JaarEs HPYS AG CO., Itd , Homoeopathic Chemists London."—Also makers of Cocosine or Cocoa Nib-extract ; A thin beverage of full flavour, now with many beneficially taking the place of tea. Sold in 'Warrington by I3owden, Bamford, and Percival. —Advt.</p>
        <p>NV Ill: DO MEN AND WOMEN SUFFER With Pains in the Back and Lumbago, when they can so easily and speedily obtain instant relief without medicine or inconvenience by applying such a world-wide and world-tried remedy as Allcock's Plasters, the proved efficacy of which is shown by the sale in England alone of upwards of Twenty Millions. Allcock's Plasters instantly stop all Aches and Pains, and promptly relieve and cure Sprains and Strains, Lumbago, Sciatica, Rheumatism, Rup- ' tures, fonchitis, Asthma, and all affec ions of the throat and lungs, acting directly on the tissues and dilating the pores of the skin they soon show their healing power, and justify all that has been said in their favour. The popular name and just fame of Allcock's Plasters has led to shameful piracy. by un- scrupulous imitators, many of the imitation plasters containing Lead and Cantharides (Spanish Fly), involving serious risks that should never be permitted, unless under the direction of a medical man; it is therefore necessary to ask the public, for their own safeguard, to see that they get filo genuine ALLCOCK'S PLASTERS. ALLCOCK'S of all Chemistz, from Is. I?id. eiidi</p>
        <p>•.. . _ • • I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITIEE ANNUAL 'REPORT.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITIEE ANNUAL 'REPORT.</p>
        <p>THE SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION FOR WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The seventeenth annual report of the School Attendance Committee has just been published. The returns of the past year were not complete owing to no examination being held in seven public ele- mentary schools in consequence of the epidemic of smallpox which prevailed in the borough during the greater part of that school year. The year's results were on the whole satisfactory. The Chief Inspector reported that the average attendance was 8,198 in 1892 against 8,175 in 1893, which showed a decrease of 23 for the year. At the close of the school year (September, 1893), tie number on the school rolls was 10,436, but of this number 1,341 were under the age of five years. The number of children attending private schools, including the Blue School, Grammar, and Workhouse Schools was 643, but nearly all the Workhouse children were at Padgate. The full attend- ance of children from 3 to 14 years of age at both pub- lic and private schools was 8,818. The number of chil- dren on the school rolls, public and private (not the average attendance) was 11,079, as shown herewith : Number on rolls, public schools, 10.436; number on rolls, private schools, 643 ; total, 11,079. The second return shows the numbei of children who were pre- sented at the general examination—ln 1892 the number presented was 10,359; In 1893 the number presented was 10,436; increase, 77. The St. James' School was not , examined in 1893, on account of an epidemic in the dis- trict, but the children who would have been examined are included in the 10,436 shown above. The number of prosecutions during the year was 384. In 1892 the number was 366,an increase of 18. Thirteen boys were sent to the "Clio" ship, and four boys to Father Nugent's home. The Board of Guardians had allowed school fees during the year to the sum of £ll 144. Compared with 1882', when the amount was £42 133. 9d., it showed a decrease of £3O 193. 9d. This was very gratifying, and required little explanation, the reduced rate in school fees having enabled the parents to pay who formally had to apply for free education. There had been 20 meetings of the committee held during the year, and 460 parents had been summoned before them.</p>
        <p>I deputation from the committee visited the ship "Clio "on July 6th, and the Boys' Refuge, Liverpool,on July 11th, to inspect the boys committed to those institu- tions from Warrington, and a report of those visits which were considered very satisfactory, had been pre- sented to the Council in September last. rho committee segretted to report that the education department had determined to remove the Cockhedge Infant School from the list of schools in receipt of annual grant on the 30th September next, in consequence of the buildings, in their present condition, being consid- ered unsuitable for continued recognitien, and the man- agers not being prepared to make the necessary altera- tions. The department had informed the committeethat a notice would shortly be issued in accordance with the Elementary Education Act, 1870, setting forth the decision of the department as to the additional Public Elementary School accommodation which would Ibe required for the part of the district affected. This was a most serious matter, and it was evident that if they could show that there was sufficient amnnmoda- don in the district the department might insist upon the same being provided by means of a School Board. The committee would be happy to co-operate in every proper way with the school managers, recognising fully the present financial burdens that were pressing so seriously upon the town. The committee had been in communica- tion with some of the schools, and were pleased to learn that increased accommodation would be provided at Fairfield and Hamilton-street, and these, with the vacant places at Silver-street and the National Schools, all within the centre affected, would, it was anticipated,see over the difficulty for the present. It must not be for- gotten, however, that the number of children upon the rolls in the public elementary schools at the end of the year was 10,436, whilst the actual accommodation, according to the return, was 12,123, so that there was not a wide margin for that steady annual increase that took place. The department had further called the attention of the committee to applications which had been made to them for free school places in the borough, and a return had been prepared and forwarded for the information of the Department of the number of free places provided in each school, and the number of vacant free places at the present time. The committee were pleased to learn that the total number of free places provided was 3,599, including in that, 1,108 in infant schools. The number of vacant free places was 728,which included 150 in the infant schools. In addition to these there were in schools where no free places were provided, 32 cases in which no fees were charged owing to the poverty of the pareats or other exceptional circumstances.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEA.LTII OF WARRINGTON. Week ending 16th June, 1894. During the week, which was the 24th of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths. Warrington 32 11 Latchford 9 2</p>
        <p>Total 4l</p>
        <p>The excess of births over deaths was 28. The death- rate from all causes was equal to 12-07 per I,ooo,against 15-4 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 18-8 the corresponding week of last year. The death- rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 0-9 per 1,000, against 3-4 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 7-5 the corresponding week ot 1893. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-60 60 up. 3 3 1 1 3 11 1 1 2</p>
        <p>The deaths included— 1 from scarlet fever, 1 from erysipelas, 1 from phthisis, 1 from cancer, 4 from brain disease, 3 from lung disease, 1 from heart disease. The following cases of infgctious disease have been reported during the week :—Scarlet fever 2 cases. Total No. of Patients in hospital on 16th June, 26. JOHN H. GORNALL. Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>PRINCE BISMARCK'S CURE FOR SOCIALISM.</head>
        <p>PRINCE BISMARCK'S CURE FOR SOCIALISM.</p>
        <p>Though the Socialists must by no means be confounded with the Anarchists,a Berlin correspondent of the "Daily News," thinks a letter written by Prince Bismarck nearly sixteen years ago is at present exceptionally in- teresting. It deals with the Anti-Socialist law, and is dated Kissingen, 15th August, 1878, the year of the Eloedel outrage on the late Emperor William, in conse- quence of which the first Anti-Socialist Bill was brou2ht in. Prince Bismarck writes :—" In my opinion, the paragraph according to which only unemployed persons can be expelled is inadequate. Further, the Bill must contain A paragraph in virtue of which all officials dis- missed shall be dismissed without pension. The m rity of the badly-paid subordinate officials in Berlin. the platelayers on the railways, the pointsmen and officials of similar categories, are Socialists—a fact the danger- ousness of which during riots and the transport of troops is clear. I further believe that if the Anti-Social- ist Bill is to prove efficient it will in the long run not be possible to leave to citizens who are proved to be Social- ists the suffrage, or the elegibility tor election to, and the privileges of members of, Parliament." The letter is significant of all Anti-Socialistic legislation. Badly- paid officials are to be robbed of their one privilege only because they are Socialists. It may be thought that an increase in their wages would have been a better means to cure the men of their Socialisti3 inclinations.</p>
        <p>Like a great many other mortals, the Prince of Wales never forgets a face, although lie is not good at remem- bering names. It chanced once that H.R.H. was saluted by an elderly gentleman whose face seerned familiar. " I believe I have met you before," he said," but for the moment your name has escaped me." "Made your breeches, your Royal Highness," the old gentleman answered with a little bow. " All, of course," said the Prince. "How d'ye do, Major Bridges ?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>EMMANUEL REFORMED CHURCH, WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL REFORMED CHURCH, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>It is fifteen years since the commencement of the reformed church movement in Warrington, and on Sunday in celebration of the fifteenth anniversary, special sermons were preached in Emmanuel Church (Reformed Church of England) 80ld-street, Warrington, by the Vicar, Rev. E. T. Reed. At the morning service the rev, gentleman spoke on the subject "The founder of the Reformed Episcopal Church." In the course of an historical sketch of Bishop George David Cummins, of the Protestant Episcopal Church, it was stated that he was ordained deacon on October 10, 1845, and presbyter on July 6th, 1847, by Bishop Lee, of Delaware. He filled important appointments in Norfolk, Richmond, Washington, Baltimore, Chicago, and on November 15th, 1866, was consecrated Assistant-Bishop of Kentucky, by seven bishops of the Church of England in America, he being the 81st in the order of succession of the American Bishops. On Sunday, October 12th, 1873, he took part in a United Communion Service held in New York in connection with the Conference of the Evangelical Alliance. For this act of Christian charity he was severely condemned by members of the Ritualis- tic party in his own Church, and the opposition aroused by his most Christian action brought to a focus the opinions, which had for years been ripening in his own mind, that the only way to successfully counteract the ritualism and sacerdotalism which had become so pro- nounced in America, as in England, was to organize a church possessing all the features of the Church of Eng- land, which are so dear to its members, but without the accompaniments which have been the outcome of the Tractarian Movement. Bishop Cummins therefore voluntarily laid down his work in the Protestant Episcopal Church.and on December 2nd, 1873, organized the Reformed Episcopal Church. Other Bishops were duly consecrated, and the work spread under the care of Bishop Cummins. His own earthly career ended on June 26th, 1876. and when asked on his deathbed if he had any message to send to his beloved Church, "Tell them," he said, " to go forward and do a grand work." A similar movement was begun in England, and in the face of great difficulties has been carried on ever since. From the history of its inauguration lay the founder it was certain that this movement was of God,and that being so it would assuredly prosper. At the evening service the Rev. E. T. Reed delivered an address to the confirmation candidates. " Luther " was the title of a service of song which was given at the church in the afternoon by the choir. There were moderate congregations at each ser- vice, and collections were taken in aid of the church funds.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening, the Rev. P. X. Eldridge, presid- ing Bishop of the Reformend Episcopal Churches,paid his first official visit to Warrington, when he officiated at a confirmation service held in the church. The Rev. E. T. Reed assisted in the service. There was a good attend- ance. About fifteen persons were confirmed. An address to the candidates was delivered by Bishop Eldridge. He said he knew how careful his friend Mr. Reed, had pre- pared them for that service, and instructed them in Christian doctrine. Betore the service continued, and they took their solemn vows in the presence of God and the congregation, he asked them to consider what the service was. He would remind them in the first place that the service was one of self-abnegation. They were all admitted to the Church at their baptism. Then they were, of course, unconscious of what took place. Now they had come to years of discretion, and they were about to dedicate themselves to the service of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, the Lamb of God, who took away the sins of the world. They came there not simply to think of God as a Father and a Saviour, hut to recog- nize him as their King. The service, therefore,was one of self-dedication. It was no secret matter. They came publicly to declare themselves on the Lords side, to publicly declare that henceforth Jesus Christ was their Master? He would ask them whether they were pre- pared to do that? They were not to regard the service as a mere custom, but a privilege of the Episcopal Church, a privilege from which they might turn and be good soldiers of Jesus Christ. After the laying on of hands and the singing of a hymn, the Bishop again ad- dressed the confirmee,s. He said they had just girded on the armour of the Lord, and they would need to put on fresh armour every day until the warfare was finished, and they stood in the presence of the all-living and glorious Lord. For the continuance of their faith they must not depend upon themselves but upon the strength of the Lord Jesus Christ. They were soldiers of Christ, and they must not expect their life to be an easy one. His work was very bard. What they had to do was to put their trust in God, and their Christian course would be maintained until the end, when they would have a glorious entering into His Kingdom. Christ wanted them to be out and out on His side. Not merely to be His in name, but to be determined to serve Him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>BIRD SHOW AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>BIRD SHOW AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The twelfth annual exhibition of canaries, mules (in first feather), British and foreign birdß, in con- nection with the St.lielens Canary and British Song- bird Society, was held on Saturday. There were twenty-four classes and 189 entries, the entrants coming from Liverpool, Warrington, NVidnes, Hindley, Runcorn, Horwich, Manchester, Preston, Rochdale, Ormskirk, Altrincham, Westleigh, Sze. Mr. J. Bexon, of Derby, was the judge, and the following were the prize- winners :-Belgian canary : 1, 2 and 3, J. Walmesley, Ormskirk. Yorkshire (clear yellow) : 1, Hampsnn and Goodwin, Penketh ; 2, S. Davies, Runcorn; 3, Heaton and Stableford, Manchester. Yorkshire (clear buff) :1, Wellings and Wood, Prescot ; 2, H. Abbot, Farnworth ; 2, [lampoon and Goodwin. Yorkshire (yellow or buff, ticked or variegated) : 1, Jackson and Longworth, Flor- wich ; 2, H. Abbott; 3, T. Fenny, Farnworth. Lizard (geld, clear or broken cap) : 1 and 2, J. Wrigley, Glossop; 3, Lloyd and Fothergill, St. Helens. Lizard (silver, clear or broken cap) : 1 and 2, J. Wrigley ; 3, Lloyd and Fotherv,ill. Crested Norwich: 1, P. Grim- shaw ; 2, A. G. Nuttall, Norwich; 3, W. Ridge, Wigan. Norwich (clear yelloW) : 1, C. Warburton, Florwich ; 2, Atkinson and Pleat), Burtonwood ; 3, Wellings and Wood. Norwich (clear buff); 1, Atkinson and Heap; 2, Wel- lings and Wood; 3, P. Pendleton, Present. Norwich (yellow, ticked or variegated) : 1, C. Warburton ; 3, H. Abbott. Norwich (buff, ticked or variegated) : 1, P. Pendleton; 2, Wellings and Wood; 3,- J. Clark, St. Helens. Cinnamon (yellow or buff) : 1 and 2, S. Sidde- ley, Altrincham ; 3, Lloyd and Forthergill. Gift class (any variety) : 1, T. Fenney ; 2, S. Andrews, Widnes ;3, H. Abbott. British bird (smaller than thrush) : land 2, Halton and Chesnall, St. Helens; 3, T. Fennev. Larger British bird: 1, J. Ball, Present ; 2, W. Gorse, St. Helens; 3, J. Cross, St. Helens. Mule or hybrid: 1, 2 and 3, Houlton and Chesnall. Small foreign bird : 1, 2 and 3, W. Osbaldeston, Preston. Large foreign bird: 1, 2 and 3,. W. Oshaldeston. Novice class: 1 and 2, E. John,on, Sutton' 3, F. Wolfall, St. Helens. Selling class (bird and cage net to exceed 7s. 6].): First prize withheld ; 2, E. G. Hall, Present; 3, H. Abbott. Selling Class (bird and cage not to exceed 12q. 6d.) : 1, C. A. Scott, Heaton Mersey; 2, H. Abbott; 3, S. Andrews.</p>
