      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>EttttrO to tin Otittor.</head>
        <p>EttttrO to tin Otittor.</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>PROTECTION AGAINST SMALLPDX.-THE RIVAL THEORIES.</p>
        <p>Sin—Between the theories and teichings of vaccinists and anti-vaccinists there 's a great gulf fixed. The vaccinists teach that "Sanitary measures have no influence on smallpox," (Simon's evidence before Select Committee, 1871) ; that however healthy a person may be he is liable to become infected with this disease unless he be cow-poxed ; that smallpox is due to a specific microbe, that it spreads by contagion, and never arises de novo ; that vaccination produces "a permanent mor- bid condition of the blood," (Sir James Paget, Lectures on Inflammation, 1863), and that this morbid condition is the best safeguard against smallpox. Anti-vaccinists, on the other hand, teach that smallpox is caused by a disregard of sanitation, and occasionally by variolous -vaccination, and that at some period of its history smallpox must have arisen de nova, and may do so now. In the February number of the "Nineteenth Century," 1884, the late Dr. W. B. Carpenter, opposes Sir James Paget. He wrote :—" The cachescia, or general blight of the constitutional powers, in persons liable to suffer from such zymotic diseases, is chiefly manifested in the diminution of the red corpuscles of the blood, with increase of the colourless corpuscles. Now what is it that determines the infective nature of the disease germs thus affecting a susceptible person ? This something appears to be supplied by overcrowding of the patients thus affected. Overcrowding means deficient air-sup- ply, and deficient air-supply means deficient oxygena- tion of the blood, producing an accumulation in the circulation current of those waste products which are normally eliminated as fast as they are poured in. Just thirty years ago I showed that all the known predispos- ing causes of epidemic diseases might he generalised under one expression, namely, the accumulation of decomposing nitrogeneus matter in the blood, either through its introduction from without in foul air, impure water, or putrescent food), or through its exces- sive generation within the body (as by unusual waste of tissue) or by an obstructed elimination of the normal waste (such as results of bad ventilation, or the misuse of alcoholic liquors). And I showed that zymotic poisons which have no action upon pure blood, will by seizing upon this appropriate pabulum increase and multiply in it ; thus setting up a " zymosis "in pure blood, just as the growth and multiplication of yeast cells at the expense of the nitrogenous matter ot a wort effects the transformation of the sugar into alcohol." Dr. W. J. Collins points out that" The one grand and fundamental conclusion at which M. Pasteur has arrived, and which he holds to be part of a universal law, is this—that the exciting causes of disease can be weakened and des- troyed outside the body by natural aeency, viz, pure air.' I could multiply quotations to show that cleanliness, good focd, uncontaminated water, and a plentiful sup- ply of pure air, are the scientific prophylactics against smallpox, but it is sufficient to show that the unvaccin- ated child with pure blood is better protected against vartola, than the vaccinated zhild with its blood mixed with cow-pox virus. The miserable laws that makedis- ease (vaccinia) a thing to be coveted, and good health a crime, should be resisted at all costa. and it is only by such resistance coupled with self-sacrifice that bad !awe can be overthrown.—Yours faithfully, JAS. R. WILLIAMSON. 11, Charles-street, Euston Square, London, N.W. June 24, 1894.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>RAILWAY MYSTERY AT WARRING-</head>
        <p>A RAILWAY MYSTERY AT WARRING-</p>
        <p>TON. STRANGE DEATH OF A WOMAN.</p>
        <p>The body of a woman named Catherine Lovett, of Swannington, Leicestershire, was found lying near the line just outside the Bank Quay High Level platform, early on Sunday morning. The woman was quite dead, and had received severe injuries to the scalp, and also had her right band cut off. An inquiry touching the death of the deceased was held at the Patten Arms Hotel on Wednesday morning, Mr. S. Brighouse was the coroner. John Lovett, was the first witness called, and stated that the deceased was his mcther, and her name was Catherine Lovett. She was the wile o: Thomas Lovett, a collier. She left his house at Swannington on Satur- day morning last, when she proceeded to Colville station and went by an excursion train to Blackpool. The train was to return from Blackpool at 11.30 p.m. the same day. From information he received, witness came to Warrington and the body lying at that hotel was that of his mother. She was of sober habits, and was in good health when she left his home. Witness had seen the articles belonging to the deceased which had been found in the train. Part of the handle of her satchel had been torn off, and her boots were missing. Walter Richardson an engine driver in the employ of the Great Western Railway stated that about a quarter to two on Sunday morning he was running through Bank Quay station in charge of a goods train from Chester to Manchester, when he noticed something lying near the up line close to the platform. He informed a porter of ' wbat he had seen. James Shaw, porter at Bank Quay Station said from information he received from a driver of a goods train he went to the top of the platform. He there saw the body of the deceased lying near to the line about 30 yards from the platform. He informed the station mas- ter, and the body was removed to the Patten Arms Hotel. At 12,55 that morning the South Laicestershire excursion train from Blackpool had gone through at a good speed. Near to where the body was found there was a signal post and iron ladder, and the body seemed to have been thrown against these. William Ileece, engine driver of the excursion train, said the train left Blackpool three minutes after the ap- pointed time. Their first stopping place, with the excep- tion of a short stay at Poulton, was Crewe. No one could have entered the train after it left Blackpool until they stopped at Crewe. He received a telegram at Crewe to examine his engine,but he did not find anything on it all. Riobard Wall said he was the guard in charge of the excursion train to South Leicestershire. He fastened the carriage doors before their departure from Blackpool and remembered speaking to a woman who was alone in one of the compartments in the carriage preceding the guards van. The woman would he about 40 years of age. He could not swear that she was perfectly sober, but she was not intoxicated. The carriage in ; which the woman VMS in was being reserved for a party! that were to join them at Crewe. A juryman remarked that if the woman wasn't sober I she must have been intoxicated but the Coroner said the thermometer for drunkenness was a very long one. The juryman contended that it was a distinction with out a difference. - Witness continuing, said he found a small hag and cricket bat in the same compartment as the woman wa in en the tra7n reaching its destination. The handle of the bag was broken. Witness did not see any hoots in the compartment. When the people got in at Crewe, ro o .e said anything to him as to seeing the articles in the compartment without an owner. Dr. Starkey Smith said he made a. post-mortem ("X amination of the deceased. There was a large wound- from the top of the skull which was a clean cut one. ,On separating the scalp and looking into the skull he found the bones fractured in all directions, particularly on the left side of the head. Ha examined to see whether there was any injury to the chest. Be found 'the ribs and breast perfectly intact, the only marks on the lower part of the body being on the left thigh. The The right arm had been cut off above the elbow. The injuries could have been caused by the deceased being thrown against the signal post and ladder near to where the body was found. The woman had certainly the appearance of not being a drunken woman. The jury after a few minutes deliberation decided to adjourn the case in order that they might he supplied with further information. The Coroner said he thought the jury would have been content to find an open ver- dict. They had not to find out how the deceased was killed. The foreman of the jury said they had been wader a misunderstanding. After another short delibera- tion the jury returned a verdict that the woman was *" Found dead."</p>
        <p>MEXICAN CURH. KNOWN AS REV. JOSEPH HOLMES' 'IE.XI- - Preserintien. is the only guaranteed Remedy for all those who suffer from the errors of youth, nervous weakness. exhausted vitAlity, kidney. bladder, gravel. prost- ates:id kindred complaints. and has stood the test tor years. Send a self-addressed stamped envelope to Joevra A-MAD&amp; Remedy Co., Bloomsbury Mansirns, Bloomsbory Square, London, and get full particulars ot this great Remedy free of charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
