      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team-hTve-a.lgain had to submit to the indignity of getting a god thrashing, and this time it is by the Oldham club,with whom they drew at the first meeting. Their batting at the match was of the poorest description. Of coune, the light was very poor, and this had a great deal to do with the failure of the bats- men. The amount of scoring done by the Warrington men may be well shown by the fact that there were two " ducks," three singles, two fours, two sevens, a ten, and a sixteen not out, which with five extras made the grand total of 56. J. Callon was the best man on the Warrington side, and had the others stayed with him,he would no doubt have put on a large number of runs. The @I:11y other batsman to make any stand was th e captain, R. Smith, who made ten.</p>
        <p>The Oldham men quickly set about to pass this small score, and they had reached it with seven wickets to spare. G. M. Swailes in particular was very much at home with the Warring~,on bowling, and his quota was 46. The only successful Warrington bowler was Bates,who secured six wickets for 22 runs. The other bowlers were completely "off." The innings of the Oldham team realised 145,which put them in the majority of 79. The afternoon was a very miserable and cold one, a con- trast to the weather at Warrington,but notwithstanding this, the fielding of both teams was excellent. It was in the batting line as usual that Warrington were de- feated.</p>
        <p>The second team had much better fortune than their seniors, for they defeated the Oldham second eleven by 84 runs. Smith for Warrington carried his bat right through the innings for 73, and Woods also contributed 2. The fielding of theOldham men was rather loose forWa.rring- ton obtained no fewer than 19 extras., The batting of the Oldhamites was not exceptionally brilliant, for five of them did not score a single run.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Wesleyans continued their victorious career on Saturday, and had the satisfaction of taking down the St. Helens Primitive Methodists by 65 to 46. This result was largely due to the splendid batting of Jefferson and Peers,anil the bowling of Watkins. Jeffer- soia,who was at one time a prominent member of the Warrington first, has not been favoured by fortune to a very great extent, but he "came off" on Saturday and batted extremely well for 26. The Wesleyans are going to see what kind of stuff the Welshmen are made of,and will be operating at Llandudno to-day, where they will meet a team representing that fashionable watering place. The second team of the Wesieyans were also victorious at St. Helens on Saturday, winning by 42 to 20.</p>
        <p>Peter iN alker and Sons first eleven, gained a victory over the Penketh Wesleyans by five wickets and one run although they were operating on" foreign sod." Extras were very plentiful in the match for although Penketh only scored 52, ten of those were extras,which certainly points to a slight laxity on the part of the brewery men. The second eleven of the Brewery men also enjoyed themselves at the expense of Weston second. They ran up a score of 83 of which Turner made 34, to which the Westonians could only reply with 26. The brewery have two excellent teams at present, and have given a good account of themselves thus far.</p>
        <p>Among other matches played in Warrington, the Cario St. Unity ran away with the St. Mary's team to the extent of 61 to 13. The Y.M.C. A. did ditto to Earles- town St. John's, Thompson taking six of the Earlestown wickets for three runs. Scarcely any of the victorious teams were content with a small win on Saturday for in addition to the above, Buckley-st., beat Whitecross by 55 to 20, Bank Quay beat Holy Trinity 2nd by 35 to 27, and Sankey Wire Mills 2tid did the same to Farnworth C.E.T.S, by 54 to 29, so that altogether, the winning teams had a glorious title of it. It is getting glorious weather new for cricket, and old Sol appears to be mak- ing up for lost time, and therefore it behoves cricketers to mak., the most of him while he is in the right humour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>BOWLS.</head>
        <p>BOWLS.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire bowling team beat Yorkshire at the Woodlands, Cheetham Hill, on Saturday by 512 to 407. There were 25 a side exclusive of the president and vice- President of each county.. In the Lancashire team, there were two players. Berry of Warrington S.; who scored 21, and Hewitt of Warrington N., who scored 14. The match was very keenly contested, and there were very numerous close games. The Yorkshire men are not however as good at bowling as they are at cricket at present, at all events.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the Warrington Liberal Club bowling team proceeded to Winnington, with the intention of in- flicting a severe defeat on the Winningtonians. Their intentions were, hosrever, not fulfilled, the game being won by the Winnington team by 351 points to 238. However, the Warrington men swallowed this defeat (also an excellent tea partaken of at the Stanley Arms, Winninton) with very good grace, and altogether en- joyed a pleasant outing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Fairfield Turner appears to have infringed the amateur rules recently at St. Helens where he took part in a professional wrestling match. A water earnival of the West Liverpool Swimming Club was held on Mon- day evening in the Lodge-lane baths and among the competitors in one of tho events was our local athlete. Mr. Barker, the judge, however announced during Vie evening that Fair Turner had not put in an appearance in consequence of taking part in the wrosiling contest above mentioned. Mr. Barker said that no doubt Mr. Turner bad infringed the amateur rule unintentionally, but on a proper explanation being given to tly-.• N.C.A.S.A. he was sure the matter would come all right.</p>
        <p>At the Tvldesley C.C. meeting on Saturday, .J. Fair, of Warrington captured the third prize in the 440 yards race. W. Kelsey, of Salford, came in first, and was followed by J. Massey, of Pendleton. Fair, who was in receipt of 33 yards and who :an well, is well known in Warrington athletic circles.</p>
        <p>THE SUB-CAPTAIN of the Warrington Cycling Club.—Mr. TED PICFAVANCE, writes May 2, 1894. Dear Sir,—l have given your DRINKSTONE EMBROCA- TION an exhaustive trial and can safely recomrAenci it as a good reliable artizle. A little of it rubs.,eci into the muscles after a long ride Lives a wonderfully soothing effect ; while for u. Sprain I know of nothing better. lam confident that when its merits are known amongst Cyclists, and Athletes generally it will command a great Sale. Your truly, E. PICKAVANC jun.</p>
        <p>To ba obtained only from the Proprietor, HENRY M. ASHTON, 63, Sankey-st., (opposite Legh-st.), Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>DREADFUL COLLIERY DISASTER IN • SOUTH WALES. DEATH OF 250 MEN.</head>
        <p>DREADFUL COLLIERY DISASTER IN • SOUTH WALES. DEATH OF 250 MEN.</p>
        <p>The inquest upr-in-slic;-victims of the Seuth Wales col- liery disaster was formally opened at Pontypridd, on Monday, before two coroners and two juries. sittine.as a joint board. Mr. Rhys, the Aberdare coroner. havine offered an expression of pubiic sympathy with the bereaved relatives, the bodies which had been brought up were viewed and the inquiry adjourned. The total number of bodies recovered up to Monday night was 193, of which 157 have been identified.. Six of the sixteen men who were brought out alive have slnce died, making the total estimated number of deaths 257. Mr. Alfred Thomas, M.P., on Monday opened a subscription list on behalf of the relatives with a donation of £lOO, and on Tuesday the Welsh members will meet to pass a vote of condolence and to take steps for enlarging the fund. Appeals for assistance have also been addressed to the Lord Mayor of London and the Mayor of Cardiff. The almost unan- imous opinion of the experts who have inspected the workings is that the explosion was caused by the ignition of a "blower" of as in the course of shot•firing. A Pontypridd correspondent telegraphed on Tuesday afternoon that 244 bodies bad been rescued from the Albion Colliery and 201 had been taken to their homes in coffins. Of the number remaining in the mortuary' most have been identified and .ticketed with labels con- taining their names arid addresses. Considerable delay has been occasioned by the scarcity of c flirts. The col- liery authorities regret to find that the estimated num- ber of killed is much too low, and it is feared that over 300 lives have been lost. 'acre is happily no truth in a rumour which was circulated that a party of 20 ex- plorers had been killed in the mine. An open-air prayer meeting was held on a mountain side in the vicinity of the pit on Tuesday afternoon. Thousands of people were present, and the proee6ings were of a most pathe- tic and touching character. Peculiarly pathetic inci- dents are hourly coming to light. In one house lie the bodies of a father, four sons, and six lodgers, and the penr wife and mother is prostrate with grief. There are some cases of extreme poverty among the surviving relatives. In one instance it is stated that when the body of the victim was taken into the house the widow and nine children had not a morsel of bread to eat. Kind neighbours relieved their distress •temporarily. Other cases of great privation and poverty are not infrequent. The work of expleration still continues at the Albion Celiery, in South Wales, the bodies recovered up to Wednesday night numrering 268. The mournful duty of burial was proceeded with, and the remains of 120 victims were during the day consigned to the grave. About 30,000 spectators assembled to witness and to participate in the ceremony, which is described ts hav- ing_been most impressive.</p>
        <p>YOUR ATTENTION PLEASE Sufferers from Gravel, Lumbago. Piles, Pains in the Back, Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaints. Diseases of Kidneys. Bladder, Urinary Organs, Stone, Meet, Strie ture, Sciatica. Rheumatism, and Gout, will find a positive cure in tioldroyd's Gravel Pitts. Try a small box, *no if not satisfied your money will be returned. Priae Is Of all chemists. or post tree for 12 stomps from oLn- R0Y1).14 Medical Hall, CleekheatAm. Yorks. Don't he put off. If you cannot get them write to the Proprietor, and • I ox will be tont a2xt post.—sidvt.</p>
        <p>DR. GO RNALL AND THE WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 30, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 30, 1894.</p>
        <p>Mr. RATCLIFFE seconded Mi. Davies' motion. He did n9t think that Dr. Gornall should receive his discharge after his long and faithful services to the Beard. Mr. MUIR also supported the motion. His hostility to Dr. Gornall would only arise when he saw him failing in Lis duty. Mr. Diorium rose to support the resolution. He must confess that he was very sorry for the spirit in which Mr. Davies had moved his resolution. He thought. a great number could not but think well of the very hand- some manner in which Mr. Shaw had met the resolution. Some of Mr. Davies' remarks had been most unneces- sary. He would, however, support his motion, and he ' was sure that the public of Warrington would agreed with it.</p>
        <p>Mr. HEATON said that he had not changed his opinion. He stlll thought that the poor people should not be com- pelled to go to Dr. Gorna:l to be vaccinated while he was the medical officer for the infectious diseases hospital. Mr. Heaton went on to quote figures to show the work Dr. Gornall did when Mr. Mum interrupting said that that had nothing to do with it. Mr. HEATON : . I don't wish to have any information from Mr. Muir because he knows nothing about it, and takes no interest in it. Referring to the memorial pre- sented before the discussion, he said that the medical men knew nothing about the question. How did they know what Dr. Gornall did ? They knew no mere about the hospital than they (the Board) did. (NoNsense.) No doubt pressure had been brought to bear upon some of the members of the Board but he had not altered his opinion. He was therefore determined to vote against Mr. Davies' motion.</p>
        <p>Mr. CLARK said that his objection to Mr. Shaw's motion was that it had not been given notice of, and therefore a number of the members had not had an opportunity to attend. He supported Mr. Davies' motion.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JoLLEy said that every medical man had a right to go into the hospital, and this was a sufficient answer to Mr. Heaton. He would also ask whether a case of infection had ever been proved to have been caused by Dr. Gornall's connection with the Infectious Diseases Hospital. There had been insinuations, but . let them have a single case proved. He considered that Mr. Heaton's arguments were completely worthless. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. H. OwErisaid that he was one of those who had supported Mr. Shaw's motion. He was, however deter- mined on the present occasion to take no part in the matter whatever, and he hoped Mr. Heaton would do the same.</p>
        <p>Mr. SHAW appealed to Mr. Heaton to withdraw his opposition in favour of the opinion of the medical faculty. Mr. Davies' motion was then put to the meeting and received 14 votes including Mr. Shaw. Two members, Messrs. Heaton and Hill voted against it, and four members, Messrs. Kilshaw, Owen, Gill and Pilling were neutral. The motion was therefore carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>R. SHAW'S MOTION RESCINDED.</head>
        <p>R. SHAW'S MOTION RESCINDED.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held in the Boardroom of the Workhouse on Wednesday, and there was a large attendance of the members, Mr. Wm. Houghton occupying the chair. After the transaction of the usual business of the meeting,Mr. S. Davies brought forward the motion of which he had given notice at the previous meeting, to rescind the motion passed on the motion of Mr. W. J. Shaw, to terminate Dr. Gor. nail's connection with the Board as vi ccination officer. Previous to the motion being proposed by Mr. Davies, the Clerk said he had received a memorial from the medical faculty in the town with reference to the vac- cination appointment. The CHAIRMAN : Have they any right to send it ? The CLERK : It is a memorial from ratepayer 2. Mr. RATCLIFFE : I propose that the memorial be re- ceived.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: lam not objecting to it. I only wanted to find out whether they had any right to send it.</p>
        <p>Mr. OWEN : I move that we do not listen to that me- morial. I don't think that we should be dictated to by the medical profession, any more than by anyone else. We "mow our own business best, and no one has any right to act as they have done. I certainly think that the memorial should be put on the table. It is a piece of impertinence. Mr. DIGNUM : I think that we have a perfect right to listen to the ratepayers or any question affecting them. (Hear, hear.) I therefore second Mr. Ratcliffe's 'no- tion. Mr. OWEN : Moved that it he not read.</p>
        <p>Mr. HEATON : I second the amendment, because I don't see anything that can possibly give any Instruction to this Board, especially after the remarks which have been passed in the local papers. The amendment was then put to the meeting and received eight votes. The motion that the memorial be read received twelve votes and was accordingly carried. The CLERK then read the memorial which was as fel- lows : " Warrington, 7th June, 1894. To the Board of Guardians, Gentlemen,—The undersigned members of the Medical Profession in Warringtn beg t 4 respectfully address you on the subject of your recent action, with , regard to the Vaccination appointment, in the proposed dismissal ef Mr. Gornall. Though without any " locus standi "for our action, we would ask you to reconsider your late decision. It is stated that the grounds for Mr. Gornall's dismissal are : that the Medical Officer to the Fever Hospital might possibly convey infection to the infants presented for vaccination. To this we should ask to be allowed to urge-1. That the same objection would always and with equal force be liable to be cited against any practitioner in that capacity. (public or private), since their work must con- tinually bring them in contact with infectious cases. 2. That from the precautions adopted by the Public Vac- cinator any such risk is reduced to a minimum. 3. That this is proved from the fact that Mr. Gornall has held the post for 22 years without any possible infection or suspicion of it having been traced to this cause. These ' are merely medical reasons to meet the objection of the Guardians. We further hope, as fellow-townsmen who have no less than yourselves the public good at heart (on a question which we humbly submit we may be allowed to have some special knowledge) to lay before you the following reasons why Mr. Gornall should be retained in his appointment :—(1). His tried services during the last quarter of a century. These cannot be gainsaid, as he has ten times received the Government Grant for efficiency. No stronger testimony than this could be adduced as to the value of his services. (2). That to retain a man who possesses this unimpeachable guaran- tee of efficiency for so long a period is a measure dis- tinctly for the public good, as it secures the services of an experienced and watchful officer. (3). That Mr. Gornall has abandoned all chance of private professional advancement by devoting himself to the public Medical Service, and that his dismissal after his distinguished services would prove a great obstacle to men of talent and experience devoting themselves to public work when an abrupt decision of the Board might for ever termin- ate the sphere of public usefulness and wreck their priv ate affairs. In conclusion, we would most respectfully urge that no one can be better judges of the value of Mr. Gornall's work that ourselves, or more ably to justly estimate the medical aspect of the question before the Guardians. Both from the view of public polity and from the consideration of tried services, Gentlemen, we would beg of you to re-consider your decision. We are, Gentlemen. Your very obedient servants, C. N. Spinks, Charles White, T. Starkey Smith, James S. Sheratt, Edward A. Fox, George W. Joseph, James Cannel!, Joseph Adams, E. Brooks, C. E. Richmond, Robert M. Jack, Michael McCafthy, E. J. Geary, J. B. Naden, Robert W. Ronald, Reginald Smith, James Henry Kisby, A. %V. Hall, Ernest E. Bowden.</p>
        <p>After the reading of the memorial, Mr. Shaw asked to be privileged to address the Board, before Mr. Davies brought forward his motion. A month ago, he had brought a motion before the Board which only "held a majority of one." He went away from the meeting rather reluctantly, as he had'expected his majority to have been greater, and he considered that the motion he had brought forward, well deserved a unanimous vote. Since that date, he had been "applied to" by the medi- cal faculty and by some of the " etninentest" men in Warrington, as well as by Mr: Robert Pierpoint, and be had been asked to reconsider the matter. He told them that he had only one objection, and that was since the time of the terrible and disastrous visitation of smallpox, he had been a traid that the disease might be conveyed owing to Dr. Gornali being at, the infections diseases hospital. He as however not going against 20 inembera—practically the whole—of the medical faculty of Warrington. fie had brought the motion forward in a public spirit, and new he found that his views did not coincide with the medical faculty, he withdrew his motion. He would like it to go forth in the press that there was nothing political connected with the affair,and to prove that statement, the member for the borough had allowed him (Mr. Shaw) to mention his name as atotious for the opposition to Dr. Gornall to cease. A great number of working people had visited him wto had received such treatment at the hands of Dr. Gornall at the hospital, as spoke volumes in his favour. He (Mr. Shaw) had been misled in the matter, and he had found that Dr. Gornall was not the man he had taken him to be at all. (Hear, hear.) As he had moved the resolu- tion, he asked the permission of the Board to be allowed respectfully to withdraw the resolution be had moved a month ago. The CHAIRMAN: You have no power to do that. You can vote for Mr. Davies' motion. Mr. HEATON as seconder of the resolution said that he must confess that he bad neither seen nor heard anything to alter his opinion regarding the vaccination question. Mr. DAVIES : I think this is hardly fair before I have moved my resolution. The CHAIRMAN ruled Mr. Heaton out of order and Mr. DAVIES then moved the following resolution :—" That the resolutions passed at the Board meeting osi May 30th last upon the subject of determining the appointment of Dr. J. H. Gornall as public vaccinator and of advertis- ing for candidates for the office of public vaccinator, be rescinded, arid that the contract with Dr. Gornall be con- tinued as hitherto." Proceeding he said that it was never too late to show the white feather as Mr. Shaw had done that morning. Mr. SHAW : I must ask Mr. Davies to withdraw that. Mr. DAVIEs : I don't see what I have said wrorla. Mr. SHAW : I object to the "white feather." (La ugheer.) Mr. DAVIES Said that if Mr. Shaw did not like that,he would withdraw the exprestion. Proceeding to speak sn the motion,he said that he had received a little abuse in connection with the matter, and he had also received support and encouragement from gentlemen holding high positions in the town. He had heard very strong condemnatory expressions with regard to the motion pass ial by the Board a month ago. He, however, ignored ail that. Therowere things said which he could not understand. He had been called a Radical. (Laugh- ter.) He did not know that it would make him any the worse if he was so. But he thought that he was as politics as anyone on the Board, as Mr. , ssohiamwd in h couldi s testify. (" Question," and laughter.) He brought forward his motion principally on the ground that sufficient time and opportunity had not been given to the members of the Board to seriously consider the motion which had been brought forward by Mr. Shaw a month ago. It was scarcely fair in his opinion, that any medical man should after serving the Beard for 22 veara,be dismissed by a majority of one. He was wish- ful that the Board should have an epportunity of recon- sidering its position, because he was sure that no one would like to do an injustice to anybody. They would remember when discussing the matter a month ago, the Clerk very wisely and very clearly gave them to under- stand that it would be better to give notice of tho mo- tion, so that everyone would have an opportunity to attend. He therefore, said that in his opinion a more ill-timed and unjust proposition had never been sub. mitted to the Board durinr, the time he had been a member of it. (Cries of "M!,00, and "You're spoi:jug your case.") When the motion was put forward, there were eleven members absent, owing to no notice having been given,anel therefore a great injustice had been done to Dr. Gornall. He did not for a moment defend Dr. t;orriall simply because he was Dr. Gornall. He was defending a principle. fie for one thought until a month ago that the question bad beer' dropped, and that they would hear no more of it. He was led to believe so from Mr. Shaw himself. He could not think why he had so suddenly changed his views. Mr. Snaw : I object to this. Mr. DAVIS (continuint!) said that suppose for a moment that his motion was not, ca rr'ed . The Board would then invite competition from the doctors of the town. Since reading the memorial he did not think that any medical man in ‘Varrington would be so mean as to take advantage of one of their brethren placed as Dr.Gornall would be. (Gi.eat disorder and cries of " Withdraw," and " No, no.") He would remind them that that would probably be the last public act of importance they would do before November. He hPe,,..:Pd of them Ow they would not insist upon th.- dismisii.ll of a inaat valu- able public officer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly meeting of this Board, which was held in the Board room of the Workhouse o Wednesday, there were present Mr. W. Houghto n (chairman) Rev. G. F. Roberts, Messrs. S. Davies, A. J. Jolley, J. Dobson, J. Muir, J. Hill, W. J. Shaw, R, Owen jun., Clarke, G. Ratcliffe, Dignum, Turton, R. Heaton, Warburton, Ilibbert, Mather, Pilling, A. Jolley, Fairclough, Gill, Kilshaw, J. Houghton, Dr. Patterson, 11. J. Longton, J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (deputy :clerk), C. H. Ward (workhouse master), if. M. Mines (superintendent at Padgate' schools).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>RECENT COUNTY COUNCIL INQUIRY.</head>
        <p>THE RECENT COUNTY COUNCIL INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>The CLERK announced that he had received the report which had been submitted to the Lancashire County Council for approval with respect to the severance of the Cheshire townships from the Warrington Union. He would suggest that at the next Board meeting, they go into committee to discuss the matter.—Mr. HEATON said that he thought the Board would hardly be justified in going against the wishes of the township by retaining them in the Warrington Union, if they wished to go elsewhere. He moved that they go into committee at the next Board meeting.—Mr. SHAW seconded.—Mr. A. J. Jolley thought that some protest should be made against the number of Guardians which it was proposed to assign to Warrington. He was prepared to move the following resolution : That the Clerk be instructed to prepare and submit to the Board at its next meeting an appeal to the Lancashire County Council praying them to object to the decision of the Joint Committee with re- gard to the number of Guardians to be selected for the urban districts of Warrington and Latchford on the ground that whilst the representation of the urban dis- tricts of Newton and Haydoek it had been increased from one guardian for 3232 people to one for 2424, the representations of the urban districts of Warrington and Latchford had been decreased from one guardian for 4612 to one for 5860 people, and that this increased dis- proportion is not justified by the respective rateaWe values, and is in contravention both of the spirit of the Local Government Act of 1894, and of recent legisla- tion.—After a short discussion, the motion was ?bled out of order, but the CLERK said that the Board could ap- proach the County Council at any time to alter the re- presentation.—The motion of Mr. Heaton was then passed unanimously. Mr. S. DAVIES' motion with reference to Dr. Gornall's appointment was the next business. A full report will be feuncl in another column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>INTERESTINU ANNIVERSARY AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>INTERESTINU ANNIVERSARY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>ENTRY OF THE STEAM TUGS INTO THE TOWN.</p>
        <p>Two events of extreme local interest and import- ance, more especially to the shipping community, were commemorated on Msnday evening at the Waterloo Hotel, Runcorn. There were firstly, the twentieth anniversary of the steam tugs coming on the Bridgewater Canal, at Runcorn ; and secondly the opening of the Manchester Ship Canal. The occasion was also one on which to do honour to "The noble master, the hon. Sir Leader Williams," as he was referred to the neighbourhood of the hotel mentioned was hung with a profusion of bunting and consequently bore a very gay and festive appearance. The proceeding took the form of a supper and enterainment. Previously, however, a number of those invieed wee() marshalled into processional order and headed by the Seamens' Mission Brass Band, under the conductorship of Mr. Wm. Shaw, missionary, paraded the principal thorough- fares of the town. Preceding the procession in a convey- ance were Mr. E. Wickenton, tug inspector, and Mr. Evans, Ship Canal and Bridgewater Undertaking agent at Warrington. On their return to the hotel thef adjourned to the large roo:n and partook of a capital supper, provided by Mr. Dyer. The company num- bered more than one hundred persons. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Subsequently, Mr. Joseph Speakman again took the chair and Mr. E. Wickenton the vice-chairs. The Chair- man, in his opening remarks, congratulated those pre- sent on the twentieth anniversary of the first steam tug comine to Runcorn on the Bridgewater Canal. Those who remembered the day upon which that tug made its appearance in theirmidst would also be cognizant of the many important changes which had since taken place— the regulation of steam and the censumption of smoke. Rapid strides had indeed been made in the engineering world and to-day Englishmen held the first place where engineering skill was concerned. In addressing the voting men of the company he wished to advise them to -follow in the footsteps of 'George Stephenson, who was the pioneer of steam locomotion ; and George Elliott, who was known as "The pit-lad of the north," a name which lie was always proud to bear. Mr. Speakman then alluded to the technical institute which is in pro- cess of being built specially for the youth of Runcorn whom lit' hoped, would recognise the usefulness of it and give it their patronage. He hoped the institution would thrive and be the means of conferring innumerable benefits upon the pegple of Runcorn. A lengthy toast list was gone through including "'The Town and Trade of Runcorn"; "Clergy of all denomi- nations"; "Success to the Manchester Ship Canal" ; and the various officials holding prominent positions under the Ship Canal Company, and the Bridgewater undertaking. —Mr. Shaw, missionary, whose name was coupled with the toast to the clergy, said that his work took him amongst numberless men engaged on tugs, steamers, sailing Yesselg, and other craft. Consequently. those present would credit him with a little knowledge con- cerning them, their ways, dispositions, &amp;c. This was : one of those oceasiens upon which they ceulcl all gather together—no matter wtat denomination they were—and enjoy themselves. He hoped their enjoyment would he of an innocent nature and that this meeting would be • the means of drawing them all closer together in the bonds of good fellowship.—Mr. Oxworth in responding to " The town and trade of Runcorn," said they were all too well aware that a state of depression existed in the trade of Runcorn as in many other towns. It was to be eorted, now that there were increased facilities for the carriage of goods to Manchester by water throueli the construction of the Ship Canal, that the trade would shortly find no diffirtie.e to obtain work. (Applause.) Other addresses were e;..en after which the following programme was sustailted in praiseworthy fashion by the various gentlemen mentioned, the piano accompanisc being Mr. l. Emit :—Senes "Sailor's Welcome Star," Mr. Penny ; "Every Bullet," Mr. _Moores ; "That's English," (humorous), Mr. Holt ; recitation, " Past Life of te Navvy," Mr. Wickington ; duet, " Larboard Watch," Messrs. Penny and Moores; character song, Mr. Feeg ; song and chorus, "Only to see her face again," Mr. Penny ; song, " That was me," (humorous), Mr. , Holt ; "Wait till his shit, comes home," Mr. Moores; comic duet. " Brothers McCarthy," Messrs. Woodward aud Aciterley.</p>
        <p>fITTRE OCHE') WITHOUT OPF.HATION.-13. J. SHERMAN. Hernia Specialist.. 64, Charcery-Lane, London ; 26, King. stre.t. 111.inebrsti..; Book on rupture and evil of trUENS pot :re. 7.1 —At4ll.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>NEW BOOKS, &amp;c.</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Last month Messrs. Macmillan and Co. published a new edition of •` Adventures In Mashonaland." The book is written by two hospital nurses (liot,e Blennerhassett and Lucy Sleeman), in a direct ani simple manner, without any attempt at showy lan- guage or literary finish, and is an exceedingly in- teresting account of their work as nurses, and experience for two years as travellers in the "Dark Continent." Just as they sacrificed their comfort and feelings to their work, have they studiously avoided any pretentious reference to themselves in their description of it, in fact the authors' are such as to make us feel proud of our fellow country- women.</p>
        <p>"The Jungle Book" is the latest of Rudyard Ki- pling's clever books. in it are seven tales, all well illustrated, suitable for youngsters of all ages, and displaying the author's genius in a new aspect. The fact that Mr. Kipling is the author tempts one to read it when perhaps "The Jungle Book' wouldn't, and before many pages are perused we yield to the charm of the fascinating way in which he describes imaginary life in the jungle. Perhaps of the seven stories "The White Seal" and "Toomai of the Elephants" are the favourites. _ _</p>
        <p>This month "The Pall Mall Budget "comes out in a new form as the first nuraber of a new series. For a quarter of a century it has been a favourite magazine, and so long as its pages reveal the origin- ality and smartness of everything interesting and pleasing, as the first June number assuredly does, it will continue to be so. In it we have a portrait of Mr. Kipling in his study, which illustrates an " interview," and the first chapters of another new Jungle story. An "At Home' section written by women for women is sure to please the fair sex, and stories by Bret Harte and Robert Louis Stevenson are promised for following numbers. Among the many "monthlies," "in Town" is sure to rank among the very best. As the editor re- marks "it begins with an already large army of supporters brought over from " Lazy Land," and is assuredly bright and beautiful, both literarily and literally, pictorially and typographically." This magazine has been conceived and produced with a first view to the pleasure of the reader, and is for entertainment pure and simple, a magazine for the moment. In it are ten cleverly Written complete stories, several full page drawings, some in colours, three of which illustrate the respective versions of three artists, of" Woman's Prerogative." As regularly as the holiday season comes round, so does the holiday number of "Cassell' Family Magazine." In addition to a capitally writtent and amusing complete story, the current number con- tains a special holiday supplement for ladies, with four holiday programmes, and the usual article on dress.</p>
        <p>We have received an advance copy of the Great Eastern Railway Company's "Tourist Guide to the Continent" for 1894, with illustrated chapters on a "New Dutch Tour," the Franco-German battle- fields, and cycling routes, edited by Percy Lindley in a manner_ that leaves nothing.to be desired.</p>
        <p>As usual "The Century Magazine" is full of high- class literature,suitable for all classes of intelligent readers. The July number contains the opening chapters of a new story (" Love in Idleness,") by F. Marion Crawford, the second part of "A Cumber- land Vendetta," by John Fox, junior, (illustrated), and so much "food for the mind," and all good, that it is difficult to tell which is the best.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>VOLUNTEER SHOOTING MATCH AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER SHOOTING MATCH AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. HARTFORD.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the return match between teams representing the volunteer detachments of Run- corn and Hartford took place at Runcorn Heath. Fine weather prevailed but it was necessary to allow from seven to eight feet in consequence of the strong wind which prevented the running up of tall figures. The scores were as follows : rEAA 200.</p>
