      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of this society was held on Sun- day afternoon, and was largely attended. Th.o chair was occupied by Mr. Pickayance who spoke the text "Son, go work in my vineyard. He said Igo Sir, but went not." In the course of his address, Mr. Pickayance gave some advice to young men, pointing out that there were always people who were going to do great things, but who never did them. Mr. Betts, of Liverpool, de- livered the address for the afternoon. He dealt with "Stages in a mis-spent life," in which he referred to the consequences of contracting such habits as smoking, drinking, and gambling. He contended that Lord Rose- bery by his patronage of horse racing was making more gamblers than converts to Home Rule. A letter was read from, the Rev. 391 in Yonge, who is at present cruis- ing about Scotland. Mr. Pendlebury admirably ren- dered a elarionet solo. Mr. W. H. Payton accompanied on the organ. Messrs. A. Birch, T. Wallington, and G. Clay also took part in the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>On Sunday owing to an interchange of pulpits the Rev. G. E. Cutting, formerly of the Warrington Circuit, preached both morning and evening at this church. He delivered two excellent discourses to large congregations, the one at the evening being exceptionally large.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ASSAESINATION OF THE FRENCH PRESIDENT.</head>
        <p>ASSAESINATION OF THE FRENCH PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>M. Carnot, President of the French Republic, was stabbed in the breast while driving through the streets of Lyons to the theatre on Sunday evening, and he died at a quarter to one o'clock on Monday morning. The assassin, who is described as a lunatic Anarchist, was seized by bystanders and was delivered over to the police. President Carnot had attended a ceremonial at the Patais du Commerce in the afternoon.and the people in the city were making holiday. The streets were filled with sightseers when the crime was committed. The tragedy has exctted painful emetions throughout the world. The assassin has been examined by a magis- trate, but he declined to answer any questions, and repeated his former declaration that he would explain his motives only before the jury at the assizes. The Italian Embassy in Paris has, it is said, received infor- mation that the miscreant is not an Italian but a Swiss. In Italy, however, no doubt seems to be felt that he is an Italian. His full name is Caseiro Santo Hieronymo, an it is stated that he was a member of a dangerous gang of Anarchists at Milan, and that he was tried only recently for inciting to outrage. The Italian Parliament and people are naturally dismayed and alarmed at the 4 crime, and in the Chamber of Deputies on Tuesday, Sig- nor Crispi gave fitting expression to the detestation in which all Italians of the better class regard the crime and the criminal. The populace of Lyons, however,has refused in the first outbreak of wrath to discriminate between Sa.ntoancl his countrymen,who abhor his crime. Italian houses in Lyons have been wrecked,and on Tues- day several incendiary fires broke out in the Italian quarter. To the entreaties of the police that they would abstain from outrage the crowd replied, " We wish to avenge Carnot."</p>
        <p>• It is a retnarkable coincidence that news of the three mort notable assassinations which have taken place during the last thirteen caars has disturbed the quiet of Sunday. It was on Sunday the 14th of March, 1881, that news was flashed to the capitals of Europe of tha assassination of the Czar. On Sunday morning the 7th of May, 1882, London awoke to hear of the murder of Lord Frederick Cavendish in Phoenix Park. The assas- sination of M. Carnot happening on Sunday evening, the news was not eenerally spread in London till Mon- day morning. But it was known on Sunday night over a wide area of club-land. THE NEW PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>The French National Assembly—consisting of the members of the two Chambers—met at Versailles on Virednesdav to elect a President of the Republic in suc- cession to the late M. Carnot. Mr. Challemel-Lacour, President of the Senate, presided. Whilethe voting was going on there were cries from the Socialist benches for the abolition of the Presidency, and the entire proceedings were carried on amid extreme excite- ment on the put of the Socialist deputies. M. Casimir-Perier received 451 votes out of '853, and was therefore declared elected President, The trial of Sant the assassin, wiil take rilscs6 t,tstA 23rd Ja:y.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>LATEST NE WS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NE WS.</p>
        <p>THE SCOTCH MINER'S STRIKE,</p>
        <p>It is intimated that there are 73,000 miners now on strike IL Scotland. Coal was firmer in price on Thurs- day in Glasgow, but inasmuch as heavy stocks had been purchased in anticipation of the labour trouble there was comparatively little demand. The attitude of both masters and men is firm, and the impression prevails that the struggle will be prolonged. The English miners will be asked to increase the amount of their levy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>AN IMPORTANT CONSTITUTIONAL QUv.STION.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday evening Mr. CHAMBERLAIN called attention to what he described ae"a grave constitutional question "—the granting of the Stewardship of the Chiltern Hundreds to the Mon. B. Coleridge at a time when he was in fact a peer of the realm. He contended that the House had by recording this fact in the resolution orderieg, a new writ for the Attercliffe division indirectly decided that the late mem- ber might be a member of the House of Commons, and not a peer, and thus it would be possible for any gentle- man in a similar position to refrain as long as he liked from applying for the writ of summons to the House of Lords. SIR W.HARCOURT agreed that the question raised ouget to be settled by • the House. But he differed from. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN as to the law Of Parliament. Mr. BALFOUR held that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN was correct and that the Government had " etarted a precedent that carried them far beyond the electioneering expediency of the Attercliffe division." Mr. VINCENT suggested that " in the interest of the Labour p irty in Sheffield" the writ should be withdrawn. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN said per- haps the most convenient course would be that he should withdraw his motion and that the Government should bring up an extended motion on a later day. It might be that the decision of the Committee would tend to show that the issue of the writ for Aetercliffe was irregular, and that might have an effect on the validity of the election. Sin WILLIAM HARCOURT could not entertain the suggestion. He offered, however, to confer with Mr. CHAMBERLAIN as to the form of the motion for a com- mittee. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN then withdrew his motion. Mr. BALFOUF questioned Slit Wet. HARCOURT about the circumstances of the divisions on the spirit duty on Wednesday. Sir WILLIAM denied that there had been any desire to stifle discussion. Mr. BALFOUR asked that the right hon. gentleman would at the proper time, in order to allay irritation move the recommittal of the spirit duty clause, so that "the opportunity of which the Opposition were robbed on Wednesday might be granted them. Sir Wu—HARCOURT declared his readi- ness to give the opportunity asked for. The right hon. gentleman then moved an address of congratulation to the Queen on the birth of an heir to the Duke of York. Mr. BALFOUR seconded the motion. Mr.J.K.FIARDIE protested against the motion in a speech which elicited loud cries of "Order '2' especially by its references to the Prince of Wales. At one point the SPEAKER interposed and told Mr.Hetrate that he was not addressing himself to the resolution before the House. Mr. FIARDIE reproached the Government for not finding; an opportenity for a vote of condolence with the fami- lies of the colliers killed in Wales, and said that if pos- sible he would carry to a division his protest against the mummery of this occasion. When the question was put Mr. HARDIE cried "No," and the House was cleared for a division. The hen. member could not, however, get a teller,and after being put a second time the motion was carried with cheers.</p>
        <p>The House again went into Committee on the Budget Bill, and clause 2,, relating to the income ray. came under discussion. Mr. DA¢Lrxa moved an amendment that a married woman's income should be assessed separately from her husband's. Sir W. HaßcotraT said the Inland Revenue authorities told him this amendment would mean a loss of half or three-quarters of a million, but he would consider whether some concession could be made. The amendment was withdrawn, and the clause was added to the bill. Just before the House rose Mr. ACLAND• introduced a bill to amend the Elementary Education Acts.</p>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE AND PARLIAMENT. Mr. Gladstone has finally determined'to retire from Parliament, and after the dissolution there will, says the "Daily Chronicle," be another member for Midlothian. We could scarcely have expected anything else, and yet the news of the departure of this incomparable figure from his accustomed place at the very cent re of our national life is sore reading. What will the House of Commons be without the man who for more than three- score years has worked for his country there? The grandeur of his passing is even greater than the pathos of it. Mr. Gladstone has not clung to office. With the high standard of pubiic duty from which he never swerved, he left it the moment he suspected his own powers, although the failing was but temporary. He did not choose to remain at the helm when his eye was dim, though his natural force was not abated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-30_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
