        <p>FURNISH at WORSLEY'S, GAYTHORN. HIGH-CLASS SECOND-HAND FURNITURE, THE PANTHEON, GAYTHORN, MANCHESTER. VVTEEKLY CHAT.—The great advantage of Fecond-hand Furniture over new lies in the fact that it has borne the test proving its stability. The world's desire io have everything at the lowest pos--ible price, together with the enormous competition, the introduction of steam power, has all tended to the production of cheap new jetry furniture. ut erly regardless of the one essential in furniture—stabilty. Worsley's warn the public against purchasing new London Furniture, when furniture by Lamb. Giliows, Ogden, Starkey, and Neild, Gai nett, Re., may be had for half the price of the New London Rubbish sold so largely by drapers. As an example, we have a Magnificent Sideboard, 7 feet long, by Henry Ogden and Sons, cost originally £BO, is in splendid condition ; our price £l5. %%That a farce to purchase new when the second-hand is so much superior in make and finish, Worsley's have n,arly 100 Sei'ond-hancl Sideboards in stock by all the best known makers of the day, from 355. to £5O. To prove the disadvantages of new Lo don fu niture. we were asked to buy a set of Drawing-room Furniture which had cost £4B this had only been in wear a little over twelve months, yet we found it impossible to give £lO for it, the fact being it was worthless, we advised it being sold by auction. Worsley's cuarantee every article sold for at least five years, all the heavy articles for a lifetime, and we claim that by combining the advantages of second-hand with the requisite and necessary portion of our new stock, which is all our own make, that we can completely put all competition in the shade. All we ask is the favour of a visit, and ak e will quickly demOnstrate the truth of these assertions. Far away the largest stock in the North. Insp ction freely invited. You are not expected to buy. The same courtesy shown to those looking rouni as to those purchasing. Established 1841.</p>
