        <p>A marriage which is of much interest to War- ringtonians, was celebrated at St,. Helens on, Tuns—. day. The coniracting parties were Miss MarY, ,Louisa. (Minnie) Glover, eldest daughter of. Mr.. f:-• 'Benjamin B. Glover, JP.. colliery proprietor and rope manufacturer, Lyme-grove, St. Helens, an4'i;,'. Mr.. Edward Aubrey White, -solicitor, Warringto four Ii son of Mr. H. B. White, solicitor and clerk to the'''. borough magistrates, Warrington. The marriage took .; place Ili the presence of a' large congregation in the In" , dependent Chapel, St. Helens. The bride was attired in white ondine silk with pearl trimming and embroidered lisse, veil and orange blossom. She also wore a sarP" phire and diamond ring, aid carried a shower bouquet of white flowers, the gift of the bridegroom. Her maids. .were Mils Fanny Glover and Miss Blanche Glover. ststers. They wore dresses of fawn habit cloth trimmed with white moil and ecru guipure. They also wore gold signet rings and carried shower bouquets of sweet peas.. ' the gift ot the bridegroom. Mr. G. F. F. White, brother acted as best man. As the wed ling party, numb44ring about seventy, assembled, Mr. H. R. Lacy, jim., organiet, played a short programme of music, including "T Song of Steep,”,by Lord Henry Somerset. 'As-the bride raYsed up the aisle leaning on the arm of her father.* who afterwards gave her away, the "Bridal March', (" L4ihengrin ") was played. The officiating ministerS were the Rev. James Allatt. of Newton-le-Willows, and the R6v. J. A. Mitchell, 8.A., of St. Helens. After Sir' ing the register the happy pair left the chapel to tbe I,eautitul strains of Mendelssohn's "Wedding March', and the best wishes of their many friends. Mr. and Mrs. B. B. Glover afterwards held a reception. at Lyra: grove. The presents numbered about 130, .and includea OLE; from emplo3 es at the Phcenix Colliery arid , Helens Ropery, and from the iitiowsley-road. Mission* - 'with which the bride had been connected. After a to" in the Lake ditrict Mr. and Mrs. White will take op their residence at Golborne Dale Cottage, 'N'ewton"le. Willows. 7 THE PLAGUE OF LIFY, A "Little Cough." THE RUIN OF , HEALTH : A "'Little Cough. THE FORERUNNER OF DEATH ' A "Little Cough." Which may be CURED now by the IMMEDIATIS HALL'S LUNG ESTORER FOR INFLUENZA lb Registered AND ALL CH EST er UNG AND T gCtA I-I RESTORER COMPLAINTS. Never Fails.. It POSSESS &amp;STEM POVV6II , • LUNG to. Soolna, RELLEVE HEAL, which exists in RESTORER other preparation. Fort COUGHS LUNG on COLDS* 'RESTORER Never Fails. It NLIVH it FAIL-3 to Glir° INSTANT RELIEF. Lu" _ RESTORER THE FIRST DOSS. IS A SOOT HIN,G, L"G LUDE TO THE CUBS. • , . • RESTORER INDUCIii SID;11 Cairn and Refreshing. - • LUNG RESTORER LAUDXNUM, ONUS' Never Fails mo upti /A.. T UN G DOES NOT CONTAIag. • . R Fort WHOOPING -f./ ESTORER • gg • LUNG IT ACTS LIKE A. 01./A -- RESTORER Children like it. . Never Fails. ONE J.l 01 BOTT-O HALL'S r' _.• t)2D. LUNU 1? ESTORER XII) Registered E NEVIO GIVES INSTANT NEVER PsTaitozsar: 47.1,111.10.11;s. , RELIEF. FAILS.</p>
